var e={"pediatrics-newborn-care":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`The **neonatal period** is the first 28 days; the IMNCI **young infant** is 0-59 days.`,`**Essential newborn care** is the basic package every baby gets at birth.`,`It includes drying, warmth, breastfeeding within 1 hour, dry cord care, vitamin K and birth-dose vaccines.`,`LBW is under 2500 g, very low under 1500 g, extremely low under 1000 g.`,`India's neonatal mortality is about **19 per 1000** live births (SRS 2021).`,`The three big killers are **prematurity, birth asphyxia and sepsis**.`,`The national target under INAP is a **single-digit NMR by 2030**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Critical illness**: convulsions, unable to feed at all, or no movement at all. Always refer.`,`**Clinical severe infection**: not feeding well, severe chest indrawing, movement only on stimulation, abnormal temperature.`,`**Fast breathing alone** (60/min or more) at 7-59 days is treated at home with amoxicillin.`,`Fast breathing alone at **0-6 days is referred** (WHO PSBI 2024).`,`Hypothermia: cold stress 36.0-36.4, moderate 32.0-35.9, **severe under 32 degrees C**.`,`Sepsis is **early-onset within 72 hours** or late-onset after 72 hours.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`Early-onset sepsis risks: rupture of membranes **over 18 hours** and maternal fever over 38 C.`,`Other risks: foul liquor, over three unclean vaginal examinations, preterm labour, untreated maternal UTI.`,`In Indian units **Klebsiella, E. coli and Acinetobacter** are the commonest sepsis organisms.`,`Preterm and LBW babies cool fastest because they have little brown fat and glycogen.`,`Late VKDB happens in exclusively breastfed babies who **missed vitamin K** at birth.`,`Cow dung, ash or oil on the cord brings **tetanus spores** and staphylococci.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. A newborn loses heat fast through a large surface area, thin skin and a big head.`,`2. Cold releases noradrenaline, which burns **brown fat** through uncoupling protein-1.`,`3. This uses oxygen and glucose, so cold causes **hypoglycaemia, acidosis and apnoea**.`,`4. Immature immunity means the baby **cannot localise infection**.`,`5. So sepsis, pneumonia and meningitis all present as poor feeding, lethargy or temperature change.`,`6. In asphyxia the heart slows from hypoxia, so **ventilation, not drugs**, restores heart rate.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask the **age in days and hours**, birth weight and gestation.`,`Ask how many feeds in 24 hours (expect **8-12**) and about prelacteal feeds.`,`**Stopped feeding well** is the commonest sign of sepsis.`,`Ask about urine within 24 hours and **meconium within 48 hours**.`,`Ask whether vitamin K and birth-dose vaccines were given.`,`Jaundice in the **first 24 hours** is always pathological.`,`**Bilious vomiting** means malrotation with volvulus until proved otherwise.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Count respiratory rate for a **full 60 seconds** in a calm baby; repeat if 60 or more.`,`Look for **severe chest indrawing**; mild indrawing is normal in a newborn.`,`Take axillary temperature for 3 minutes; a cold abdomen means hypothermia.`,`Observe a breastfeed for the **four signs of good attachment**.`,`Check umbilicus: redness spreading to abdominal skin is serious omphalitis.`,`Feel **femoral pulses** for coarctation; check red reflex, hips, spine and anus.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Check **glucose by glucometer** in every sick, cold or LBW baby; treat under 45 mg/dL.`,`Take a **blood culture before the first antibiotic**, but never delay the dose for it.`,`Sepsis screen: TLC under 5000, I:T ratio 0.2 or more, CRP over 1 mg/dL.`,`A negative sepsis screen does not exclude sepsis in a sick baby.`,`Pulse oximetry for critical CHD after 24 hours: pass is **95% or more**, difference 3% or less.`,`TSH heel prick for hypothyroidism after **48-72 hours**, never earlier.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Dry within 30 seconds and put the baby **skin-to-skin** with a cap.`,`Clamp the cord after **1-3 minutes** if no resuscitation is needed.`,`Start breastfeeding **within 1 hour**; exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months.`,`Give **vitamin K1 1 mg IM** at birth (0.5 mg if under 1000 g).`,`Give **vitamin D3 400 IU** daily from birth to 1 year.`,`Delay the first bath for at least 24 hours; apply nothing to the cord.`,`**Kangaroo mother care** for every baby under 2000 g, started as soon as possible.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Hospital PSBI: **ampicillin plus gentamicin** for at least 10 days (WHO 2024).`,`Referral refused: **oral amoxicillin plus IM gentamicin for 7 days**; 2 days only if 7 is not feasible.`,`Meningitis: switch to **cefotaxime** and treat for 21 days.`,`**Avoid ceftriaxone** in neonates; it displaces bilirubin and precipitates with calcium.`,`Before referral give the first antibiotic dose, prevent hypoglycaemia and keep the baby warm.`,`Resuscitation: start bag-and-mask ventilation **within 60 seconds** if apnoeic or heart rate under 100.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Refer the same hour for **any danger sign** or critical illness.`,`Also refer jaundice in 24 hours or on palms and soles, bilious vomiting, cyanosis or bleeding.`,`HBNC visits after home birth: **days 1, 3, 7, 14, 21, 28 and 42**.`,`After institutional birth, HBNC visits start on day 3 (six visits).`,`HBYC visits at **3, 6, 9, 12 and 15 months**.`,`Discharge at least **48 hours** after vaginal and 72-96 hours after caesarean birth.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Key newborn drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Vitamin K1`,`1 mg IM thigh (0.5 mg if under 1000 g)`,`Once at birth`,`IM is standard; oral fails in cholestasis`],[`Vitamin D3`,`400 IU oral daily`,`Birth to 1 year`,`Hypercalcaemia only with overdose`],[`Ampicillin`,`50 mg/kg/dose IV/IM 12-hourly week 1, then 8-hourly`,`At least 10 days in hospital`,`Rash, diarrhoea`],[`Gentamicin`,`5 mg/kg once daily (4 mg/kg if under 2000 g)`,`Usually 7 days`,`Nephrotoxic, ototoxic; watch urine output`],[`Gentamicin (referral refused)`,`IM 5 mg/kg under 2 kg, 7.5 mg/kg 2 kg or more`,`7 days (2 if 7 not feasible)`,`Daily review; deterioration means refer`],[`Amoxicillin`,`50 mg/kg/dose oral twice daily`,`7 days`,`Dissolve in expressed breast milk`],[`Cefotaxime`,`50 mg/kg/dose IV 12-hourly week 1, then 8-hourly`,`21 days for meningitis`,`Use instead of ceftriaxone`],[`10% dextrose`,`2 mL/kg IV, then 6-8 mg/kg/min`,`Until glucose stable`,`Recheck glucose after 30 minutes`],[`Adrenaline (0.1 mg/mL)`,`0.02 mg/kg IV or IO; ET 0.1 mg/kg`,`Every 3-5 minutes`,`Only after ventilation and compressions fail`]]},{heading:`Sick young infant (0-59 days): classify and act`,columns:[`Class`,`Signs`,`Action`],rows:[[`Critical illness`,`Convulsions, cannot feed, no movement`,`Pre-referral antibiotics; always refer`],[`Clinical severe infection`,`Poor feeding, severe indrawing, abnormal temperature`,`Refer; if refused, amoxicillin plus gentamicin 7 days`],[`Fast breathing only, 7-59 days`,`RR 60/min or more, no other sign`,`Oral amoxicillin 7 days at home; review day 4`],[`Fast breathing only, 0-6 days`,`RR 60/min or more`,`Refer; amoxicillin 7 days if referral impossible`],[`Local bacterial infection`,`Red umbilicus or under 10 pustules`,`Gentian violet or mupirocin; review in 2 days`]]},{heading:`Neonatal resuscitation numbers (NRP 2025)`,columns:[`Step`,`Trigger`,`What to do`],rows:[[`Ventilation`,`Apnoea, gasping or HR under 100`,`Bag and mask, 40-60 breaths/min within 60 s`],[`Starting oxygen`,`By gestation`,`21% at 35+ wk; 21-30% at 32-34; 30%+ under 32`],[`Compressions`,`HR under 60 after 30 s good ventilation`,`Two-thumb, 3:1 ratio, 100% oxygen`],[`Adrenaline`,`HR under 60 after 60 s compressions`,`0.02 mg/kg IV via umbilical vein`],[`Volume`,`Blood loss or shock`,`Normal saline 10 mL/kg over 5-10 min`]]}],remember:[`Seven danger signs: any one means PSBI, first dose and refer.`,`A newborn does not localise infection; poor feeding is sepsis until proved otherwise.`,`Ventilation, not adrenaline, treats neonatal bradycardia.`,`APGAR never decides resuscitation.`,`Avoid ceftriaxone in neonates; use cefotaxime.`,`No cooling for HIE outside a tertiary NICU (HELIX trial).`,`Late VKDB: intracranial bleed at 2-12 weeks in a breastfed baby who missed vitamin K.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`AHA-AAP NRP 2025: initial oxygen 21% at 35 weeks or more, 21-30% at 32-34 weeks and 30% or more below 32 weeks (was 21-30% for all under 35 weeks)`,`WHO PSBI 2024: clinical severe infection with referral refused - oral amoxicillin 7 days plus IM gentamicin 7 days preferred, 2 days (India 2019 regimen) only if 7 is not feasible`,`WHO PSBI 2024: fast breathing alone at 0-6 days - refer; oral amoxicillin for at least 7 days only if referral is impossible (no longer automatically clinical severe infection)`,`WHO PSBI 2024: hospitalised suspected sepsis - ampicillin (or penicillin) plus gentamicin for at least 10 days first choice; third-generation cephalosporins no clear benefit`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Newborn care and the sick young infant`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Neonate = **first 28 days**; IMNCI young infant = **0-59 days**. **Essential newborn care**: drying, warmth, **delayed cord clamping 1-3 min**, **breastfeeding within 1 hour**, dry cord care, **vitamin K**, birth-dose vaccines, danger-sign counselling.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**PSBI (WHO 2024 / India 2019)**: **critical illness** (convulsions, unable to feed, no movement) - refer always; **clinical severe infection** - refer; **fast breathing only (60/min or more)** - 7-59 days home amoxicillin, 0-6 days refer. Hypothermia: **cold stress 36.0-36.4, moderate 32-35.9, severe under 32 C**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Deaths: **prematurity, birth asphyxia, sepsis**. EOS risk: **PROM over 18 h**, maternal fever, foul liquor, over 3 unclean PVs, preterm labour, maternal UTI. Indian sepsis organisms: **Klebsiella, E. coli, Acinetobacter**. NMR about **19/1000** (SRS 2021).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Cold -> noradrenaline -> **brown fat UCP-1** -> O2 and glucose burn -> **hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, acidosis, apnoea**; immature immunity -> **no localisation** -> sepsis presents as poor feeding, lethargy, abnormal temperature.`],[`Key history`,`Age in **days and hours**, birth weight, gestation; feeds (**8-12/24 h**, prelacteals); urine within 24 h, **meconium within 48 h**; maternal risk factors; vitamin K and birth doses given; each **danger sign**; jaundice onset; **bilious vomiting**.`],[`Key examination`,`Calm baby: **RR for full 60 s** (repeat if 60 or more), **severe chest indrawing**, axillary temp 3 min, weight, movement, observe a breastfeed (4 attachment signs), skin, umbilicus, fontanelle, **femoral pulses**, red reflex, hips, anus.`],[`Investigations`,`**Glucometer - treat under 45 mg/dL**; TcB or TSB on AAP 2022 chart; **blood culture before antibiotic** (never delay dose); sepsis screen (TLC under 5000, I:T 0.2 or more, CRP over 1 mg/dL); **pulse-oximetry CCHD screen after 24 h** (pass 95% or more, difference 3% or less).`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**Seven danger signs**: not feeding well, convulsions, **RR 60 or more**, severe chest indrawing, movement only on stimulation, **temp 37.5 or more or under 35.5 C**, yellow palms and soles - **any one = PSBI**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Vitamin K1 1 mg IM** (0.5 mg if under 1000 g); **vitamin D3 400 IU/day to 1 year**; PSBI in hospital: **ampicillin 50 mg/kg/dose 12-hourly (8-hourly after week 1) + gentamicin 5 mg/kg once daily (4 mg/kg under 2000 g)**; WHO 2024 duration at least 10 days.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Referral refused: **amoxicillin 50 mg/kg/dose BD 7 days + IM gentamicin 7 days** (5 mg/kg under 2 kg, 7.5 mg/kg 2 kg or more; **2 days if 7 not feasible**). Meningitis: **cefotaxime**, 21 days; **avoid ceftriaxone** in neonates.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**NRP 2025**: term, tone, breathing? -> warm, dry, stimulate -> **ventilation within 60 s** if apnoeic or HR under 100 at **40-60/min**; **O2 21% (35 weeks or more), 21-30% (32-34), 30% or more (under 32)**; compressions **3:1** if HR under 60; adrenaline **0.02 mg/kg IV**.`],[`Special groups`,`**LBW under 2000 g - KMC immediately**; feeding by maturity (**34 wk breast, 32-33 paladai, 28-31 orogastric**); **HIV-exposed - exclusive breastfeeding with maternal ART and infant nevirapine**; HBsAg mother - vaccine + HBIG within 12 h; maternal TB - breastfeed, infant IPT.`],[`Complications`,`Hypothermia, **hypoglycaemia**, sepsis-meningitis, **VKDB (late 2-12 weeks, intracranial)**, HIE, omphalitis, neonatal tetanus, kernicterus, ophthalmia neonatorum.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Any danger sign (same hour); **jaundice in 24 h or palms-soles**; bilious vomiting or no meconium by 48 h; **SpO2 under 90% or failed oximetry**; bleeding; spreading umbilical redness; weight loss over 10%.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`**HBNC**: home birth days **1, 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, 42**; institutional 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, 42; extra for LBW; **HBYC 3, 6, 9, 12, 15 months**; discharge after **48 h (vaginal), 72-96 h (caesarean)**; ROP screen under 34 weeks or 2000 g.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**INAP** (single-digit NMR by 2030), Newborn Care Corner, NBSU, **SNCU**, HBNC, RBSK newborn screening (TSH after 48 h, OAE, pulse oximetry, red reflex), **chlorhexidine only for home births** with unhygienic practice.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**NRP 2025** gestation-banded starting oxygen; **WHO PSBI 2024** - 7 days gentamicin preferred in simplified regimen, 0-6 day fast breathing referred, hospital ampicillin-gentamicin at least 10 days.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Ventilation, not drugs, treats neonatal bradycardia; **APGAR never decides resuscitation**; no routine tracheal suction for meconium; **no cooling outside tertiary NICU (HELIX)**; never delay first antibiotic for culture.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Young infant 0-59 days - classify and act`,caption:`WHO PSBI 2024 with the India 2019 programme fallback.`,steps:[{label:`Check the seven danger signs`,detail:`Not feeding, convulsions, RR 60+, chest indrawing, movement only on stimulation, temp 37.5+ or under 35.5 C, yellow palms-soles`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Critical illness?`,detail:`Convulsions, unable to feed at all, no movement - first dose ampicillin + gentamicin, glucose, warmth, refer; never home`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Clinical severe infection`,detail:`Refer; if refused amoxicillin 50 mg/kg BD 7 days + gentamicin IM 7 days (2 if 7 not feasible), daily review`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Fast breathing only`,detail:`7-59 days: amoxicillin 50 mg/kg/dose BD 7 days, review day 4; 0-6 days: refer, amoxicillin only if referral impossible`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Local infection or none`,detail:`Under 10 pustules or stump redness - gentian violet/mupirocin, review 2 days; none - home care, EBF, warmth`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`NRP 2025 - escalation at birth`,steps:[{label:`Initial steps in 30 s`,detail:`Warm, position, clear airway only if obstructed, dry and stimulate; delay cord clamping 60 s or more if vigorous`},{label:`Ventilate within 60 s`,detail:`Apnoea, gasping or HR under 100: bag-mask 40-60/min; O2 21% at 35 wk+, 21-30% at 32-34 wk, 30%+ under 32 wk`},{label:`Corrective steps MR SOPA`,detail:`If HR not rising in 15 s: mask, reposition, suction, open mouth, pressure up, alternative airway`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Chest compressions`,detail:`HR under 60 after 30 s effective ventilation: two-thumb, lower third sternum, 3:1 (90:30/min), 100% O2`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Adrenaline and volume`,detail:`Adrenaline 0.02 mg/kg IV/IO (0.01-0.03) every 3-5 min; ET 0.1 mg/kg; saline 10 mL/kg for blood loss`,tone:`good`}]}]},"pediatrics-immunisation":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and classification`,points:[`The **Universal Immunisation Programme** gives free vaccines against 12 diseases, plus vitamin A; **UIP is the floor, not the ceiling**.`,`A **fully immunised child** (12-23 months) has BCG, three pentavalent, three OPV and MR-1.`,`Full immunisation is about **87% in NFHS-6**; measles elimination needs 95% with two doses.`,`**Live vaccines**: BCG, OPV, MR or MMR, rotavirus, varicella, live hepatitis A, live JE.`,`**Inactivated vaccines**: pertussis, toxoids, hepatitis B, Hib, IPV, PCV, typhoid conjugate, HPV.`,`Conjugate vaccines (Hib, PCV, TCV) are **T-cell dependent** and work in infancy; plain polysaccharides do not under 2.`,`Two live injectable vaccines go on the **same day or 4 weeks apart**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Dendritic cells carry vaccine antigen to the lymph node and show it to T-helper cells.`,`2. B cells mature in germinal centres into plasma cells and **memory B cells**.`,`3. Infant responses are weak, so primary series need **three doses 4 weeks apart**.`,`4. A booster after 6 months or more recalls memory to a higher, longer peak.`,`5. **Maternal measles antibody** neutralises the vaccine, so MR waits until 9 months.`,`6. Memory persists, so a long gap does no harm; **never restart** the schedule.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask for the **MCP card or U-WIN certificate** at every child contact.`,`Record the date of birth and the date of every dose given.`,`Ask about reactions: fever, persistent crying, febrile seizure are **not contraindications**.`,`Ask about immune status, steroid dose, chemotherapy and recent immunoglobulin or blood.`,`Ask about previous **intussusception** or SCID before rotavirus vaccine.`,`Ask why doses were missed, because each reason has a different fix.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Vaccinate with mild illness or low fever; defer only for **moderate or severe illness**.`,`**Malnutrition is a reason to immunise**, not to defer.`,`Site: **anterolateral thigh under 1 year**, deltoid after 1 year, never the buttock.`,`BCG in the left upper arm; fIPV and MR in the right upper arm.`,`No BCG scar is **not a reason to revaccinate**.`,`Observe every child for **30 minutes** after vaccination for anaphylaxis.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**No routine test is needed** before vaccination.`,`Check anti-HBs (target 10 mIU/mL or more) only in high-risk groups.`,`Infant of HBsAg-positive mother: test **HBsAg and anti-HBs at 9-12 months**.`,`HIV-infected child: give MR and varicella if **CD4 is 15% or more**.`,`For a serious AEFI, keep the vial, syringe and diluent for investigation.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Birth: **BCG 0.05 mL intradermal**, OPV-0 and hepatitis B within 24 hours.`,`6, 10, 14 weeks: pentavalent, OPV and rotavirus; fIPV and PCV at 6 and 14 weeks.`,`9-12 months: **MR-1**, JE-1, PCV booster, fIPV-3 and vitamin A 1 lakh IU.`,`16-24 months: MR-2, JE-2, DPT booster-1, OPV booster, vitamin A 2 lakh IU.`,`DPT booster-2 at 5-6 years; **Td at 10 and 16 years**.`,`HPV: free single dose for **girls aged 14** under the 2026 national campaign.`,`Give **all due vaccines at one visit** at separate sites.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Catch-up rule 1: **never restart**; continue from the last documented dose.`,`Rule 2: no pentavalent, OPV, rotavirus or PCV before 6 weeks; no MR before 9 months.`,`Keep **4 weeks** between primary doses and 6 months before the first booster.`,`Rotavirus first dose must be **before 1 year**, otherwise omit it.`,`After 1 year give DPT instead of pentavalent; after **7 years give Td** only.`,`Do not give **prophylactic paracetamol**; it blunts the antibody response.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Preterm babies are vaccinated by **chronological age**, not corrected age.`,`Under 2000 g the hepatitis B birth dose is not counted; give 4 doses.`,`Withhold live vaccines on prednisolone **2 mg/kg/day or 20 mg/day for 14 days** or more.`,`Wait 1 month after stopping steroids and 3 months after chemotherapy.`,`HIV-exposed infants get BCG at birth if asymptomatic and all inactivated vaccines.`,`Pregnant women get **Td-1 and Td-2 four weeks apart**, or one booster.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`AEFI is any untoward event after a vaccine, **not necessarily caused by it**.`,`True contraindications: **anaphylaxis** to a dose or component, and encephalopathy within 7 days of pertussis vaccine.`,`Anaphylaxis: **adrenaline 1 mg/mL, 0.01 mL/kg IM** in the thigh, repeat every 5-15 minutes.`,`A faint has a **slow pulse** and recovers lying flat; it needs no adrenaline.`,`BCG lymphadenitis: observe; aspirate if fluctuant; **no incision and no anti-TB drugs**.`,`Serious AEFI is notified **within 24 hours** and investigated within 48 hours.`,`Red-currant-jelly stool after rotavirus vaccine means intussusception; refer.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`Cold chain: store vaccines at **+2 to +8 degrees C**; OPV is the most heat-sensitive.`,`Never freeze alum vaccines; use the **shake test** if freezing is suspected.`,`VVM stages 1 and 2: use; **stages 3 and 4: discard**.`,`Open multi-dose liquid vials last 4 weeks; reconstituted BCG, MR and JE go after 4 hours.`,`**Mission Indradhanush** runs catch-up rounds in low-coverage and migrant areas.`,`Dropout rate (Penta-1 to Penta-3) **under 10%** is acceptable.`]}],tables:[{heading:`UIP vaccines`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`BCG`,`0.05 mL intradermal, left deltoid (0.1 mL after 1 month)`,`Single dose up to 1 year`,`Ulcer then scar; lymphadenitis; avoid in SCID`],[`OPV (bivalent)`,`2 drops oral`,`Birth, 6, 10, 14 wk, 16-24 mo`,`VAPP and cVDPV (rare)`],[`Pentavalent`,`0.5 mL IM thigh`,`6, 10, 14 weeks`,`Fever, local pain; never freeze`],[`Rotavirus`,`Rotavac 5 drops or Rotasiil 2.5 mL oral`,`6, 10, 14 weeks`,`Intussusception 1-2 per 100,000`],[`fIPV`,`0.1 mL intradermal, right arm`,`6 weeks, 14 weeks, 9 months`,`Local nodule`],[`PCV`,`0.5 mL IM thigh`,`6, 14 weeks; booster 9 months`,`Fever, irritability`],[`MR`,`0.5 mL subcutaneous, right arm`,`9-12 and 16-24 months`,`Fever and rash day 5-12; not in pregnancy`],[`DPT / Td`,`0.5 mL IM`,`DPT 16-24 mo, 5-6 y; Td 10, 16 y`,`Local reaction; Td only after 7 years`],[`Hepatitis B birth dose`,`0.5 mL IM thigh, plus HBIG if mother HBsAg+`,`Within 24 hours`,`HBIG at a different site within 12 hours`],[`Vitamin A`,`1 lakh IU at 9 mo, then 2 lakh IU oral`,`6-monthly to 5 years (9 doses)`,`Bulging fontanelle, vomiting if overdosed`]]},{heading:`Catch-up limits (UIP)`,columns:[`Vaccine`,`Minimum age`,`Minimum interval`,`Upper age`],rows:[[`BCG`,`Birth`,`Single dose`,`1 year`],[`Pentavalent`,`6 weeks`,`4 weeks`,`1 year; then DPT to 7 years`],[`OPV`,`Birth`,`4 weeks`,`5 years`],[`Rotavirus`,`6 weeks`,`4 weeks`,`First dose before 1 year`],[`PCV`,`6 weeks`,`4 weeks`,`1 year`],[`MR`,`9 months`,`4 weeks`,`5 years`],[`DPT booster`,`16 months`,`6 months after dose 3`,`7 years`]]},{heading:`Minor reaction or true contraindication?`,columns:[`Event`,`Contraindication?`,`What to do`],rows:[[`Fever, local swelling`,`No`,`Paracetamol only if needed`],[`Persistent crying or HHE after DPT`,`No`,`Continue schedule`],[`Febrile seizure after DPT or MR`,`No`,`Continue schedule`],[`Egg allergy`,`No`,`Give MR, MMR and influenza`],[`Anaphylaxis to previous dose`,`Yes`,`Omit that vaccine; refer`],[`Encephalopathy within 7 days of pertussis`,`Yes`,`Continue with DT or Td`]]}],remember:[`A dose given is a dose counted; never restart.`,`Only two true contraindications: anaphylaxis and post-pertussis encephalopathy.`,`Mild illness, malnutrition and prematurity: vaccinate today.`,`Rotavirus first dose before 1 year, or not at all.`,`Heat kills live vaccines; freezing kills alum vaccines.`,`fIPV is three doses: 6 weeks, 14 weeks and 9 months.`,`After 7 years, diphtheria toxoid only as Td.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`IAP ACVIP 2025: influenza vaccine now trivalent (A/H1N1, A/H3N2, B/Victoria) - B/Yamagata dropped`,`IAP ACVIP 2025: single dose of HPV vaccine for immunocompetent girls 9-15 years; 2 doses for boys; 3 doses at 15-26 years or immunocompromised`,`MoHFW 2026: national HPV campaign launched 28 February 2026 - free single dose of quadrivalent vaccine to girls aged 14, continued on routine immunisation days`,`IAP ACVIP 2025: nirsevimab (RSV monoclonal) for high-risk infants only, not universal`,`NFHS-6 (2023-24): full immunisation at 12-23 months about 87% (83.8% on the same indicator in NFHS-5)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Immunisation (UIP and IAP 2025)`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Immunisation** = making immune (active vaccine or passive antibody); **vaccination** = giving the vaccine. UIP gives vaccines against **12 diseases** free, birth to 16 years plus pregnant women.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Live**: BCG, OPV, MR/MMR, rotavirus, varicella, live JE, live hep A. **Inactivated**: whole-cell/acellular, toxoid (D, T), recombinant (HBsAg, HPV), **conjugate (Hib, PCV, TCV)** - conjugates work under 2 years and give memory.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Missed doses: did not know date, fear of fever, migration, session not held, stock-out; **zero-dose and left-outs in slums, tribal, migrant groups**; false contraindications given by doctors.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Antigen -> **dendritic cell -> MHC II -> CD4 -> germinal centre** -> plasma cells + memory B; maternal IgG blocks early live doses (**MR at 9 months**); primary series 4 weeks apart; booster 6 months or more later recalls memory.`],[`Key history`,`**MCP card / U-WIN** at every contact; exact dates; previous reactions (**anaphylaxis or encephalopathy within 7 days of pertussis** = true contraindications); immune status, steroids, recent immunoglobulin; intussusception; pregnancy before live vaccines.`],[`Key examination`,`Temperature and general condition (mild illness - vaccinate); weight (**malnutrition = vaccinate**); BCG scar (absence not a reason to revaccinate); site - **thigh under 1 year, deltoid after**; observe **30 minutes**.`],[`Investigations`,`**None before vaccination**. Anti-HBs (10 mIU/mL or more) only for HBsAg-mother infants (test at 9-12 months), health workers; CD4 15% or more before MR/varicella in HIV; serious AEFI - preserve vial and syringe.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**Fully immunised (12-23 months)**: BCG, 3 pentavalent, 3 OPV, MR-1; **completely immunised**: plus 16-24 month boosters. Dropout (Penta-1 to Penta-3) **under 10% acceptable**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**UIP**: birth BCG 0.05 mL ID, OPV-0, HepB 0.5 mL IM; **6-10-14 weeks** pentavalent, OPV, rotavirus; **fIPV 0.1 mL ID at 6, 14 weeks, 9 months**; PCV 6, 14 weeks + 9-month booster; **MR 9-12 and 16-24 months**; DPT boosters; **Td 10 and 16 years**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**IAP 2025** adds hexavalent option, MMR, TCV from 6-9 months, hep A, varicella, **trivalent influenza** yearly to 5 years, Tdap 10-12, **HPV single dose girls 9-15**, nirsevimab for high-risk infants.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Cold chain **+2 to +8 C** (OPV -20 C at stores); **VVM 1-2 use, 3-4 discard**; **shake test** for frozen adsorbed vaccine; open-vial policy 4 weeks; reconstituted **BCG, MR, JE discarded at 4 h**; AD syringe, hub cutter.`],[`Special groups`,`**Preterm - chronological age** (HepB birth dose not counted under 2000 g); steroids **2 mg/kg or 20 mg/day for 14 days** - defer live 1 month; HIV - all inactivated, BCG at birth if asymptomatic; asplenia/nephrotic - PCV + PPSV23, Hib, MenACWY.`],[`Complications`,`Minor: fever, local reaction, MR rash day 5-12, BCG ulcer-scar; **anaphylaxis** (adrenaline 0.01 mL/kg of 1 mg/mL IM); BCG adenitis (aspirate if fluctuant, no ATT); HHE; febrile seizure (not a contraindication); intussusception after rotavirus.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Previous anaphylaxis, suspected SCID, chemotherapy or transplant, disseminated BCG, suspected intussusception; **AEFI cluster = immunisation error** until proved otherwise.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Next date on MCP card + **U-WIN**; serious AEFI notified **within 24 h**, investigated **within 48 h**; catch-up: **never restart**, minimum intervals 4 weeks, rotavirus first dose before 1 year, DPT to 7 years then **Td**.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`UIP, **Mission Indradhanush / IMI 5.0** (catch-up to 5 years), U-WIN, AFP and fever-rash surveillance; polio-free 2014; MNT eliminated 2015; MR elimination target; **national HPV campaign 2026**.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**IAP ACVIP 2025**: trivalent influenza, single-dose HPV for girls 9-15, nirsevimab for high-risk only; **MoHFW 2026 HPV single dose at 14 years**; NFHS-6 full immunisation about 87%.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`A dose given is a dose counted; only two true contraindications; **no prophylactic paracetamol**; OPV most heat-sensitive, alum vaccines freeze-sensitive; after 7 years diphtheria only as Td.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Catch-up decision at the OPD desk`,steps:[{label:`Read the card - dates, not ticks`,detail:`MCP card or U-WIN; verbal history overestimates coverage`},{label:`True contraindication?`,detail:`Anaphylaxis to dose or component; encephalopathy within 7 days of pertussis vaccine (give DT/Td); live vaccines - severe immunodeficiency, pregnancy`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Count, never restart`,detail:`Continue from last documented dose; 4 weeks between primary doses, 6 months to booster`,tone:`good`},{label:`Check age ceilings`,detail:`BCG, pentavalent, PCV, fIPV to 1 year; rotavirus first dose before 1 year; OPV, MR to 5 years; DPT to 7 years then Td`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Give all due vaccines today`,detail:`Separate sites 2.5 cm apart, observe 30 min, write next date, four key messages`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`UIP versus IAP 2025 - what differs`,columns:[`Item`,`UIP (free)`,`IAP ACVIP 2025`],rows:[[`Primary series`,`Pentavalent (DTwP-HepB-Hib) + OPV + fIPV`,`DTwP/DTaP + IPV + Hib + HepB; hexavalent allowed`],[`Measles`,`MR at 9-12 and 16-24 months`,`MMR at 9 months, 15 months, 4-6 years`],[`Influenza`,`Not included`,`Trivalent, 2 doses first year then yearly to 5 years`],[`HPV`,`Single dose for girls at 14 (2026 campaign)`,`Single dose girls 9-15; 2 doses boys; 3 doses 15-26 or immunocompromised`],[`Adolescent booster`,`Td at 10 and 16 years`,`Tdap at 10-12 years`],[`Extras`,`JE in endemic districts`,`TCV, hepatitis A, varicella, MenACWY; nirsevimab for high-risk infants`]]}]},"pediatrics-growth-monitoring":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Growth monitoring and promotion** is serial plotting of growth plus counselling and action.`,`Use **WHO 2006 standards** from birth to 5 years.`,`Use **IAP 2015 charts** from 5 to 18 years; overweight and obesity at adult-equivalent BMI 23 and 27.`,`The **direction of the curve** matters more than any single point.`,`NFHS-6: 29.3% stunted, 19.0% wasted and 31.8% underweight under five.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Weight-for-age below -2 SD is **underweight**.`,`Height-for-age below -2 SD is **stunting** (chronic undernutrition).`,`Weight-for-height below -2 SD is **wasting** (acute undernutrition).`,`Below -3 SD is the severe form of each.`,`**SAM**: WFH below -3 SD, MUAC under 11.5 cm, or bilateral pitting oedema.`,`**Growth faltering**: flat or falling curve for 2-3 months, or a fall across two centile lines.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Infancy growth is driven by nutrition; childhood growth by **growth hormone and IGF-1**.`,`2. After 6 months breast milk alone cannot meet needs for energy, iron, zinc and vitamin A.`,`3. Thin watery foods fill a small stomach before energy needs are met.`,`4. Repeated infection and **environmental enteric dysfunction** cut absorption and raise needs.`,`5. Wasting from recent deficit is **reversible**; stunting from the first 1000 days is largely not.`,`6. Nutritional faltering drops weight first; **endocrine causes drop height first**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Take a **24-hour dietary recall** of everything eaten and drunk yesterday.`,`Check consistency, frequency, quantity, diversity, responsive feeding and hygiene.`,`Ask who feeds the child, from what utensil, and whether screens are on.`,`Ask birth weight and gestation; plot preterms by **corrected age**.`,`Ask about recurrent diarrhoea, chronic cough, **TB contact** and HIV risk.`,`Ask parents' heights to work out the mid-parental height.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Weigh **naked** on the same calibrated scale each visit.`,`Length lying under 2 years; height standing after 2 years (differs by **0.7 cm**).`,`Measure **MUAC** on the left mid-arm in every child aged 6-59 months.`,`Press both feet for 3 seconds to look for **bilateral pitting oedema**.`,`Plot every point in front of the mother and look at at least three points.`,`Look for micronutrient signs, murmur, clubbing, goitre and dysmorphism.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Most faltering in Indian primary care is **nutritional and psychosocial**.`,`Check **haemoglobin**; under 11 g/dL at 6-59 months needs treatment.`,`Check urine routine and culture for occult infection.`,`Test for TB with chest X-ray, TST or IGRA and gastric NAAT when indicated.`,`Targeted tests only: TSH, anti-tTG IgA, HIV, renal function.`,`**Bone age** separates constitutional delay from familial short stature.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months**, then complementary food with breastfeeding to 2 years.`,`Food should be **thick enough to stay on a spoon**; mash, do not dilute.`,`Add **one teaspoon of ghee or oil** and a protein source at each meal.`,`Aim for at least **5 of 8 food groups** daily.`,`Feed responsively from a separate bowl, without force and without screens.`,`Give one or two specific changes and write them on the card.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`There is **no drug for nutritional faltering**; no appetite stimulants or tonics.`,`Link to anganwadi supplementary nutrition: **500 kcal and 12-15 g protein** a day.`,`Severely underweight children get 800 kcal and 20-25 g protein a day.`,`Set a catch-up target of **1.5-2 times** normal weight gain.`,`Re-weigh in **2-4 weeks** with a written date.`,`Treat causes: levothyroxine for hypothyroidism, gluten-free diet for coeliac disease.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Anganwadi weighing is **monthly under 3 years** and quarterly from 3-6 years.`,`Refer complicated SAM to a **Nutrition Rehabilitation Centre**.`,`Refer growth velocity **under 5 cm a year** after age 2, or height below -3 SD.`,`Refer a head crossing centiles, a dysmorphic child or disordered puberty.`,`Refer urgently faltering with developmental regression, suspected TB or HIV.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**Poshan Abhiyaan** and Poshan 2.0 target stunting, anaemia and low birth weight.`,`The focus is the **first 1000 days**, from conception to the second birthday.`,`IFA syrup 1 mL twice weekly from 6-59 months under Anemia Mukt Bharat.`,`Vitamin A 6-monthly and albendazole on National Deworming Days.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and micronutrients`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Vitamin D3 (prophylaxis)`,`400 IU oral daily`,`Birth to 1 year`,`Hypercalcaemia only with overdose`],[`Vitamin D3 (rickets)`,`2000 IU oral daily plus calcium 500 mg`,`At least 12 weeks`,`Check calcium if overdose suspected`],[`IFA syrup (AMB)`,`1 mL (20 mg iron) twice weekly`,`6-59 months`,`Black stools, tooth staining; not with milk`],[`Iron (therapeutic)`,`3 mg/kg/day elemental iron oral`,`About 3 months in total`,`Continue 2-3 months after Hb normal`],[`Vitamin A`,`1 lakh IU at 9 months, then 2 lakh IU`,`6-monthly to 5 years`,`Vomiting, bulging fontanelle if excess`],[`Albendazole`,`200 mg at 1-2 years; 400 mg over 2 years`,`Every 6 months`,`Chew with water after food`],[`Zinc (diarrhoea)`,`10 mg under 6 months; 20 mg after`,`14 days`,`Vomiting on empty stomach`],[`Levothyroxine`,`10-15 microgram/kg/day newborn; 3-5 older child`,`Long term`,`Empty stomach; away from iron and soy`]]},{heading:`WHO classification (under 5 years)`,columns:[`Index`,`What it shows`,`Moderate (-2 to -3 SD)`,`Severe (below -3 SD)`],rows:[[`Weight-for-age`,`Underweight; screening only`,`Moderately underweight`,`Severely underweight`],[`Height-for-age`,`Stunting; chronic`,`Moderate stunting`,`Severe stunting`],[`Weight-for-height`,`Wasting; acute`,`MAM`,`SAM`],[`MUAC (6-59 months)`,`Best predictor of death`,`11.5-12.5 cm`,`Under 11.5 cm`],[`Bilateral oedema`,`Oedematous malnutrition`,`Not applicable`,`SAM whatever the weight`]]},{heading:`Complementary feeding by age`,columns:[`Age`,`Consistency`,`Meals`,`Amount per meal`],rows:[[`6-8 months`,`Thick mashed`,`2-3 plus breastfeeds`,`2-3 spoons up to half katori`],[`9-11 months`,`Chopped, finger foods`,`3-4 plus 1-2 snacks`,`Half to three-quarter katori`],[`12-23 months`,`Family food`,`3-4 plus 1-2 snacks`,`Three-quarter to full katori`]]},{heading:`Growth numbers for the OPD`,columns:[`Measure`,`Key values`],rows:[[`Weight`,`Doubles by 5 months, triples by 1 year, quadruples by 2 years`],[`Estimated weight 1-6 years`,`(Age x 2) + 8 kg`],[`Length`,`50 cm birth, 75 cm 1 year, 100 cm 4 years`],[`Height velocity after 2 years`,`5-7 cm a year; under 5 cm needs evaluation`],[`Head circumference`,`34-35 cm birth, 47 cm 1 year, 49 cm 2 years`],[`Anterior fontanelle`,`Closes at 9-18 months`]]}],remember:[`Direction beats position: plot at least three points.`,`Say stunting, wasting or underweight; weight-for-age cannot tell short from thin.`,`Stunting is set in the first 1000 days; wasting is reversible.`,`A thin 8-month-old in India usually has a watery feed: thicken it, add oil.`,`Weight falls first in nutrition; height falls first in endocrine disease.`,`MUAC under 11.5 cm is SAM and predicts death best.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`NFHS-6 (2023-24): under-five stunting 29.3% (35.5% in NFHS-5), wasting 19.0% (severe 5.2%), underweight 31.8% - stunting falling, wasting and underweight static`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Growth monitoring and faltering`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Growth monitoring and promotion** = serial measurement and plotting **followed by counselling and action**; **growth faltering** = flat or falling curve over 2-3 months, fall across **two centile lines**, or new fall **below -2 SD**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**WHO 2006 (0-5 y)**: WFA under -2 SD **underweight**, HFA under -2 SD **stunting**, WFH under -2 SD **wasting**; under -3 SD severe. **SAM**: WFH under -3 SD, **MUAC under 11.5 cm** or oedema; MAM MUAC 11.5-12.5. **IAP 2015 (5-18 y)**: BMI adult-equivalent **23 / 27**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Mostly **nutritional and psychosocial**: late, watery complementary feeds, repeated infection, LBW, maternal undernutrition, poverty; organic: TB, UTI, HIV, coeliac, CHD, CP, hypothyroidism. **NFHS-6: stunting 29.3%, wasting 19.0%, underweight 31.8%**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Energy/protein deficit -> **low IGF-1, GH resistance** -> weight falls first, then length; **environmental enteric dysfunction** + infection-malnutrition cycle -> stunting set in **first 1000 days**, largely irreversible after 2 years.`],[`Key history`,`**24-hour dietary recall**; IYCF domains (age started, consistency, frequency, amount, diversity, responsive feeding, hygiene); birth weight and gestation; infections, TB contact; stools; development; family food security, mother's health; parents' heights.`],[`Key examination`,`Naked weight on same scale; **length lying under 2 y, height standing after** (0.7 cm difference); HC; **MUAC 6-59 months**; **bilateral pitting oedema 3 s**; plot in front of mother; micronutrient signs; organic clues; body proportions.`],[`Investigations`,`Most need none; **Hb**, urine routine and culture, TB work-up if contact or cough over 2 weeks, stool microscopy; targeted **TSH, anti-tTG IgA, HIV**; bone age for short stature.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Single point never diagnostic - **at least three points**; short stature = height under -2 SD with **velocity under 5 cm/year after 2 years** or far below **mid-parental height (plus or minus 8.5 cm)**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Feeding counselling**: EBF 6 months; complementary from 6 months - **200, 300, 550 kcal/day** at 6-8, 9-11, 12-23 months; **thick, add 1 tsp ghee/oil**, 5 of 8 food groups, animal food daily, responsive feeding; one or two specific changes.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`ICDS supplementary nutrition (**500 kcal + 12-15 g protein; 800 kcal for severely underweight**); treat cause (TB, UTI, hypothyroidism - levothyroxine 10-15 mcg/kg neonate); **SAM -> NRC or CMAM**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Infantometer, stadiometer, calibrated scale (10 g under 2 y), non-stretch tape, **colour-coded MUAC tape**, MCP card and **Poshan Tracker**.`],[`Special groups`,`**Preterm - corrected age** until 2 years; overweight child - screen time under 1 h at 2-5 y, no sugary drinks, 60 min activity; endocrine short stature - **height falls before weight (plump child)**.`],[`Complications`,`Stunting -> poor cognition, school performance, adult height and earnings; wasting -> mortality; **double burden** - early stunting then rapid gain -> diabetes, hypertension.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`WFH under -3 SD, MUAC under 11.5 cm or oedema; HC crossing centiles; faltering with TB/HIV clues or **regression**; velocity under 5 cm/year after 2; height under -3 SD; disproportion or dysmorphism.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Anganwadi weighing **monthly under 3, quarterly 3-6 years**; catch-up target **1.5-2 times normal gain**, re-weigh in **2-4 weeks** with a written date.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Poshan Abhiyaan / Poshan 2.0**, ICDS take-home ration, AMB IFA 1 mL twice weekly, vitamin A 9 doses, NDD albendazole, zinc + ORS for diarrhoea, WASH.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**NFHS-6 (2023-24)**: stunting fell to 29.3%, but wasting (19.0%) and underweight (31.8%) barely changed - wasting remains the stubborn indicator.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Direction beats position**; WFA cannot separate short from thin; birth weight doubles at 5 months, triples at 1 year; **(age x 2) + 8 kg** for 1-6 years; length 75 cm at 1 year.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Faltering curve - OPD approach`,steps:[{label:`Confirm the curve`,detail:`Same scale, naked weight, at least three points; correct for prematurity`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Classify by z-score and MUAC`,detail:`Wasting, stunting, underweight; MUAC under 11.5 cm or oedema = SAM`,tone:`decision`},{label:`SAM or danger sign?`,detail:`Appetite test and complications - NRC or CMAM`,tone:`warn`},{label:`24-hour recall and observed meal`,detail:`Usual fix: thicken food, add 1 tsp ghee/oil, increase frequency, responsive feeding`},{label:`Targeted tests if no response`,detail:`Hb, urine culture, TB work-up, TSH, anti-tTG, HIV as clues suggest`},{label:`Re-weigh in 2-4 weeks`,detail:`Target catch-up 1.5-2 times normal gain; ICDS link`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Nutritional versus endocrine short child`,columns:[`Feature`,`Nutritional / psychosocial`,`Endocrine (hypothyroid, GH deficiency)`],rows:[[`What falls first`,`Weight, then length`,`Height, weight preserved`],[`Look`,`Thin, wasted`,`Short and plump`],[`Bone age`,`Delayed`,`Markedly delayed`],[`Velocity`,`Recovers with feeding`,`Stays under 5 cm/year`],[`Action`,`Feeding plan, treat infection`,`TSH, GH studies; levothyroxine or GH by specialist`]]}]},"pediatrics-imnci":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**IMNCI** is India's syndromic, colour-coded case management for sick children from birth to 5 years.`,`It adapts WHO IMCI by adding the **first week of life**.`,`Three components: health-worker skills, health-system support, and family and community practices.`,`Two charts: **young infant 0-59 days** and **child 2 months to 5 years**.`,`Six steps: **assess, classify, identify treatment, treat, counsel, follow up**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Pink**: urgent pre-referral treatment and referral.`,`**Yellow**: specific treatment at the facility with a follow-up date.`,`**Green**: home management with the four rules of home care.`,`General danger signs: **unable to drink, vomits everything, convulsions, lethargic or unconscious**.`,`Any one general danger sign makes the child pink.`,`Fast breathing: **60 under 2 months, 50 at 2-11 months, 40 at 12-59 months**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Without a laboratory, a few reproducible signs pick out the children most likely to die.`,`2. Danger signs mark shock, sepsis, meningitis or hypoglycaemia, where oral treatment fails.`,`3. Stiff, hypoxic lungs raise respiratory rate before cyanosis appears.`,`4. Falling lung compliance pulls in the soft lower chest wall as **chest indrawing**.`,`5. Pneumonia, diarrhoea, malaria and malnutrition overlap, so every visit checks everything.`,`6. Young infants **cannot localise infection**, so all serious infection is grouped as PSBI.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask whether this is an **initial or follow-up visit**.`,`Ask the general danger sign questions first.`,`Cough for **14 days or more** needs evaluation for TB, asthma or pertussis.`,`Diarrhoea for 14 days or more is **persistent**; visible blood is **dysentery**.`,`For fever ask duration, malaria risk and **measles in the last 3 months**.`,`Assess feeding in every child under 2 years and any child with anaemia or low weight.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Look for lethargy and watch the child drink or breastfeed.`,`Count breaths for a **full minute** in a calm child.`,`Look for chest indrawing and listen for **stridor in a calm child**.`,`**SpO2 under 90%** means severe pneumonia needing oxygen and referral.`,`Dehydration: general condition, sunken eyes, drinking test and **skin pinch**.`,`Check stiff neck, tender swelling behind the ear, **palmar pallor**, MUAC and oedema.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`IMNCI works **without any laboratory**; no test delays pre-referral treatment.`,`**Pulse oximetry** is the one device the revised charts ask for.`,`Malaria RDT or smear in every febrile child in a risk area.`,`Check glucose where possible; treat under **54 mg/dL** in children, 45 in young infants.`,`Do not order chest X-ray for yellow pneumonia or Widal for fever under 7 days.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Pneumonia: **oral amoxicillin 40 mg/kg/dose twice daily for 5 days**.`,`Chest indrawing without danger signs is now **treated at home** (2014 change).`,`Diarrhoea: ORS by plan plus **zinc 20 mg daily for 14 days** (10 mg under 6 months).`,`Dysentery: **ciprofloxacin 15 mg/kg/dose twice daily for 3 days**.`,`Acute ear infection: amoxicillin for 5 days, paracetamol and **dry wicking**.`,`Some pallor: **iron 3 mg/kg/day** for 3 months, with albendazole if over 1 year.`,`No cough syrups, antihistamines or antidiarrhoeals under 5 years.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Pink child: first dose of **ampicillin plus gentamicin**, or ceftriaxone if meningitis suspected.`,`Prevent hypoglycaemia with breast milk or **sugar water** (4 teaspoons in 200 mL).`,`Convulsions: **rectal diazepam 0.5 mg/kg** (max 10 mg) or buccal midazolam.`,`Suspected severe malaria: **artesunate IM** or rectal artesunate, then refer.`,`Keep the child warm and send a written referral note.`,`If referral is refused, give the simplified regimen and review **daily**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Four rules of home care: **extra fluids, continue feeding, when to return immediately, when to follow up**.`,`Follow up pneumonia in **3 days** and dysentery in 2 days.`,`Follow up ear infection and persistent diarrhoea in 5 days; anaemia in 14 days.`,`At follow-up use the follow-up box; **do not reclassify from scratch**.`,`Teach with checking questions: ask **'How will you give this?'**`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`Delivered by ANMs, AWWs and ASHAs under NHM with HBNC and HBYC.`,`**SAANS** strengthens pneumonia detection and pulse oximetry.`,`**STOP Diarrhoea Campaign** promotes ORS and zinc.`,`Every sick visit also updates immunisation, vitamin A and deworming.`,`Limitation: skills decay **without supportive supervision**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`IMNCI drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Amoxicillin (dispersible 250 mg)`,`40 mg/kg/dose oral twice daily`,`5 days`,`Diarrhoea, rash; complete the course`],[`Amoxicillin (young infant)`,`50 mg/kg/dose oral twice daily`,`7 days`,`Dissolve in breast milk`],[`Gentamicin`,`7.5 mg/kg IM pre-referral`,`Single dose before referral`,`Nephrotoxic, ototoxic`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`80-100 mg/kg IM/IV, max 2 g`,`Single pre-referral dose`,`Avoid in jaundiced neonates`],[`Ciprofloxacin`,`15 mg/kg/dose oral twice daily`,`3 days`,`Review in 2 days; azithromycin if resistant`],[`ORS and zinc`,`ORS by plan; zinc 20 mg (10 mg under 6 mo)`,`Zinc 14 days`,`Give ORS slowly by spoon`],[`Diazepam`,`0.5 mg/kg rectal, max 10 mg`,`Repeat once after 10 min`,`Respiratory depression`],[`Artesunate`,`3 mg/kg IM/IV (under 20 kg); rectal 10 mg/kg`,`Pre-referral`,`Delayed haemolysis`],[`Paracetamol`,`15 mg/kg/dose 6-hourly`,`Max 60 mg/kg/day`,`Only for fever 38.5 C or pain`],[`Vitamin A`,`50,000 IU, 1 lakh or 2 lakh IU by age`,`Days 1, 2 and 14`,`Vomiting, bulging fontanelle`]]},{heading:`IMNCI colour code`,columns:[`Colour`,`Examples`,`Action`],rows:[[`Pink`,`Severe pneumonia, severe dehydration, mastoiditis, complicated SAM`,`First dose, prevent hypoglycaemia, refer`],[`Yellow`,`Pneumonia, some dehydration, dysentery, malaria, ear infection`,`Treat, teach mother, fixed follow-up`],[`Green`,`Cough or cold, no dehydration, no anaemia`,`Home care and return signs`]]},{heading:`Cough or difficult breathing (2 months-5 years)`,columns:[`Classification`,`Signs`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Severe pneumonia`,`Danger sign, stridor when calm, or SpO2 under 90%`,`First dose antibiotic, oxygen, refer`],[`Pneumonia`,`Fast breathing or chest indrawing`,`Oral amoxicillin 5 days; review in 3 days`],[`Cough or cold`,`No pneumonia signs`,`Home care; evaluate if cough 14 days`]]}],remember:[`Assess, classify, identify treatment, treat, counsel, follow up.`,`Danger signs first; one danger sign overrides everything.`,`IMNCI classifies, it does not diagnose.`,`Fast breathing cut-offs: 60, 50, 40 by age.`,`Dehydration needs two signs in a column, never one.`,`Chest indrawing without danger signs: oral amoxicillin at home.`,`No cough syrups or antidiarrhoeals under five.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`WHO 2024 (pneumonia and diarrhoea): chest-indrawing pneumonia without danger signs - oral amoxicillin 5 days as outpatient; fast breathing alone 3 days in low-HIV settings`,`WHO PSBI 2024: young infant clinical severe infection when referral refused - gentamicin 7 days preferred (2 days if not feasible); fast breathing at 0-6 days referred; critical illness without referral - ampicillin plus gentamicin at least 10 days`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - IMNCI`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Integrated Management of Neonatal and Childhood Illness** - India's adaptation of WHO IMCI adding the **first week of life**; syndromic, colour-coded case management for **0-59 days and 2 months-5 years**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Pink** - pre-referral treatment and urgent referral; **yellow** - treat at facility with follow-up date; **green** - home care. Fast breathing: **60 (under 2 months), 50 (2-11 months), 40 (12-59 months)**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Under-five deaths: neonatal causes, pneumonia, diarrhoea, malaria, measles - **malnutrition underlies about 45%**; delay in recognising danger at home.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Danger signs mark **shock, sepsis, meningitis, cerebral malaria, hypoglycaemia** -> oral route fails; hypoxaemia and stiff lungs -> **fast breathing, chest indrawing**; young infants **do not localise** -> treated together as PSBI.`],[`Key history`,`Initial or follow-up visit; **general danger signs** (cannot drink, vomits everything, convulsions, lethargic); cough duration (**14 days or more** - TB, asthma, pertussis); diarrhoea duration and blood; fever days, malaria risk, measles in 3 months; ear; feeding; immunisation.`],[`Key examination`,`Count RR 1 minute calm; chest indrawing, stridor, wheeze; **SpO2 under 90%**; two dehydration signs; stiff neck; mastoid swelling; **visible wasting, oedema, MUAC**; **palmar pallor**; young infant - temp, umbilicus, pustules, jaundice, observe breastfeed.`],[`Investigations`,`Designed to need **no laboratory**; **pulse oximetry**; malaria **RDT or smear** in every fever in risk area; glucometer (treat under 54 mg/dL child, 45 mg/dL young infant); Hb if available.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Severe pneumonia/very severe disease = any danger sign, stridor in calm child or SpO2 under 90%; **pneumonia = fast breathing and/or chest indrawing**; dehydration on **two signs in a column**; persistent diarrhoea 14 days or more.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Amoxicillin 40 mg/kg/dose BD** - 5 days (3 days fast-breathing only, low HIV); dysentery **ciprofloxacin 15 mg/kg BD 3 days**; ORS + **zinc 20 mg x 14 days** (10 mg under 6 months); iron 3 mg/kg/day; paracetamol 15 mg/kg.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Pink: **ampicillin 50 mg/kg + gentamicin 7.5 mg/kg IM** or **ceftriaxone 80-100 mg/kg**; **artesunate 2.4-3 mg/kg IM** or rectal 10 mg/kg; **10% dextrose 5 mL/kg** or sugar water; diazepam 0.5 mg/kg PR or **midazolam 0.2-0.3 mg/kg buccal/IN**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Teach-show-practise-check**; dispersible amoxicillin in breast milk; ear **wicking TDS**; pMDI + spacer for wheeze; pulse oximeter; written referral note.`],[`Special groups`,`**Young infant**: PSBI - critical illness never at home; CSI refused referral - amoxicillin 7 days + **gentamicin 7 days (2 if not feasible)**; fast breathing 7-59 days amoxicillin 7 days, **0-6 days refer**.`],[`Complications`,`Over-treatment of wheeze as pneumonia; misses TB, CHD, UTI, surgical abdomen; **skills decay without supervision**; drug stock-outs undermine the algorithm.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Any general danger sign; stridor in calm child or SpO2 under 90%; stiff neck; mastoiditis; **severe pallor**; complicated SAM; any danger sign in young infant; cough 14 days or fever over 7 days.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`**Pneumonia 3 days**, dysentery 2, fever 2, ear 5, persistent diarrhoea and feeding problem 5, anaemia and low weight 14 days; use the **follow-up box**, do not reclassify.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`NHM/RMNCH+A via ANM, ASHA, AWW; linked to **HBNC, HBYC, STOP Diarrhoea, SAANS**; F-IMNCI at FRUs; every visit updates immunisation, vitamin A, deworming, feeding.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WHO 2024** pneumonia-diarrhoea guideline: chest-indrawing pneumonia outpatient amoxicillin 5 days, fast breathing 3 days in low-HIV areas; **WHO PSBI 2024** simplified regimen gentamicin 7 days preferred.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Assess-classify-identify treatment-treat-counsel-follow up; **danger sign overrides everything**; count RR full minute; four rules of home care - **extra fluids, continue feeding, when to return immediately, follow-up date**.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`IMNCI 2 months-5 years - the case management sequence`,steps:[{label:`General danger signs first`,detail:`Cannot drink, vomits everything, convulsions, lethargic - any one = pink`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Main symptoms`,detail:`Cough/difficult breathing, diarrhoea, fever, ear problem - classify each`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Check every child`,detail:`Malnutrition (MUAC, oedema), anaemia (palmar pallor), immunisation, feeding`},{label:`Pink - pre-referral and refer`,detail:`First antibiotic dose, artesunate, glucose, warmth, oxygen if SpO2 under 90%`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Yellow - treat and fix follow-up`,detail:`Amoxicillin 40 mg/kg BD, ORS-zinc, ciprofloxacin for dysentery, iron; follow-up in 2-14 days`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Green - four rules of home care`,detail:`Extra fluids, continue feeding, when to return, follow-up date`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Cough or difficult breathing - classification`,columns:[`Class`,`Signs`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Severe pneumonia / very severe disease`,`Danger sign, stridor in calm child, SpO2 under 90%`,`First dose injectable antibiotic, oxygen, refer`],[`Pneumonia`,`Fast breathing and/or chest indrawing`,`Amoxicillin 40 mg/kg BD 5 days (3 if fast breathing only, low HIV); review day 3`],[`Cough or cold`,`No pneumonia signs`,`Soothe throat; no cough syrups; evaluate if 14 days or more`]]}]},"pediatrics-diarrhoea-dehydration":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Diarrhoea** is 3 or more loose or watery stools in 24 hours.`,`Consistency matters more than number; pasty stools of a breastfed baby are normal.`,`Children die of **water and salt loss**, so rehydration is the core treatment.`,`Diarrhoea causes about **8-9% of under-five deaths**; NFHS-5: only 60% got ORS, 30% zinc.`,`**Rotavirus** is the commonest cause of severe dehydrating diarrhoea under 2 years.`,`ETEC, cholera, norovirus and Giardia cause other watery episodes; **Shigella** causes most dysentery.`,`Risk of death: age under 1, no breastfeeding, malnutrition, **recent measles**, HIV.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Acute watery diarrhoea**: under 14 days without blood, including cholera.`,`**Dysentery**: visible blood in the stool.`,`**Persistent diarrhoea**: an acute episode lasting 14 days or more.`,`Severe dehydration (10% or more deficit) gets **Plan C**.`,`Some dehydration (5-10%) gets **Plan B**; no dehydration gets **Plan A**.`,`Each classification needs **two signs in the same column**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Diarrhoea happens when gut **secretion exceeds absorption**.`,`2. Cholera toxin raises cAMP, opening the **CFTR chloride channel**, so fluid pours out.`,`3. Rotavirus destroys villous tip cells and their lactase, reducing absorption.`,`4. The **sodium-glucose co-transporter** stays intact, so ORS is absorbed even then.`,`5. Stool loses bicarbonate and potassium, causing **acidosis and hypokalaemia**.`,`6. Fluid loss shrinks the interstitium first, then plasma volume, then causes shock.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask duration: **14 days or more is persistent**.`,`Ask stool type: rice-water, **blood and mucus**, or greasy and bulky.`,`Ask about vomiting everything and any **bilious vomiting**.`,`Ask about urine output and thirst.`,`Ask what fluids and drugs were given; many children arrive already over-treated.`,`Red-currant-jelly stool with drawing up of legs suggests **intussusception**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Look first for danger signs and **shock**: weak pulse, cold hands, refill over 3 seconds.`,`General condition: well, **restless and irritable**, or lethargic.`,`Eyes: normal or **sunken**; ask the mother if they look unusual.`,`Offer a cup: drinks normally, **drinks eagerly**, or drinks poorly.`,`**Skin pinch**: goes back slowly (under 2 s) or very slowly (over 2 s).`,`Look for **SAM**, because dehydration signs mislead and the fluid plan changes.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Most acute diarrhoea needs no test.**`,`Check sodium, potassium, creatinine and blood gas in severe dehydration or seizures.`,`Check **blood glucose** in any lethargic, convulsing or malnourished child.`,`Stool culture only in suspected cholera outbreaks or failed dysentery treatment.`,`**Visible blood decides antibiotics**, not stool pus cells.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Plan A rules: **extra fluid, zinc, continue feeding, when to return**.`,`Mix one ORS sachet in **1 litre of clean water**; discard after 24 hours.`,`After each stool give **50-100 mL under 2 years**, 100-200 mL at 2-10 years.`,`If the child vomits, **wait 10 minutes** and give more slowly.`,`Give **zinc for 14 days**: 10 mg under 6 months, 20 mg from 6 months.`,`Continue feeding and give **one extra meal a day for 2 weeks** after recovery.`,`Plan B: **ORS 75 mL/kg over 4 hours** at the facility, then reassess.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Plan C: **Ringer lactate 100 mL/kg** IV.`,`Under 12 months: 30 mL/kg in 1 hour, then 70 mL/kg over 5 hours.`,`From 1 to 5 years: 30 mL/kg in 30 minutes, then 70 mL/kg over 2.5 hours.`,`No IV: **ORS by nasogastric tube 20 mL/kg/hour** for 6 hours.`,`Dysentery: **ciprofloxacin 15 mg/kg/dose twice daily for 3 days**; review in 2 days.`,`Suspected cholera: **azithromycin 20 mg/kg single dose**, plus Plan C.`,`**Never loperamide** or other antimotility drugs in children.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`SAM: **no Plan C unless in shock**; the weak heart fails with boluses.`,`SAM rehydration: **ReSoMal 5 mL/kg every 30 minutes** for 2 hours, then 5-10 mL/kg/hour.`,`SAM in shock: 15 mL/kg over 1 hour, repeat once if improving.`,`Hypernatraemia: correct over **48 hours**, sodium down no more than 10-12 mmol/L per day.`,`Persistent diarrhoea: zinc, multivitamins, six small meals and curd for half the milk.`]},{title:`Complications, red flags and prevention`,points:[`Lethargy, cannot drink or very slow skin pinch means severe dehydration.`,`Pallor, oedema and low urine a week after bloody diarrhoea means **HUS**.`,`Abdominal distension with absent bowel sounds suggests **hypokalaemic ileus**.`,`Puffy eyelids during ORS mean **overhydration**; pause ORS.`,`Convulsions need immediate glucose and sodium checks.`,`Prevent with exclusive breastfeeding, **rotavirus and measles vaccines**, handwashing, safe water and toilets.`,`**STOP Diarrhoea Campaign** (1 July-31 August) pre-positions ORS; notify cholera clusters the same day.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and fluids`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Low-osmolarity ORS`,`Plan B 75 mL/kg oral; NG 20 mL/kg/h if no IV`,`Plan B over 4 hours`,`Vomiting if fast; puffy eyelids if excess`],[`Ringer lactate`,`100 mL/kg IV as 30 then 70 mL/kg`,`6 h under 1 year; 3 h over 1 year`,`Not in SAM unless shock`],[`Zinc`,`10 mg under 6 months; 20 mg after, oral`,`14 days`,`Vomiting on empty stomach`],[`Ciprofloxacin`,`15 mg/kg/dose oral twice daily`,`3 days`,`Separate from zinc and milk`],[`Azithromycin`,`Cholera 20 mg/kg single; shigella 10-20 mg/kg daily`,`1 dose; 3 days for shigella`,`Vomiting, QT prolongation`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`50-100 mg/kg IV/IM once daily`,`3-5 days (severe dysentery)`,`Diarrhoea, biliary sludge`],[`Metronidazole`,`Amoeba 10 mg/kg TDS; Giardia 5 mg/kg TDS`,`5-10 days; 5-7 days`,`Metallic taste; add luminal agent for amoeba`],[`Ondansetron`,`0.15 mg/kg oral once, max 4 mg`,`Single dose, over 6 months`,`QT prolongation, more stools`],[`ReSoMal (SAM)`,`5 mL/kg every 30 min, then 5-10 mL/kg/h`,`Up to 12 hours`,`Overload if given fast`]]},{heading:`Dehydration classification (two or more signs)`,columns:[`Feature`,`Severe`,`Some`,`No dehydration`],rows:[[`General condition`,`Lethargic or unconscious`,`Restless, irritable`,`Well, alert`],[`Eyes`,`Sunken`,`Sunken`,`Normal`],[`Drinking`,`Not able or poorly`,`Eagerly, thirsty`,`Normally`],[`Skin pinch`,`Very slowly (over 2 s)`,`Slowly`,`Immediately`],[`Deficit`,`10% or more`,`5-10%`,`Under 5%`],[`Plan`,`C`,`B`,`A`]]},{heading:`Types of diarrhoea`,columns:[`Type`,`Definition`,`Usual cause`,`Key treatment`],rows:[[`Acute watery`,`Under 14 days, no blood`,`Rotavirus, ETEC`,`ORS and zinc; no antibiotic`],[`Cholera`,`Rice-water stool, severe dehydration`,`Vibrio cholerae`,`Plan C, azithromycin, notify`],[`Dysentery`,`Visible blood`,`Shigella`,`Ciprofloxacin 3 days`],[`Persistent`,`14 days or more`,`Mucosal damage, malnutrition`,`Zinc, feeding plan`]]}],remember:[`Four signs, two in a column, decide the plan.`,`Plan B 75 mL/kg in 4 hours; Plan C 100 mL/kg as 30 then 70.`,`ORS plus zinc for 14 days is the whole prescription for most children.`,`Antibiotics only for blood in stool and suspected cholera.`,`Vomiting ORS: wait 10 minutes, then go slower.`,`In SAM use ReSoMal slowly; a bolus can kill.`,`Never antimotility drugs in a child.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - Acute diarrhoea and dehydration`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**3 or more loose or watery stools in 24 h** (or more than usual for that child); acute under 14 days, **persistent 14 days or more**, **dysentery = visible blood**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**IMNCI dehydration - two signs in a column**: severe (10% or more) - **Plan C**; some (5-10%) - **Plan B**; no dehydration (under 5%) - **Plan A**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**Rotavirus** (severe under 2 y), ETEC, cholera, Cryptosporidium, norovirus, Giardia; **Shigella** for dysentery; risk of death: under 1 year, not breastfed, **malnutrition, measles in 3 months**, HIV.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Toxin -> **cAMP/cGMP -> CFTR chloride secretion** -> isotonic loss; but **SGLT1 sodium-glucose co-transport stays intact** -> basis of ORS; losses -> hypovolaemia, **bicarbonate loss acidosis, hypokalaemia**, sodium disorders.`],[`Key history`,`Duration, stool number and nature, **blood**, vomiting (vomits everything, bilious), urine output, fever, convulsions, fluids and drugs given at home, measles, HIV, household cases (cholera).`],[`Key examination`,`Danger signs and shock first; **general condition, sunken eyes, drinking test, skin pinch (very slow over 2 s)**; weight (1 kg = 1 L); SAM signs; acidotic breathing; distension; other infection foci.`],[`Investigations`,`Most need none; **electrolytes, creatinine, VBG** for severe or IV cases, convulsions, doughy skin; glucose; stool culture only for cholera outbreak or failed dysentery; persistent - urine culture, HIV, reducing substances.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Severe: lethargic, sunken eyes, drinks poorly, pinch very slow; some: restless, sunken eyes, drinks eagerly, pinch slow - **any two in a column**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Plan A**: ORS **50-100 mL (under 2 y), 100-200 mL (2-10 y) per stool**; **zinc 20 mg x 14 days (10 mg under 6 months)**; continue feeding; **Plan B ORS 75 mL/kg over 4 h**, reassess.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Plan C: Ringer lactate 100 mL/kg** - under 1 y 30 mL/kg in 1 h then 70 in 5 h; 1-5 y 30 in 30 min then 70 in 2.5 h; no IV - **NG ORS 20 mL/kg/h x 6 h**. Dysentery **ciprofloxacin 15 mg/kg BD 3 days**; cholera **azithromycin 20 mg/kg single dose**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Low-osmolarity ORS 245 mOsm/L** (Na 75, glucose 75); 1 sachet in 1 L, discard after 24 h; teaspoon every 1-2 min under 2 y; vomit - wait 10 min then slower; **puffy eyelids = overhydration**.`],[`Special groups`,`**SAM - no Plan C unless shock**; ReSoMal 5 mL/kg every 30 min x 2 h then 5-10 mL/kg/h; shock in SAM 15 mL/kg over 1 h; young infant - refer; ondansetron 0.15 mg/kg once if vomiting blocks ORS (over 6 months).`],[`Complications`,`Hypovolaemic shock, **hypo- and hypernatraemia** (correct Na by max 10-12 mmol/L/24 h), hypokalaemic ileus, acidosis, AKI, **HUS after Shigella**, persistent diarrhoea, malnutrition.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Severe dehydration without IV facility, SAM, young infant, dysentery with toxicity or convulsions, **pallor-oliguria a week after bloody diarrhoea (HUS)**, bilious vomiting, distension, red-currant jelly stool.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Dysentery **2 days**, persistent **5 days**, Plan A 5 days if not better; one extra meal a day for 2 weeks; notify cholera clusters under IDSP/IHIP.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**STOP Diarrhoea Campaign** (ORS pre-positioning, ORS-zinc corners), EBF, **rotavirus and measles vaccines**, handwashing with soap, safe water, Swachh Bharat.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - **WHO 2024** guideline re-endorses low-osmolarity ORS, zinc 10-14 days (India 14 days), antibiotics only for dysentery and suspected cholera.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Never antimotility drugs in children; no antibiotic for watery diarrhoea; salt-sugar solution only a stop-gap; **ORS + zinc + feeding** is the whole prescription.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Dehydration - choose the plan`,steps:[{label:`Danger signs or shock?`,detail:`Weak pulse, cold hands, CRT over 3 s, lethargy - start Plan C now`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Two signs in a column`,detail:`General condition, eyes, drinking, skin pinch`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Severe - Plan C`,detail:`RL 100 mL/kg: 30 then 70 mL/kg (1 h + 5 h under 1 y; 30 min + 2.5 h older); repeat first 30 mL/kg if pulse weak`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Some - Plan B`,detail:`ORS 75 mL/kg over 4 h, reassess and reclassify`},{label:`None - Plan A`,detail:`ORS after each stool, zinc 14 days, continue feeding, when to return`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Types of diarrhoea - treatment`,columns:[`Type`,`Key feature`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Acute watery`,`Under 14 days, no blood`,`ORS + zinc; no antibiotic`],[`Suspected cholera`,`Rice-water stool, severe dehydration, over 2 y or outbreak`,`Plan C + azithromycin 20 mg/kg once; notify`],[`Dysentery`,`Visible blood`,`Ciprofloxacin 15 mg/kg BD 3 days; review 2 days`],[`Persistent`,`14 days or more`,`Zinc, multivitamin, feeding plan; refer if dehydrated`],[`Diarrhoea in SAM`,`Signs unreliable`,`ReSoMal slowly; IV only for shock 15 mL/kg`]]}]},"pediatrics-severe-acute-malnutrition":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`SAM at 6-59 months is any one of three criteria.`,`**Weight-for-height below -3 SD**, **MUAC under 11.5 cm**, or **bilateral pitting oedema**.`,`**MAM** is WFH -3 to -2 SD or MUAC 11.5 to under 12.5 cm, without oedema.`,`NFHS-6: 19.0% of under-fives are wasted and **5.2% severely wasted**.`,`Undernutrition underlies about **45% of under-five deaths**.`,`Good NRC care should give mortality **under 5%** and recovery over 75%.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Complicated SAM** goes to the Nutrition Rehabilitation Centre.`,`Complicated means failed appetite test, danger sign, **oedema +++**, or a medical complication.`,`**Uncomplicated SAM**: alert, passes appetite test, no complication; treat in the community.`,`Oedema grades: + both feet, ++ legs or hands, **+++ generalised including face**.`,`**Marasmus**: severe wasting, alert and hungry. **Kwashiorkor**: oedema, apathy, flaky-paint skin.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. **Reductive adaptation**: every organ slows down to survive starvation.`,`2. Tiny reserves mean any missed feed causes **hypoglycaemia**, and low fat causes hypothermia.`,`3. The small heart cannot handle sodium or volume, so boluses cause **heart failure**.`,`4. The sodium pump fails: body sodium is high, while potassium and magnesium are low.`,`5. Weak immunity means **infection without fever** or leucocytosis.`,`6. Low transferrin leaves free iron that feeds bacteria, so **iron is withheld** early.`,`7. Sudden carbohydrate drives phosphate and potassium into cells: **refeeding syndrome**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about appetite; **anorexia is a medical complication** in SAM.`,`Take a feeding history and ask about food insecurity.`,`Ask about diarrhoea, vomiting and urine output.`,`Ask about **TB contact**, cough over 2 weeks and HIV risk.`,`Ask about measles in the last 3 months and night blindness.`,`Ask about use of anganwadi services and take-home ration.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Weigh naked, measure length or height, and calculate the **WFH z-score**.`,`Measure **MUAC** on the left arm and press both feet for 3 seconds for oedema.`,`**Appetite test**: offer RUTF for 30 minutes; failure means complicated SAM.`,`Check temperature (**under 35 C** is hypothermia) and glucose (**under 54 mg/dL**).`,`Look for corneal xerosis or ulcers; **keratomalacia can blind within a day**.`,`**Dehydration signs are unreliable** in SAM; rely on history and pulse.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Treatment never waits for tests.**`,`Check blood glucose on admission and whenever the child is cold or lethargic.`,`Transfuse only if Hb is **under 4 g/dL**, or under 6 g/dL with respiratory distress.`,`Test **every SAM child for HIV** and screen for TB.`,`Send urine culture, malaria test and blood culture where available.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Steps 1-2: treat and prevent **hypoglycaemia and hypothermia**; feed 2-hourly day and night.`,`Step 3: rehydrate with **ReSoMal 5 mL/kg every 30 minutes** for 2 hours.`,`Step 4: give extra potassium and magnesium; **no diuretics** for oedema.`,`Step 5: **antibiotics for every child**, because infection is silent.`,`Step 6: vitamin A, folic acid, zinc and copper; **no iron** in stabilisation.`,`Step 7: **F-75 at 130 mL/kg/day** (100 kcal/kg/day), by cup and spoon.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Transition to F-100 at the same volume for 2 days when appetite returns.`,`Then increase each feed by **10 mL** until some is left.`,`Step 8: F-100 or RUTF at **150-220 kcal/kg/day** for catch-up growth.`,`Target weight gain **over 10 g/kg/day**.`,`Start **iron 3 mg/kg/day** only once the child is gaining weight.`,`Steps 9-10: play and stimulation daily, then follow-up after discharge.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Shock in SAM: **15 mL/kg over 1 hour** of Ringer lactate with 5% dextrose.`,`Never a 20 mL/kg bolus, plain saline, albumin or diuretics for oedema.`,`Stop fluids if **pulse rises by 15** or breathing by 5, or eyelids become puffy.`,`Pulse up by 25 during transition suggests **refeeding heart failure**.`,`The four early killers: **hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, fluid overload, missed infection**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`NRC discharge at **15% weight gain** over admission weight, with oedema gone and good appetite.`,`After NRC, follow up **fortnightly for 2 months**, then monthly.`,`Community care: RUTF or energy-dense food, amoxicillin 5 days, **weekly review**.`,`Refer from community if weight loss for 2 weeks or no gain for 3 weeks.`,`Poor gain under 5 g/kg/day means look for **TB, HIV, UTI** or feeding errors.`]}],tables:[{heading:`SAM drugs and feeds`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`F-75`,`130 mL/kg/day oral or NG (100 with severe oedema)`,`Stabilisation, about days 1-7`,`Refeeding syndrome if advanced too fast`],[`F-100 / RUTF`,`150-220 kcal/kg/day; RUTF 2-3 sachets a day`,`Rehabilitation, weeks 2-6`,`Heart failure if transition rushed`],[`ReSoMal`,`5 mL/kg every 30 min, then 5-10 mL/kg/h`,`2 h, then 4-10 h`,`Stop if signs of overload`],[`10% glucose`,`50 mL oral or NG; 5 mL/kg IV if unconscious`,`Once, recheck in 30 min`,`Follow with F-75 feed`],[`Amoxicillin`,`15 mg/kg oral 8-hourly`,`5 days (uncomplicated)`,`Diarrhoea, rash`],[`Ampicillin + gentamicin`,`Ampicillin 50 mg/kg 6-hourly; gentamicin 7.5 mg/kg daily`,`Ampicillin 2 days then oral; gentamicin 7 days`,`Ceftriaxone if no improvement in 48 h`],[`Vitamin A`,`50,000, 1 lakh or 2 lakh IU oral by age`,`Day 1; days 2 and 14 if eye signs`,`Bulging fontanelle, vomiting`],[`Folic acid`,`5 mg day 1, then 1 mg daily`,`Throughout`,`None significant`],[`Iron`,`3 mg/kg/day elemental oral`,`Only once gaining weight`,`Harmful if started early`],[`Packed cells`,`10 mL/kg over 3 hours, furosemide 1 mg/kg`,`Once, first 24 h`,`Only if Hb under 4 (or 6 with distress)`]]},{heading:`Where to treat`,columns:[`Class`,`Criteria`,`Where treated`],rows:[[`Complicated SAM`,`SAM plus failed appetite test, danger sign, oedema +++ or complication`,`Nutrition Rehabilitation Centre`],[`Uncomplicated SAM`,`SAM, alert, passes appetite test, no complication`,`Community with RUTF and weekly review`],[`MAM`,`WFH -3 to -2 SD or MUAC 11.5-12.5 cm`,`Anganwadi supplementary nutrition`]]},{heading:`WHO ten steps`,columns:[`Step`,`Stabilisation (days 1-7)`,`Rehabilitation (weeks 2-6)`],rows:[[`1-2. Hypoglycaemia, hypothermia`,`Treat; 2-hourly feeds, warmth`,`Prevent`],[`3-4. Dehydration, electrolytes`,`ReSoMal; K and Mg; no diuretics`,`Continue K and Mg`],[`5. Infection`,`Routine antibiotics`,`Deworm; measles vaccine`],[`6. Micronutrients`,`Vitamin A, folate, zinc; no iron`,`Add iron once gaining`],[`7-8. Feeding`,`F-75 100 kcal/kg/day`,`F-100 or RUTF 150-220 kcal/kg/day`],[`9-10. Stimulation, follow-up`,`Mother present, loving care`,`Play daily; discharge at 15% gain`]]}],remember:[`Any one of three: WFH below -3 SD, MUAC under 11.5 cm, bilateral oedema.`,`Appetite test sorts the child: eats goes home, refuses is admitted.`,`F-75 stabilises; F-100 or RUTF builds.`,`Three prohibitions: no early iron, no diuretics, no IV bolus.`,`Every SAM child gets antibiotics, an HIV test and TB screening.`,`Poor weight gain is a diagnosis to investigate.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`NFHS-6 (2023-24): wasting 19.0% and severe wasting 5.2% (NFHS-5: 19.3% and 7.7%) - wasting has barely moved`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Severe acute malnutrition`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**SAM (6-59 months)** = any one of **WFH under -3 SD, MUAC under 11.5 cm, bilateral pitting oedema**; MAM = WFH -3 to -2 SD or MUAC 11.5-12.5 cm.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Complicated** (failed appetite test, danger sign, oedema +++, medical complication, under 6 months not feeding) -> **NRC**; **uncomplicated** -> community (CMAM). Oedema +, ++, +++; marasmus, kwashiorkor, marasmic-kwashiorkor.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Poor IYCF, repeated diarrhoea and infection, LBW, maternal undernutrition, food insecurity; underlying **TB, HIV, CHD, CP**. **NFHS-6: wasting 19.0%, severe 5.2%**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`**Reductive adaptation** -> small heart, low Na-K pump activity (**high cell Na, low K, Mg**), silent infection, iron paradox (low transferrin) -> treating like a normal child (bolus, iron, high protein) kills; **refeeding -> insulin -> low phosphate, K, Mg**.`],[`Key history`,`Appetite (anorexia = complication), 24-h diet, diarrhoea and urine, fever, cough, **TB contact**, measles, night blindness, HIV risk, development, social history, prior IV fluids or diuretics.`],[`Key examination`,`Weight, length/height, **WFH z-score, MUAC**, oedema grading; **appetite test** with RUTF; danger signs, shock, **temp under 35 C, glucose under 54 mg/dL**, RR, eyes (Bitot, corneal), skin, pallor, underlying disease.`],[`Investigations`,`Treatment never waits for tests; glucometer, **Hb (transfuse only under 4, or under 6 with distress)**, **HIV test and TB screen in every child**, urine culture, malaria RDT; electrolytes - do not correct low Na with Na.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Any one SAM criterion; complicated if appetite test fails or any complication; **poor weight gain under 5 g/kg/day** is itself a diagnosis to investigate.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Ten steps**: treat hypoglycaemia (50 mL 10% glucose or 10% dextrose 5 mL/kg IV), hypothermia, dehydration (**ReSoMal 5 mL/kg every 30 min x 2 h then 5-10 mL/kg/h**), electrolytes (K 3-4, Mg 0.4-0.6 mmol/kg/day), infection, micronutrients, **F-75 130 mL/kg/day (100 kcal/kg)** 2-hourly.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Antibiotics: uncomplicated **amoxicillin 15 mg/kg 8-hourly 5 days**; complicated **ampicillin 50 mg/kg 6-hourly 2 days then amoxicillin to 7 days + gentamicin 7.5 mg/kg x 7 days**; ceftriaxone if no response 48 h. Rehabilitation **F-100/RUTF 150-220 kcal/kg/day**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Cup-spoon or paladai (never bottle); NG if under 80% of feed taken; **RUTF 500 kcal per 92 g sachet**; MUAC tape; weight chart in g/kg/day; play and stimulation 15-30 min/day.`],[`Special groups`,`**Under 6 months** - support breastfeeding, admit if not feeding; **shock in SAM - RL + 5% dextrose 15 mL/kg over 1 h**, repeat once, then ReSoMal; no response - septic shock, transfuse 10 mL/kg.`],[`Complications`,`Hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, **fluid overload and heart failure**, **refeeding syndrome (days 2-5)**, silent sepsis, keratomalacia, dermatosis, death (target under 5% in NRC).`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Shock, temp under 35 C, glucose under 54 mg/dL, failed appetite test, oedema +++, corneal clouding, **pulse up 25 or RR up 5 during transition**, community weight loss 2 weeks or no gain 3 weeks.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Daily weight in NRC; **discharge at 15% gain** (or WFH -2 SD / MUAC 12.5 cm), oedema gone 2 weeks; follow-up **fortnightly 2 months then monthly**; CMAM weekly; exit at WFH -2 SD or MUAC 12.5 cm for 2 weeks.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`NRCs, **Poshan 2.0 CMAM protocol 2023**, ICDS take-home ration, IYCF, measles/rotavirus vaccine, vitamin A, ORS-zinc, WASH, adolescent and maternal nutrition.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**NFHS-6**: wasting unchanged at about 19%; management unchanged (WHO 2023 wasting guideline, MWCD/MoHFW 2023 protocol).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Three prohibitions - **no iron in week 1, no diuretics for oedema, no IV bolus**; every SAM child gets antibiotics and an HIV test; F-75 stabilises, F-100 builds.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`SAM - sort the child`,steps:[{label:`Any SAM criterion?`,detail:`WFH under -3 SD, MUAC under 11.5 cm or bilateral pitting oedema`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Complication check`,detail:`Danger sign, oedema +++, hypothermia, hypoglycaemia, pneumonia, dehydration, severe anaemia, eye or skin lesions`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Appetite test`,detail:`Eats weight-banded RUTF in 30 min with mother - passes or fails`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Fails or complicated - NRC`,detail:`Ten steps: F-75, ReSoMal, antibiotics, no iron, warmth, 2-hourly feeds day and night`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Passes, uncomplicated - CMAM`,detail:`RUTF or energy-dense food, amoxicillin 5 days, weekly review`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`NRC phases - from stabilisation to discharge`,steps:[{label:`Day 1-2: stabilise`,detail:`F-75 130 mL/kg/day 2-hourly (100 with severe oedema); glucose, warmth, antibiotics`},{label:`Day 3-7: reduce frequency`,detail:`3-hourly then 4-hourly F-75; vitamin A, folic acid, zinc, copper, K and Mg`},{label:`Transition`,detail:`Same volume F-100 for 2 days, then +10 mL per feed; stop if pulse +25 or RR +5`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Rehabilitation week 2-6`,detail:`F-100/RUTF 150-220 kcal/kg/day; iron 3 mg/kg/day once gaining; gain over 10 g/kg/day`},{label:`Discharge and follow-up`,detail:`15% gain, oedema gone 2 weeks; fortnightly 2 months then monthly`,tone:`good`}]}]},"pediatrics-fever-febrile-seizure":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Fever in IMNCI is an **axillary temperature of 37.5 C or more**.`,`**The height of the fever does not decide severity**; danger signs do.`,`Any fever in an infant **under 2 months** is possible serious bacterial infection.`,`**Prolonged fever** is fever every day for more than 7 days; refer it.`,`A **febrile seizure** occurs at 6-60 months with fever of 38 C or more, without CNS infection.`,`Febrile seizures affect **2-5% of children**, peaking at 12-18 months.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Very severe febrile disease**: any general danger sign or stiff neck; refer.`,`**Malaria**: fever with positive RDT or smear.`,`**Fever, malaria unlikely**: negative test or another clear cause.`,`**Simple febrile seizure**: generalised, under 15 minutes, once in 24 hours.`,`**Complex**: focal, 15 minutes or more, or recurs within 24 hours.`,`**Febrile status**: 30 minutes or more.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Microbes trigger macrophages to release **IL-1, IL-6 and TNF**.`,`2. These cytokines induce COX-2 and **prostaglandin E2** near the hypothalamus.`,`3. PGE2 **raises the set point**, so the child shivers and vasoconstricts.`,`4. Paracetamol and ibuprofen block COX and lower the set point.`,`5. The immature brain at 6-60 months is excitable, and IL-1 lowers seizure threshold.`,`6. Simple febrile seizures cause **no brain injury**; febrile status can.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask the duration; 7 days or more suggests enteric fever, TB, UTI, malaria or Kawasaki.`,`Ask the general danger signs first.`,`Ask focal symptoms: cough, ear, diarrhoea, urine, rash, limp.`,`Ask malaria and dengue risk; **every fever in a malaria area needs a test**.`,`For a fit, get an eyewitness account and the **duration by watch**.`,`Rigors and febrile delirium are common mimics of a seizure.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Check ABC, capillary refill, AVPU and glucose if drowsy.`,`**Look at the child, not the thermometer.**`,`Examine undressed; **ear, urine and bone** are the commonest missed foci.`,`A **non-blanching rash** is meningococcaemia until proved otherwise.`,`Look for an **eschar** in axilla, groin and behind ears (scrub typhus).`,`In fever of 5 days or more, look for **Kawasaki features**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Malaria RDT or smear** the same day in every fever in an endemic area.`,`Well child of 3-36 months with no focus: **urine dipstick, microscopy and culture**.`,`Blood culture for suspected enteric fever; **Widal is unreliable** in the first week.`,`Dengue: NS1 in the first 5 days, IgM after day 5, daily CBC and haematocrit.`,`Simple febrile seizure: **no bloods, EEG or imaging**; glucose only if prolonged.`,`Lumbar puncture if meningeal signs, or consider at 6-12 months if unimmunised.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Treat the uncomfortable child, not the number.**`,`**Paracetamol 15 mg/kg per dose** every 6 hours, maximum 4 doses a day.`,`Write the dose in mL for the family's bottle; drops are 100 mg/mL.`,`Give extra fluids and continue breastfeeding.`,`Tepid sponging only after the drug; **never ice or alcohol**.`,`**Antibiotics treat a focus, never a temperature.**`,`Febrile seizure: **counselling is the treatment**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Fit over 5 minutes: **buccal midazolam 0.3 mg/kg** or intranasal 0.2 mg/kg, max 10 mg.`,`With IV access: lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg; repeat once after 5-10 minutes.`,`Established status: **levetiracetam 40-60 mg/kg IV**, max 3 g.`,`**No continuous prophylaxis** with phenobarbitone or valproate.`,`Intermittent clobazam during fever only for frequent or prolonged recurrences.`,`Very severe febrile disease: first dose ampicillin plus gentamicin and refer.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Petechial or purpuric rash: give **ceftriaxone 100 mg/kg** before transfer.`,`Dengue warning signs: abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, bleeding, rising haematocrit.`,`Fit over 5 minutes, focal, or child not awake within 1 hour.`,`Fever 5 days with red eyes, cracked lips and swollen hands suggests **Kawasaki**.`,`Recurrent prolonged febrile fits from infancy suggest **Dravet syndrome**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review any persisting fever in **2 days**; dengue daily on days 3-7.`,`Refer complex seizures, febrile status, or a first fit under 6 months or over 5 years.`,`Tell parents: **recurrence 1 in 3**, epilepsy 1-2%, no brain damage.`,`First-aid: time the fit, lay on side, **nothing in the mouth**.`,`Vaccinations continue on schedule after a febrile seizure.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Fever and seizure drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`15 mg/kg/dose oral 6-hourly, max 60 mg/kg/day`,`While distressed`,`Liver toxicity in overdose; check bottle strength`],[`Ibuprofen`,`5-10 mg/kg/dose oral 6-8 hourly`,`Over 3-6 months only`,`Avoid in dengue, varicella, dehydration`],[`Midazolam`,`Buccal 0.3 or intranasal 0.2 mg/kg, max 10 mg`,`At 5 minutes; repeat once`,`Respiratory depression`],[`Lorazepam`,`0.1 mg/kg IV, max 4 mg`,`Repeat once after 5-10 min`,`Apnoea; bag-mask ready`],[`Diazepam (rectal)`,`0.5 mg/kg, max 10 mg`,`Repeat once`,`Apnoea, sedation`],[`Levetiracetam`,`40-60 mg/kg IV, max 3 g, over 5-15 min`,`Established status`,`Sedation, irritability`],[`Clobazam`,`0.75-1 mg/kg/day in 2 doses, max 20 mg`,`2-3 days from fever onset`,`Sedation can mask meningitis`],[`Ampicillin + gentamicin`,`50 mg/kg IM + 7.5 mg/kg IM`,`Single pre-referral dose`,`Separate sites`],[`Azithromycin (enteric)`,`20 mg/kg once daily, max 1 g`,`7 days`,`GI upset, QT`],[`Doxycycline (scrub typhus)`,`2.2 mg/kg twice daily, max 100 mg`,`7 days, any age`,`Short course does not stain teeth`]]},{heading:`Febrile seizure types`,columns:[`Type`,`Definition`,`Epilepsy risk`,`Work-up`],rows:[[`Simple`,`Generalised, under 15 min, once in 24 h`,`1-2%`,`None routinely`],[`Complex`,`Focal, 15 min or more, or recurs in 24 h`,`5-10%`,`Refer; LP, EEG, MRI as decided`],[`Febrile status`,`30 min or more`,`Highest`,`Treat as status; admit`]]},{heading:`IMNCI fever classification`,columns:[`Classification`,`Signs`,`Action`],rows:[[`Very severe febrile disease`,`Danger sign or stiff neck`,`First dose antibiotic, artesunate if risk, refer`],[`Malaria`,`Positive RDT or smear`,`Species-specific treatment; review 2 days`],[`Fever, malaria unlikely`,`Negative test or other cause`,`Paracetamol; treat cause; review 2 days`],[`Measles`,`Rash with cough, coryza or red eyes`,`Vitamin A days 1 and 2`]]}],remember:[`The temperature does not decide severity; danger signs do.`,`Fever without focus at 3-36 months: send the urine.`,`Paracetamol 15 mg/kg, max 4 doses; write it in mL.`,`No ibuprofen in dengue, varicella or dehydration; no aspirin except Kawasaki.`,`Simple febrile seizure: no EEG, no imaging, no bloods.`,`Recurrence 1 in 3, epilepsy 1-2%, brain damage nil.`,`Treat a fit at 5 minutes with buccal or nasal midazolam.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`AHA 2024 Kawasaki statement (Jone et al.) replaces McCrindle 2017 as the reference - IVIG 2 g/kg within 10 days plus aspirin unchanged`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Fever in the under-five and febrile seizure`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Fever = **axillary 37.5 C or more** (rectal 38). **Febrile seizure** = seizure at **6-60 months** with temp **38 C or more**, no CNS infection, metabolic cause or previous afebrile seizure; 2-5% of children.`],[`Relevant classification`,`IMNCI fever: **very severe febrile disease** (danger sign or stiff neck), **malaria** (test positive), **fever - malaria unlikely**, measles rows. Febrile seizure: **simple** (generalised, under 15 min, once in 24 h), **complex** (focal, 15 min or more, recurs in 24 h), **febrile status** (30 min or more).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Viral fevers (HHV-6 roseola, influenza), UTI (commonest occult bacterial), malaria, dengue, enteric, scrub typhus, pneumonia, otitis, meningitis, Kawasaki; febrile seizure risk - family history, GEFS+, **Dravet (SCN1A)**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Pyrogens -> **IL-1, IL-6, TNF** -> OVLT -> **COX-2 -> PGE2 -> EP3 raises set point**; immature brain + IL-1 beta + hyperventilation alkalosis -> febrile seizure; antipyretics lower set point but **do not prevent seizures**.`],[`Key history`,`Duration (**7 days or more** - enteric, TB, UTI, Kawasaki), danger signs, focal symptoms, malaria/dengue risk, immunisation, drugs given; for a fit - **eyewitness account, timed duration, focal features, recovery time**, family history, development.`],[`Key examination`,`ABC, AVPU, glucose if drowsy; colour, activity, cry; RR; hydration; **undressed exam** (ear, urine, bone); meningeal signs; **non-blanching rash**; eschar; Kawasaki features after 5 days; dengue signs; full neuro exam after a fit.`],[`Investigations`,`**Malaria RDT in risk areas**; **urine dipstick, microscopy and culture** in 3-36 months with no focus; blood culture for enteric; NS1 days 1-5 / IgM after; **no tests, EEG or imaging after a simple febrile seizure**; LP if meningeal signs (consider 6-12 months if unimmunised).`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Simple febrile seizure needs all of: age 6-60 months, generalised, under 15 min, single in 24 h, back to baseline, no CNS infection; **temperature height does not decide IMNCI class**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Paracetamol 15 mg/kg/dose 6-hourly, max 4 doses (60 mg/kg/day)** only if distressed or 38.5 C or more; extra fluids; tepid sponge only after drug; treat the focus; **counselling** (recurrence 1 in 3, epilepsy 1-2%, no brain damage).`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Fit over 5 min: **buccal midazolam 0.3 mg/kg or IN 0.2 mg/kg (max 10 mg)**, or **IV lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg**; repeat once; then **levetiracetam 40-60 mg/kg IV**, fosphenytoin 20 mg PE/kg or valproate 40 mg/kg; refractory - midazolam infusion in PICU.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Write paracetamol dose **in mL for the family's bottle** (drops 100 mg/mL vs syrup 125 mg/5 mL); dropper or syringe; home rescue midazolam pack; first aid - side position, time it, nothing in mouth.`],[`Special groups`,`**Under 2 months - any fever = PSBI**; dengue - no ibuprofen, no IM injections; varicella - no ibuprofen or aspirin; **Kawasaki - IVIG 2 g/kg within 10 days + aspirin** (AHA 2024); intermittent clobazam 0.75-1 mg/kg/day only for selected cases.`],[`Complications`,`Febrile status -> hippocampal injury, mesial temporal sclerosis; missed meningitis; paracetamol hepatotoxicity with repeated excess; ibuprofen AKI in dehydration; coronary aneurysm in missed Kawasaki.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Danger sign or stiff neck; any fever under 2 months; **non-blanching rash - ceftriaxone 100 mg/kg before transfer**; fit over 5 min, focal, recurrent or not awake in 1 h; dengue warning signs; fever 7 days or more; Kawasaki.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review persistent fever in **2 days**, dengue daily on days 3-7 with Hct and platelets; after febrile seizure check development and rescue plan; Kawasaki echo at diagnosis, 1-2 and 4-6 weeks.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Hib, PCV, MR, **TCV**, influenza vaccines; vector control and nets (NCVBDC); **continue vaccines after febrile seizure** (not a contraindication).`],[`Latest guideline change`,`Kawasaki reference updated to **AHA 2024 scientific statement**; no change to fever or febrile seizure management (NICE NG143, AAP, IAP 2022 status epilepticus consensus).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Treat the child, not the thermometer; **no continuous anticonvulsant prophylaxis**; levetiracetam is now preferred second line; recurrent prolonged focal febrile fits before 1 year - think Dravet, avoid carbamazepine.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Febrile child - triage and act`,steps:[{label:`Age under 2 months?`,detail:`Any fever = possible serious bacterial infection - first dose antibiotic, refer`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Danger sign, stiff neck, purpura?`,detail:`Very severe febrile disease - ampicillin + gentamicin or ceftriaxone 100 mg/kg, artesunate in malaria area, refer`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Malaria risk - RDT now`,detail:`Positive: vivax chloroquine + primaquine 14 days; falciparum ACT + single-dose primaquine`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Look for the focus`,detail:`Ear, chest, urine (3-36 months no focus - urine culture), skin, eschar, joints`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Treat cause, paracetamol if distressed`,detail:`15 mg/kg/dose 6-hourly, max 4 doses; review in 2 days; refer if 7 days or more`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Convulsing child - time-based escalation`,steps:[{label:`0-5 min: stabilise`,detail:`Airway, side position, oxygen, glucose - under 54 mg/dL give 10% dextrose 2-5 mL/kg`},{label:`5 min: first benzodiazepine`,detail:`Buccal midazolam 0.3 mg/kg or IN 0.2 mg/kg (max 10 mg), or IV lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg`,tone:`warn`},{label:`10 min: second dose`,detail:`Repeat benzodiazepine once only - a third causes apnoea`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Established status`,detail:`Levetiracetam 40-60 mg/kg IV (max 3 g), or fosphenytoin 20 mg PE/kg, or valproate 40 mg/kg`},{label:`Refractory - PICU`,detail:`Midazolam infusion with airway support; refer early from primary care`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Simple versus complex febrile seizure`,columns:[`Feature`,`Simple`,`Complex`],rows:[[`Type`,`Generalised`,`Focal onset`],[`Duration`,`Under 15 minutes`,`15 minutes or more`],[`Recurrence in 24 h`,`No`,`Yes`],[`Epilepsy risk`,`1-2%`,`5-10%`],[`Work-up`,`None; glucose only if prolonged`,`Refer; LP, EEG, MRI as decided`]]}]},"pediatrics-anaemia-deworming":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Anaemia at 6-59 months is **haemoglobin under 11 g/dL**.`,`Cut-off is under 11.5 at 5-11 years and under 12 at 12-14 years.`,`NFHS-5: **67.1% of children** aged 6-59 months are anaemic.`,`**Iron deficiency** causes half to two thirds of childhood anaemia in India.`,`The peak is at **6-24 months**, when birth stores run out.`,`India still uses 11 g/dL at 6-59 months, though WHO 2024 lowered 6-23 months to 10.5.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Under-fives: **mild 10-10.9, moderate 7-9.9, severe under 7 g/dL**.`,`At 5-14 years, **severe is under 8 g/dL**.`,`IMNCI uses palmar pallor: **severe pallor** means refer; some pallor means treat.`,`**Microcytic**: iron deficiency, thalassaemia trait, inflammation, lead.`,`**Macrocytic**: B12 or folate deficiency. **Normocytic**: haemolysis, marrow failure, blood loss.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Birth iron stores last 4-6 months in term babies, only 2-3 months in preterms.`,`2. Cereal-based, late complementary feeding gives too little absorbable iron.`,`3. **Phytates, tea and milk** block iron absorption; vitamin C helps it.`,`4. **Hepcidin** rises for 24 hours after each dose, so once-daily dosing works best.`,`5. Stores empty first, then erythropoiesis fails, then **microcytic hypochromic anaemia**.`,`6. Iron is also needed for myelination, so deficiency delays development.`,`7. **Hookworm** causes steady gut blood loss.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask how much **cow's or packet milk**; over 500 mL a day displaces iron foods.`,`Ask about tea with meals and a vegetarian household.`,`Ask about prematurity, low birth weight and maternal anaemia.`,`Ask about **pica**, irritability and poor school performance.`,`Ask about worms, walking barefoot and the last deworming dose.`,`Ask about previous iron courses; most failures are **short or missed courses**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Look for pallor of palms, conjunctiva, tongue and nail beds.`,`Look for koilonychia, angular stomatitis and smooth tongue.`,`**Splenomegaly or jaundice** points away from simple iron deficiency.`,`Petechiae, nodes or bone tenderness suggest leukaemia or marrow failure.`,`Check for **heart failure**: gallop, tender liver, oedema.`,`Tremors and lost milestones in a breastfed infant suggest **infantile tremor syndrome**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Digital haemoglobinometer** at the point of care under Anemia Mukt Bharat.`,`CBC shows **low MCV and MCH with high RDW** in iron deficiency.`,`**Mentzer index** over 13 suggests iron deficiency; under 13 suggests thalassaemia trait.`,`Confirm trait by **HPLC (HbA2 over 3.5%)**.`,`**Ferritin under 12** (under 30 with infection) confirms depleted stores.`,`A therapeutic trial is a test: **Hb rises 1 g/dL in 4 weeks**.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Elemental iron 3 mg/kg once daily** between meals with vitamin C.`,`Never give iron with milk, tea or antacids.`,`Continue **2-3 months after Hb normalises** to refill stores.`,`IFA syrup has **20 mg iron per mL**; a 10 kg child gets 1.5 mL daily.`,`Deworm with **albendazole 200 mg at 12-23 months, 400 mg from 2 years**.`,`Advise iron-rich diet, cooking in iron vessels and fortified foods.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`No rise after 4 weeks: check adherence, blood loss, worms, trait or malabsorption.`,`Parenteral **iron sucrose** only for intolerance, malabsorption or non-adherence.`,`B12 deficiency: **IM B12** 250-1000 mcg daily for 1 week, then weekly, then monthly.`,`**Never give folic acid alone** if B12 status is unknown.`,`Transfuse only if **Hb under 4**, or under 6 with heart failure: 10 mL/kg over 3-4 hours.`,`In heart failure give **5 mL/kg with furosemide 1 mg/kg**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Recheck haemoglobin at **4 weeks** and at the end of treatment.`,`IMNCI anaemia follow-up is at **14 days**.`,`Refer severe anaemia: under 7 g/dL in under-fives, under 8 in older children.`,`Refer non-responders and anaemia with splenomegaly, jaundice or bleeding.`,`Iron overdose over 20 mg/kg elemental needs referral and serum iron.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**Anemia Mukt Bharat 6x6x6**: six groups, six interventions, six institutional mechanisms.`,`Children 6-59 months: **IFA syrup 1 mL twice weekly**.`,`**National Deworming Day**: 10 February and 10 August.`,`Delayed cord clamping is the first iron intervention.`,`Target: a **3 percentage point fall** in anaemia every year.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Anaemia drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Oral iron (elemental)`,`3 mg/kg once daily oral (up to 6 in severe)`,`About 3 months; 2-3 months after Hb normal`,`Black stools, nausea, tooth staining; fatal in overdose`],[`IFA syrup (prophylaxis)`,`1 mL (20 mg iron) twice weekly`,`6-59 months`,`Stop while on treatment dose`],[`Iron sucrose`,`3-7 mg/kg IV per dose, max 200 mg`,`Until Ganzoni deficit replaced`,`Hypotension, anaphylactoid reaction`],[`Albendazole`,`200 mg at 12-23 months; 400 mg from 2 years`,`Single dose every 6 months`,`Not under 1 year; chew the tablet`],[`Mebendazole`,`100 mg twice daily or 500 mg once`,`3 days or single dose`,`Over 1 year only`],[`Vitamin B12`,`250-1000 mcg IM`,`Daily 1 week, weekly 4 weeks, then monthly`,`Hypokalaemia in first 48 hours`],[`Folic acid`,`0.5-1 mg/day infants; 5 mg/day over 1 year`,`4 months`,`Never alone if B12 unknown`],[`Packed red cells`,`10 mL/kg over 3-4 h (5 mL/kg if failing)`,`Once`,`Only if Hb under 4, or under 6 with distress`]]},{heading:`Severity by haemoglobin (g/dL)`,columns:[`Group`,`Mild`,`Moderate`,`Severe`],rows:[[`6-59 months`,`10-10.9`,`7-9.9`,`Under 7`],[`5-11 years`,`11-11.4`,`8-10.9`,`Under 8`],[`12-14 years`,`11-11.9`,`8-10.9`,`Under 8`],[`Pregnant women`,`10-10.9`,`7-9.9`,`Under 7`]]},{heading:`Iron deficiency or thalassaemia trait?`,columns:[`Feature`,`Iron deficiency`,`Thalassaemia trait`],rows:[[`RDW`,`High`,`Normal`],[`Mentzer index`,`Over 13`,`Under 13`],[`Ferritin`,`Low`,`Normal`],[`HbA2 on HPLC`,`Normal`,`Over 3.5%`],[`Response to iron`,`Hb up 1 g/dL in 4 weeks`,`None`]]},{heading:`Anemia Mukt Bharat prophylaxis`,columns:[`Group`,`Prophylactic IFA`,`Deworming`],rows:[[`6-59 months`,`1 mL syrup twice weekly`,`Albendazole 200 or 400 mg twice a year`],[`5-9 years`,`Pink tablet (45 mg iron) weekly`,`Albendazole 400 mg twice a year`],[`10-19 years`,`Blue tablet (60 mg iron) weekly`,`Albendazole 400 mg twice a year`],[`Pregnant women`,`60 mg iron daily for 180 days`,`Albendazole 400 mg once, 2nd trimester`]]}],remember:[`Under 11 g/dL at 6-59 months; severe under 7.`,`Iron 3 mg/kg once daily between meals, with vitamin C.`,`Expect 1 g/dL rise a month; continue 2-3 months after normal.`,`Mentzer under 13 means thalassaemia trait until HPLC says otherwise.`,`Never folic acid alone in possible B12 deficiency.`,`Deworming days: 10 February and 10 August.`,`Anemia Mukt Bharat is 6x6x6.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - Childhood anaemia and deworming`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Hb **under 11 g/dL at 6-59 months**, under 11.5 at 5-11 years, under 12 at 12-14 years; from 15 years under 12 (girls), under 13 (boys). WHO 2024 lowered 6-23 months to 10.5, India keeps 11.`],[`Relevant classification`,`6-59 months: **mild 10-10.9, moderate 7-9.9, severe under 7**; 5-14 years severe **under 8**. IMNCI by palmar pallor: severe (refer), some (iron, deworm, review 14 days). Morphology: microcytic, macrocytic, normocytic.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**Iron deficiency (half to two thirds)**, hookworm, B12/folate, thalassaemia trait, sickle cell, malaria, inflammation; peak **6-24 months**; cow's milk over 500 mL, tea with meals, prematurity, early cord clamping. NFHS-5: **67.1%** of 6-59 months.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Low non-haem iron absorption (phytates, tannins, calcium) -> depleted ferritin -> iron-deficient erythropoiesis -> **microcytic hypochromic, high RDW**; **hepcidin rises 24 h after a dose** -> once-daily or alternate-day dosing.`],[`Key history`,`Diet and milk volume, tea, complementary feeding; perinatal history; pica, irritability, school performance; worms, barefoot walking, last deworming; blood loss; jaundice, transfusions, family history; previous iron courses and adherence.`],[`Key examination`,`Palmar, conjunctival, nail-bed pallor; growth; koilonychia, angular stomatitis; jaundice, **splenomegaly (not simple iron deficiency)**; petechiae, nodes (leukaemia); heart failure signs; knuckle pigmentation and tremor (B12).`],[`Investigations`,`**Digital haemoglobinometer**; CBC with indices and smear; **Mentzer index (over 13 iron, under 13 trait)**; ferritin under 12 (under 30 with inflammation); **therapeutic trial - Hb up 1 g/dL in 4 weeks**; stool ova; malaria RDT; HPLC if trait suspected.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Iron deficiency = low MCV, high RDW, low ferritin or response to iron; thalassaemia trait = high RBC, normal RDW, **HbA2 over 3.5%**; megaloblastic = macrocytes, hypersegmented neutrophils.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Elemental iron 3 mg/kg once daily** between meals with vitamin C for 3 months (**2-3 months after Hb normal**); IFA syrup 20 mg/mL - 10 kg child 1.5 mL daily; **albendazole 200 mg (1-2 y), 400 mg (2 y+)**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Up to 6 mg/kg in severe; parenteral **iron sucrose 3-7 mg/kg (max 200 mg)** by Ganzoni deficit for intolerance or malabsorption; **B12 250-1000 mcg IM** daily 1 week, weekly 4 weeks, monthly; folic acid never alone if B12 unknown.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`AMB auto-dispenser bottle; dropper to back of tongue then brush (staining); **packed cells 10 mL/kg over 3-4 h only if Hb under 4, or under 6 with distress** (5 mL/kg + furosemide in heart failure).`],[`Special groups`,`LBW/preterm - **iron 2 mg/kg/day (3 mg/kg under 1500 g) from 2-6 weeks to 1 year**; SAM - no iron until gaining weight; adolescents 60 mg (mild) or 120 mg (moderate) daily 2 months; thalassaemia trait - no iron without ferritin.`],[`Complications`,`Developmental and cognitive delay, poor school performance, anaemic heart failure, pica; **iron poisoning (over 60 mg/kg fatal risk)** - desferrioxamine.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Hb under 7 (6-59 months) or under 8 (5-14 years); heart failure; anaemia with jaundice, splenomegaly, bleeding, nodes, bicytopenia; **no rise after 4 weeks supervised iron**; infantile tremor syndrome; iron ingestion.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Hb at **4 weeks** (expect 1 g/dL rise) and end of treatment, then 6-monthly with deworming; IMNCI review at 14 days; return to prophylaxis after cure.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Anemia Mukt Bharat 6x6x6**: IFA syrup **1 mL twice weekly (6-59 months)**, pink tablet weekly (5-9 y), **blue WIFS tablet weekly (10-19 y)**; **NDD 10 February and 10 August**; fortified rice, DFS salt; delayed cord clamping; target fall 3 points/year.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - national 11 g/dL cut-off retained despite WHO 2024 10.5 g/dL at 6-23 months; NFHS-6 fact sheet dropped the child anaemia indicator.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Prescribe **elemental** iron; reticulocytes peak day 5-10; cow's milk under 1 year causes iron deficiency; never folic acid alone in possible B12 deficiency; 20 adult iron tablets can kill a toddler.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Pale child - test and treat pathway`,steps:[{label:`Hb by digital meter`,detail:`Classify mild, moderate, severe by age band`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Severe or red flags?`,detail:`Hb under 7 (under 5 y) or under 8 (older), heart failure, jaundice, spleen, bleeding - refer`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Microcytic, no red flags`,detail:`Iron 3 mg/kg/day elemental + albendazole; malaria test in endemic area`},{label:`Recheck at 4 weeks`,detail:`Rise of 1 g/dL confirms iron deficiency; continue 2-3 months after normal`,tone:`decision`},{label:`No rise`,detail:`Check adherence, blood loss, worms; ferritin, HPLC (trait), coeliac - not a higher dose`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Back to AMB prophylaxis`,detail:`IFA 1 mL twice weekly, deworming every 6 months`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Anemia Mukt Bharat - prophylaxis by age`,columns:[`Group`,`IFA`,`Deworming`],rows:[[`6-59 months`,`Syrup 1 mL (20 mg Fe + 100 mcg FA) twice weekly`,`Albendazole 200 mg (12-23 months), 400 mg (2-5 y) 6-monthly`],[`5-9 years`,`Pink tablet 45 mg Fe + 400 mcg FA weekly`,`Albendazole 400 mg 6-monthly`],[`10-19 years`,`Blue tablet 60 mg Fe + 500 mcg FA weekly (WIFS)`,`Albendazole 400 mg 6-monthly`],[`Pregnant and lactating`,`60 mg Fe + 500 mcg FA daily 180 days each`,`Albendazole 400 mg once in 2nd trimester`]]}]},"pediatrics-congenital-heart-disease":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`CHD is a structural heart defect present at birth, in **8-10 per 1000 live births**.`,`About one fifth are **critical** and need intervention in the first year.`,`**VSD is the commonest** lesion (30-35%).`,`**Fallot** is the commonest cyanotic lesion after infancy; **TGA** in the first week.`,`Innocent murmurs occur in **50-70% of children**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Acyanotic shunts** (VSD, ASD, PDA, AVSD): plethoric lungs and heart failure.`,`**Acyanotic obstructive** (PS, AS, coarctation): pressure load, normal lungs.`,`**Cyanotic, low pulmonary flow**: Fallot, pulmonary atresia, tricuspid atresia.`,`**Cyanotic, high pulmonary flow**: TGA, TAPVC, truncus.`,`Infant heart failure severity uses the **Ross classification** (I to IV).`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. At birth pulmonary resistance falls and the **duct closes** within 24-72 hours.`,`2. Duct-dependent lesions therefore present with cyanosis or shock in the first 2 weeks.`,`3. A large VSD presents at **6-8 weeks**, as pulmonary resistance falls.`,`4. Shunts flood the lungs; breathing work burns calories and growth fails.`,`5. Untreated high flow causes **Eisenmenger syndrome**; the shunt reverses and cannot be closed.`,`6. In Fallot, a **tet spell** is infundibular spasm or low SVR increasing right-to-left shunt.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about feeds taking over 20-30 minutes with **forehead sweating**.`,`Fast breathing, recurrent chest infections and poor weight gain suggest a large shunt.`,`Ask about blue spells with hyperpnoea; **tet spells peak at 2-6 months**.`,`**Squatting** after play is classic for Fallot.`,`Ask about exertional syncope or chest pain, and sudden death in the family.`,`Ask about maternal diabetes, rubella and drugs in pregnancy.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Pulse oximetry in right hand and foot: **under 90%** or difference over 3% is abnormal.`,`Feel femorals; an **arm-leg gradient over 20 mmHg** suggests coarctation.`,`Infant heart failure: RR over 60, HR over 160, **liver over 3 cm**.`,`S2: **wide fixed split** in ASD, single in Fallot and TGA, loud P2 in pulmonary hypertension.`,`Any thrill is pathological.`,`Innocent murmur: grade 1-2, systolic, soft, normal S2, thriving child.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Echocardiography** for every pathological murmur, cyanosis or heart failure.`,`An innocent murmur in a thriving child **needs no echo**.`,`Newborn oximetry screen: pass is **95% or more** with difference 3% or less.`,`X-ray: **boot-shaped heart** (Fallot), egg on a string (TGA), rib notching (coarctation).`,`ECG: left or superior axis in an ASD means **primum ASD or AVSD**.`,`Check CBC and MCV in every cyanotic child.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Drugs buy time; **no diuretic closes a defect**.`,`**Furosemide 1-2 mg/kg per dose**, 1-3 times daily, for heart failure.`,`Add **spironolactone 1-3 mg/kg/day** and enalapril titrated to 0.5 mg/kg/day.`,`Feed **140-170 kcal/kg/day**; concentrate feeds, do not fluid-restrict.`,`Fallot: **propranolol 0.5-1 mg/kg 6-8 hourly** until repair.`,`Give iron to keep MCV normal; **never venesect** a cyanotic child.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Tet spell: **knee-chest position**, 100% oxygen and morphine 0.1-0.2 mg/kg.`,`Then saline 10-20 mL/kg, IV propranolol 0.1 mg/kg and phenylephrine.`,`**Never inotropes or digoxin** in a spell.`,`A spell is an indication for **early surgery**.`,`Shocked or cyanotic neonate: start **PGE1** and watch for apnoea.`,`Large VSD or PDA closed **by 6 months**; Fallot repaired at 3-6 months.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Eisenmenger, endocarditis, brain abscess and stroke from polycythaemia.`,`IE prophylaxis only for the **highest-risk group**: amoxicillin 50 mg/kg once.`,`No prophylaxis for small VSD, secundum ASD or rheumatic valves.`,`Fever over 48 hours in a high-risk child: **blood cultures before antibiotics**.`,`Grey, shocked neonate with weak femorals: duct-dependent; start PGE1.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Refer the same day: cyanosis, failed oximetry, tet spell, weak femorals, infant heart failure.`,`Refer any pathological or uncertain murmur for echo.`,`Small muscular VSDs often close by 2 years; primum defects never close.`,`Secundum ASD device closure at **3-5 years** if significant.`,`Free surgery through **RBSK** or PM-JAY; cost is the real barrier.`]}],tables:[{heading:`CHD drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Furosemide`,`1-2 mg/kg/dose oral 1-3 times daily; IV 0.5-1 mg/kg`,`Until repair`,`Low K, Na; check electrolytes`],[`Spironolactone`,`1-3 mg/kg/day oral in 1-2 doses`,`With furosemide`,`Hyperkalaemia; no K supplements`],[`Enalapril`,`0.1 mg/kg/day, titrate to 0.5 mg/kg/day`,`Until repair`,`Hypotension; avoid in outflow obstruction`],[`Digoxin`,`8-10 mcg/kg/day in 2 doses (infant)`,`Second line`,`Toxicity worse with hypokalaemia`],[`Propranolol`,`0.5-1 mg/kg oral 6-8 hourly; spell 0.1 mg/kg IV`,`Until repair`,`Hypoglycaemia, bradycardia`],[`Morphine (spell)`,`0.1-0.2 mg/kg SC or IM`,`Single dose`,`Respiratory depression`],[`Alprostadil (PGE1)`,`0.01-0.05 mcg/kg/min IV`,`Until surgery`,`Apnoea, fever, hypotension`],[`Ibuprofen (preterm PDA)`,`10 mg/kg then 5 mg/kg at 24 and 48 h`,`3 doses`,`Avoid in renal failure, bleeding, NEC`],[`Amoxicillin (IE prophylaxis)`,`50 mg/kg oral, max 2 g`,`Single dose 30-60 min before`,`Highest-risk group only`]]},{heading:`Innocent or pathological murmur?`,columns:[`Feature`,`Innocent`,`Pathological`],rows:[[`Timing`,`Systolic (or venous hum)`,`Diastolic, pansystolic or continuous`],[`Grade`,`1-2, no thrill`,`3 or more, or any thrill`],[`S2 and clicks`,`Normal, no click`,`Single, loud or fixed split`],[`Posture`,`Changes with position`,`Little change`],[`Child`,`Thriving, normal pulses`,`Cyanosis, poor growth, weak femorals`],[`Action`,`Reassure; no echo`,`Echo and cardiology referral`]]},{heading:`Common lesions at a glance`,columns:[`Lesion`,`Key sign`,`X-ray / ECG`,`Management`],rows:[[`Small VSD`,`Loud pansystolic murmur, thrill`,`Normal`,`Observe; often closes`],[`Large VSD`,`Softer murmur, loud P2, failure`,`Cardiomegaly, plethora`,`Drugs; close by 6 months`],[`Secundum ASD`,`Wide fixed split S2`,`RAD, rSR in V1`,`Device closure at 3-5 years`],[`PDA`,`Continuous machinery murmur`,`LVH, plethora`,`Device or ligation`],[`Fallot`,`Ejection murmur, single S2`,`Boot-shaped heart`,`Propranolol; repair 3-6 months`],[`Coarctation`,`Weak delayed femorals`,`Rib notching after 8 years`,`PGE1 in neonate; repair`]]}],remember:[`The louder the VSD, the smaller the hole.`,`Large VSD presents at 6-8 weeks, not at birth.`,`Fixed split S2 means ASD.`,`Tet spell: knee-chest, oxygen, morphine, fluid, propranolol; never inotropes.`,`Cyanotic child with normal Hb is iron deficient; never venesect.`,`IE prophylaxis: one dose amoxicillin, highest-risk group only.`,`Close large VSD or PDA by 6 months, before Eisenmenger.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`IAP ACVIP 2025: nirsevimab recommended for high-risk infants (including significant CHD) rather than universally; annual influenza vaccine now trivalent`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Congenital heart disease`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Structural heart or great-vessel abnormality present at birth; **8-10 per 1000 live births**, about one fifth critical (intervention in first year).`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Acyanotic**: L-to-R shunts (VSD, ASD, PDA, AVSD) and obstructive (PS, AS, coarctation). **Cyanotic**: low pulmonary flow (**Fallot**, pulmonary/tricuspid atresia) vs high flow (**TGA**, TAPVC, truncus). Infant heart failure - **Ross class I-IV**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Down (AVSD), 22q11.2 (Fallot, truncus), Turner (coarctation), Noonan, Williams, Holt-Oram, **congenital rubella (PDA)**, maternal diabetes (TGA), valproate, lithium; sibling recurrence 2-3%. **VSD commonest**, Fallot commonest cyanotic after infancy.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Falling PVR by 6-8 weeks -> **large VSD/PDA presents at 6-8 weeks** -> pulmonary overload -> failure to thrive; untreated -> **Eisenmenger** (PVR exceeds SVR, shunt reverses) - then never close. Duct closes 24-72 h -> duct-dependent lesions collapse.`],[`Key history`,`Feeding time over 20-30 min, **forehead sweating**, fast breathing, chest infections, poor weight; cyanosis and **tet spells** (2-6 months); squatting; syncope on exertion; antenatal and family history; newborn oximetry screen.`],[`Key examination`,`**SpO2 right hand and foot**; four-limb pulses and BP (**arm-leg gradient over 20 mmHg = coarctation**); growth; infant failure - **liver over 3 cm**, RR over 60; precordium, thrill, **S2** (fixed split ASD, single Fallot/TGA); murmur timing; dysmorphism.`],[`Investigations`,`**Echocardiography** for every pathological murmur, cyanosis, failed screen; **CCHD screen pass 95% or more, difference 3% or less, fail under 90%**; X-ray signs (boot, egg on string, snowman, rib notching); ECG axis; CBC with MCV in cyanosis.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**Innocent murmur**: grade 1-2, systolic, localised, musical, varies with posture, normal S2, thriving child (Still's, venous hum). **Pathological**: diastolic, pansystolic, grade 3+ or thrill, abnormal S2, click, symptoms.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Heart failure: **furosemide 1-2 mg/kg/dose (1-3 mg/kg/day)**, **spironolactone 1-3 mg/kg/day**, **enalapril 0.1 to 0.5 mg/kg/day**; **140-170 kcal/kg/day**, concentrate feeds to 0.8-1 kcal/mL (do not fluid-restrict); iron 3-6 mg/kg/day in cyanosis.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Digoxin 8-10 mcg/kg/day (second line); **propranolol 0.5-1 mg/kg 6-8 hourly** in Fallot; **PGE1 0.01-0.05 mcg/kg/min** for duct-dependent lesions (apnoea risk); repair - **large VSD/PDA by 6 months, AVSD 3-6 months, Fallot 3-6 months, ASD device 3-5 years**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Pulse oximeter, four-limb BP; device closure (ASD, PDA) with **aspirin 3-5 mg/kg for 6 months**; NG feeding; one-page summary card.`],[`Special groups`,`Preterm PDA - ibuprofen 10-5-5 mg/kg or paracetamol 15 mg/kg 6-hourly 3 days; Down syndrome - echo all newborns; girls - avoid oestrogen in cyanosis/pulmonary hypertension, **Eisenmenger contraindicates pregnancy**.`],[`Complications`,`Heart failure, failure to thrive, recurrent pneumonia, **tet spells**, polycythaemia with relative iron deficiency, stroke, **brain abscess**, infective endocarditis, arrhythmia, Eisenmenger.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Same day: cyanosis or SpO2 under 90%, failed oximetry, tet spell, weak femorals, infant heart failure, exertional syncope; **grey shocked neonate - PGE1**; fever over 48 h in high-risk child - **3 blood cultures first**.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Monthly in infancy: weight, RR, liver, SpO2, doses by weight, electrolytes; after Fallot repair - annual ECG/echo, **cardiac MRI from adolescence**, QRS over 180 ms risk; transition at 16-18 years.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**RBSK** screening and free surgery via DEIC, PM-JAY; maternal rubella immunity, diabetes control, avoid teratogens; **fetal echo 18-22 weeks**; full immunisation + influenza + PCV; **nirsevimab or palivizumab for significant CHD**.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**IAP ACVIP 2025**: nirsevimab for high-risk infants only (CHD qualifies), trivalent influenza; IE prophylaxis unchanged (**AHA 2021 / ESC 2023** - amoxicillin 50 mg/kg, highest-risk only, no clindamycin).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`The louder the VSD, the smaller the hole; **never inotropes or digoxin in a tet spell**; cyanotic child with Hb 12 is iron deficient - never venesect; left axis in ASD = primum/AVSD.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Murmur in the OPD - innocent or refer`,steps:[{label:`Symptoms or poor growth?`,detail:`Feeding sweats, tachypnoea, failure to thrive, cyanosis, syncope`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Check SpO2 and femorals`,detail:`SpO2 under 90% or hand-foot gap over 3%; weak femorals or arm-leg gradient over 20 mmHg`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Murmur features`,detail:`Diastolic, pansystolic, grade 3+, thrill, abnormal S2 or click = pathological`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Pathological - echo and cardiology`,detail:`Start heart-failure drugs and high-calorie feeds while waiting; plan repair window`},{label:`Innocent - reassure and name it`,detail:`Thriving child, grade 1-2, musical, varies with posture: no echo, no restriction, no prophylaxis`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Tet spell - stepwise management`,steps:[{label:`Calm, knee-chest position`,detail:`In mother's arms; raises SVR and reduces venous return of acidotic blood`},{label:`100% oxygen`,detail:`Then morphine 0.1-0.2 mg/kg SC/IM (0.05-0.1 mg/kg slow IV)`},{label:`Volume and acidosis`,detail:`Normal saline 10-20 mL/kg; sodium bicarbonate 1-2 mEq/kg if pH under 7.2`},{label:`Relax infundibulum, raise SVR`,detail:`Propranolol 0.1 mg/kg slow IV; phenylephrine 5-20 mcg/kg IV; ketamine 1-2 mg/kg`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Refractory - ventilate, surgery`,detail:`A spell is an indication for early shunt or complete repair; never inotropes`,tone:`good`}]}]},"pediatrics-nocturnal-enuresis":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Enuresis** is intermittent wetting in discrete episodes during sleep at **age 5 or more**.`,`By 5 years, **80-85%** of children are dry at night.`,`Prevalence is 15-20% at 5 years, 10% at 7 and 5% at 10; boys 2:1.`,`About **15% become dry each year** without treatment.`,`Treat because of the **child's distress**, not because of the birthday.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Primary**: never dry for 6 continuous months (75-80%).`,`**Secondary**: wetting after 6 months dry; look for an acquired cause.`,`**Monosymptomatic**: no daytime symptoms (about two thirds).`,`**Non-monosymptomatic**: any daytime symptom such as urgency, daytime wetting or straining.`,`**Continuous dampness** day and night is not enuresis; think ectopic ureter.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. A wet bed needs night urine to exceed bladder capacity **and** the child to stay asleep.`,`2. **Nocturnal polyuria**: the normal night-time rise of vasopressin is blunted.`,`3. **Small bladder**: nocturnal detrusor overactivity empties it early.`,`4. **High arousal threshold**: the brain fails to wake to a full bladder.`,`5. **Constipation** presses on the bladder and triggers overactivity.`,`6. It runs in families: about 40% risk with one parent, 70-77% with both.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Count wet nights in the last 2 weeks.`,`Ask: **ever dry for 6 months?** and **any daytime symptoms?**`,`Take a timed fluid history and ask about caffeine.`,`Take a **bowel history every time**; ask about soiling.`,`New wetting with thirst and weight loss is **type 1 diabetes** until proved otherwise.`,`Ask about snoring (OSA), night itch (threadworm) and how the family responds.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Plot growth and measure **blood pressure**.`,`Feel for a faecal mass and a **bladder palpable after voiding**.`,`Look at the **lumbosacral spine** for dimple, hair tuft or lipoma.`,`Check lower-limb reflexes and gait for a tethered cord.`,`Look at genitalia, tonsils and for signs of abuse.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`A well child needs **only a urine dipstick and a bladder-bowel diary**.`,`Morning specific gravity **over 1.020** excludes diabetes insipidus.`,`Expected bladder capacity (mL) = **(age + 1) x 30**.`,`Overnight output **over 130%** of EBC means nocturnal polyuria.`,`Maximum voided volume **under 65%** of EBC means a small bladder.`,`Ultrasound with residual only for daytime symptoms, poor stream or UTI.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Remove blame** in front of the child; punishment makes wetting worse.`,`Treat **constipation**, UTI, OSA and threadworm first.`,`Normal fluids spread through the day; no fluid restriction as treatment.`,`Void 4-7 times a day and just before bed; cut caffeine.`,`**Reward effort, never dry nights**; do not lift the child at night.`,`**Enuresis alarm**: first-line definitive treatment; the only one that cures.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Judge the alarm after **6-8 weeks**; continue to **14 consecutive dry nights**.`,`**Desmopressin melt 120 micrograms** 30-60 minutes before bed; increase to 240 if partial.`,`Fluid rule: **no more than 240 mL** from 1 hour before to 8 hours after.`,`Skip desmopressin on days with vomiting, diarrhoea or fever.`,`Continue for 3 months, then **structured withdrawal**.`,`Small bladder: add **oxybutynin**; check post-void residual first.`,`Imipramine only from a specialist; overdose is fatally cardiotoxic.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Headache, vomiting or seizure on desmopressin means **hyponatraemia**; stop and check sodium.`,`Poor stream or palpable bladder in a boy suggests **posterior urethral valves**.`,`Sacral stigma or abnormal gait suggests **occult spinal dysraphism**; MRI.`,`Growth failure, hypertension or proteinuria suggests CKD.`,`Abrupt secondary wetting with behaviour change: consider **sexual abuse**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review alarm and desmopressin at **4 weeks**, then at 3 months.`,`Complete response is 100% dry nights; relapse is more than 1 wet night a month.`,`Before calling failure, recheck adherence, constipation, OSA and ADHD.`,`Refer refractory enuresis and any red flag.`,`**Never intranasal desmopressin** for enuresis.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Enuresis drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Desmopressin melt`,`120 mcg sublingual, up to 240 mcg (tablet 200-400 mcg)`,`3 months, then structured withdrawal`,`Hyponatraemia; max 240 mL fluid; never intranasal`],[`Oxybutynin`,`2.5-5 mg at bedtime or twice daily, max 15 mg/day`,`With desmopressin if small bladder`,`Dry mouth, constipation, retention`],[`Solifenacin`,`5 mg oral once daily`,`As needed`,`Check post-void residual`],[`Macrogol (PEG 3350)`,`1-1.5 g/kg/day, then 0.4-0.8 g/kg/day`,`3-6 days, then 2-3 months or more`,`Bloating, loose stools`],[`Lactulose`,`1-2 mL/kg/day in 1-2 doses`,`As maintenance`,`Bloating`],[`Imipramine (specialist)`,`25-75 mg at bedtime by age`,`Up to 3 months`,`Fatal cardiotoxicity in overdose`],[`Albendazole (threadworm)`,`400 mg single dose, whole household`,`Repeat after 2 weeks`,`Mild abdominal pain`]]},{heading:`Monosymptomatic or non-monosymptomatic?`,columns:[`Feature`,`Monosymptomatic`,`Non-monosymptomatic`],rows:[[`Daytime symptoms`,`None`,`Urgency, wetting, straining`],[`Main mechanism`,`Nocturnal polyuria, high arousal`,`Overactive or small bladder`],[`Diary`,`Night output over 130% EBC`,`Max volume under 65% EBC`],[`Tests`,`Dipstick and diary only`,`Add ultrasound and residual`],[`First treatment`,`Alarm or desmopressin`,`Constipation and bladder first`]]},{heading:`Alarm or desmopressin?`,columns:[`Feature`,`Alarm`,`Desmopressin`],rows:[[`Effect`,`Cures (conditions waking)`,`Suppresses (concentrates urine)`],[`Success`,`About two thirds`,`Response in 60-70%`],[`Relapse`,`30-50% (lowest)`,`60-70% if stopped abruptly`],[`Best for`,`Motivated family, frequent wetting`,`Nocturnal polyuria, camps and trips`],[`Main risk`,`Family fatigue`,`Hyponatraemia if fluid rule broken`]]}],remember:[`Two questions: ever dry 6 months? any daytime symptoms?`,`Dipstick and diary only for a well child.`,`EBC = (age + 1) x 30 mL.`,`Treat constipation first.`,`Alarm cures; desmopressin suppresses.`,`Desmopressin: one glass of fluid from 1 hour before to 8 hours after.`,`Sudden wetting in a dry child is diabetes until a glucose says otherwise.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - Nocturnal enuresis`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**ICCS**: intermittent incontinence in discrete episodes **during sleep at 5 years or more**; DSM-5-TR - twice a week for 3 months or distress. Continuous dampness is never enuresis.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Primary** (never dry 6 months, 75-80%) vs **secondary** (after 6 months dry); **monosymptomatic** (no daytime LUTS, two thirds) vs **non-monosymptomatic** (urgency, day wetting, voids under 4 or over 7, holding, straining).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Genetic (**40% one parent, 70-77% both**), boys 2:1, constipation (30-50%), OSA, ADHD; secondary - **diabetes**, UTI, threadworm, stress, abuse, CKD, DI, spinal dysraphism, seizures.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`**Three-factor model**: nocturnal polyuria (**blunted night AVP**) + small functional bladder (detrusor overactivity) + **failure to arouse** -> wet bed; loaded rectum compresses bladder.`],[`Key history`,`Wet nights in 2 weeks; **ever dry 6 months?**; daytime symptoms; timed fluids, caffeine; **bowel history (Bristol)**; polyuria/polydipsia; UTI, threadworm; snoring; family history; treatments tried; family response (punishment).`],[`Key examination`,`Height, weight, **BP**; abdomen (faecal mass, palpable bladder); genitalia; **lumbosacral stigmata**; lower-limb neuro and gait; tonsils; observe stream in boys.`],[`Investigations`,`Primary monosymptomatic: **urine dipstick + bladder-bowel diary only**; first-morning SG over 1.020 excludes DI; glucose if polyuria or abrupt onset; ultrasound with PVR if daytime symptoms; MRI spine only with neuro signs.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Diary: **overnight output over 130% of EBC = nocturnal polyuria**; **max voided volume under 65% of EBC = small bladder**; **EBC (mL) = (age + 1) x 30**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Remove blame; treat constipation, UTI, OSA, threadworm; normal fluids spread through day; cut caffeine; void before bed; **enuresis alarm** (cures, lowest relapse) or **desmopressin melt 120 mcg (tablet 200 mcg) 30-60 min before bed**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Desmopressin to **240 mcg melt / 400 mcg tablet** after 1-2 weeks partial; 3 months then structured withdrawal; **antimuscarinic (oxybutynin 2.5-5 mg)** for small bladder after checking PVR; combination; **imipramine** specialist only (cardiotoxic).`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Alarm every night; child wakes fully, switches off, finishes in toilet, resets; review at 4 weeks; continue to **14 consecutive dry nights**; **overlearning** with 200-250 mL extra fluid; do not judge before 6-8 weeks.`],[`Special groups`,`Child under 7 or not distressed - general measures 6-8 weeks first; camps and trips - short-term desmopressin; ADHD - adapt programme; neurogenic bladder - urology.`],[`Complications`,`Low self-esteem, punishment and abuse; **desmopressin hyponatraemia and water intoxication**; antimuscarinic retention and constipation; imipramine overdose death.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Secondary wetting with polyuria (**check glucose today**), poor stream or palpable bladder (PUV), sacral stigma or abnormal gait, continuous dampness (ectopic ureter), hypertension or proteinuria, safeguarding concerns.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Wet-night chart; review at **4 weeks and 3 months**; ICCS response - none under 50%, partial 50-99%, complete 100%; relapse over 1 wet night/month.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`No national programme; school health and RBSK can identify; counsel parents early that enuresis is common and inherited.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - **ICCS 2020** and NICE CG111 remain current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Desmopressin safety: **no more than 240 mL from 1 h before to 8 h after**, skip on sick days, **never intranasal**; reward effort not dry nights; about 15% resolve each year.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Bedwetting child - two questions then treat`,steps:[{label:`Ever dry for 6 months?`,detail:`Yes = secondary - look for diabetes, UTI, stress, abuse`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Any daytime symptoms?`,detail:`Yes = non-monosymptomatic - treat constipation and daytime bladder first`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Dipstick and diary`,detail:`Glucose, nitrite, SG; overnight output vs 130% EBC; max voided volume vs 65% EBC`},{label:`Red flags?`,detail:`Poor stream, palpable bladder, sacral stigma, gait change, hypertension - refer`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Alarm or desmopressin`,detail:`Alarm to 14 dry nights; desmopressin 120-240 mcg melt with the fluid rule`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Alarm versus desmopressin`,columns:[`Feature`,`Enuresis alarm`,`Desmopressin`],rows:[[`Mechanism`,`Conditions arousal`,`V2 agonist, concentrates night urine`],[`Best for`,`Frequent wetting, motivated family`,`Nocturnal polyuria, rapid or short-term effect`],[`Success`,`About two thirds dry`,`60-70% respond, 30% full`],[`Relapse`,`30-50% (lowest)`,`60-70% on abrupt stop`],[`Key safety`,`Parent support first weeks`,`Fluid limit 1 h before to 8 h after; skip on sick days`]]}]},"pediatrics-oral-thrush":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Oral thrush is overgrowth of the commensal **Candida albicans** in the mouth.`,`Candida is a commensal, so thrush means **the host or environment has changed**.`,`It affects about **5% of healthy newborns**; in a well term baby it is trivial.`,`**C. krusei** is intrinsically fluconazole-resistant; C. glabrata often is.`,`Unexplained persistent oral thrush after 2 months is **WHO HIV stage 3**.`,`Oesophageal candidiasis is **WHO stage 4**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Acute pseudomembranous** (thrush): white plaques that scrape off.`,`**Acute erythematous**: red, glazed, painful mouth after antibiotics; no plaque.`,`**Chronic erythematous**: denture stomatitis under the upper plate.`,`**Chronic hyperplastic**: adherent white plaque in smokers; malignant potential, so biopsy.`,`Also angular cheilitis and median rhomboid glossitis.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Saliva, normal flora, epithelium and **Th17 immunity** normally keep Candida in check.`,`2. Antibiotics, inhaled steroids, dry mouth, diabetes or HIV weaken these defences.`,`3. Candida switches from **yeast to invasive hyphae** and forms biofilm.`,`4. The plaque is matted hyphae, dead cells and fibrin on inflamed mucosa.`,`5. Teats, dummies and the **mother's nipples** reseed the baby's mouth.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about feeding refusal and urine output in an infant.`,`Ask about bottles, teats, dummies and how they are cleaned.`,`Ask the mother about **nipple pain** after feeds.`,`Ask about recent antibiotics and **inhaled steroids** without a spacer.`,`Ask about **painful swallowing**, which suggests oesophageal disease.`,`Ask about HIV risk, diabetes symptoms and chemotherapy.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Thrush **scrapes off to a raw red base**; milk curds leave normal pink mucosa.`,`Leukoplakia and lichen planus **do not scrape off**.`,`Weigh and plot every infant with persistent thrush.`,`In a neonate look for **sepsis signs**; they need a septic screen, not nystatin.`,`Look for pallor, nodes and hepatosplenomegaly for an underlying cause.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Typical thrush in a well infant is a **clinical diagnosis**.`,`KOH mount shows budding yeasts with **pseudohyphae**.`,`Culture and sensitivity only for failure, recurrence or immunosuppression.`,`**HIV test** for unexplained, persistent or recurrent thrush beyond infancy.`,`Glucose, Hb and B12 in older children; biopsy a white patch that does not scrape off.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Nystatin 100,000 units/mL**: neonate 1 mL four times daily.`,`Give it **after feeds** and paint it over all the mucosa.`,`Continue **7-14 days** and at least **48 hours after** the mouth looks clear.`,`**Miconazole gel** from 4 months: 1.25 mL four times daily (2.5 mL from 2 years).`,`**Never miconazole with warfarin**; avoid it under 4 months (choking).`,`Boil teats and bottles daily and **treat the mother's nipples** at the same time.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Fluconazole** for severe disease, immunosuppression or topical failure.`,`Child dose: **6 mg/kg on day 1**, then 3 mg/kg daily for 7-14 days.`,`Oesophageal candidiasis always needs **systemic fluconazole** for 14-21 days.`,`Neonates need fluconazole every 48-72 hours because of renal immaturity.`,`In HIV, **ART is the definitive treatment** of recurrent thrush.`,`Inhaler users: spacer, then **rinse, gargle and spit** after every dose.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review at **7-14 days**; most thrush clears within a week.`,`If it fails, check diagnosis, technique, the cause and resistant species, in that order.`,`Admit an unwell or preterm neonate with thrush; it may be **invasive candidiasis**.`,`Admit an infant who is not feeding or is dehydrated.`,`Refer mouth, nail and skin candidiasis from infancy for immunology (APECED, CMC).`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antifungal drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Nystatin suspension`,`Neonate 1 mL QID; child 1-2 mL; older 4-6 mL QID`,`7-14 days and 48 h after clear`,`Bitter taste; give after feeds`],[`Miconazole oral gel 2%`,`1.25 mL QID (4 mo-2 y); 2.5 mL QID over 2 y`,`7 days after healing`,`Choking under 4 months; raises warfarin INR`],[`Clotrimazole`,`10 mg troche 5 times daily or 1% paint`,`7-14 days`,`Older children only for troche`],[`Fluconazole`,`6 mg/kg day 1, then 3 mg/kg daily oral`,`7-14 days; 14-21 if oesophageal`,`Hepatotoxicity, QT, drug interactions`],[`Gentian violet 0.5%`,`Paint once or twice daily`,`Up to 3 days`,`Staining, mucosal ulceration`],[`Miconazole 2% cream`,`Mother's nipples after every feed`,`14 days`,`Wipe off before next feed`]]},{heading:`White lesions of the mouth`,columns:[`Lesion`,`Scrapes off?`,`Key clue`],rows:[[`Thrush`,`Yes, raw red base`,`Predisposing factor present`],[`Milk curds`,`Yes, normal mucosa`,`Happy feeding baby`],[`Leukoplakia`,`No`,`Tobacco user; biopsy`],[`Lichen planus`,`No`,`Bilateral lacy striae`],[`Hairy leukoplakia`,`No`,`Lateral tongue; HIV marker`],[`Koplik spots`,`No`,`Measles prodrome`]]},{heading:`Clinical types`,columns:[`Type`,`Appearance`,`Typical patient`],rows:[[`Pseudomembranous`,`White curd-like plaques`,`Neonate, antibiotics, HIV`],[`Acute erythematous`,`Red glazed painful tongue`,`After antibiotics or inhaled steroids`],[`Denture stomatitis`,`Red palate under denture`,`Elderly, denture worn at night`],[`Chronic hyperplastic`,`Adherent white plaque`,`Male smoker; biopsy`],[`Angular cheilitis`,`Fissured mouth angles`,`Iron or B12 deficiency, drooling`]]}],remember:[`Candida is a commensal; thrush means something changed. Find it.`,`Thrush scrapes off to a red base; curds leave pink mucosa.`,`Nystatin after feeds, painted on, and 48 hours beyond cure.`,`Nystatin under 4 months; no miconazole with warfarin.`,`Treat baby, nipples and teats together.`,`Painful swallowing means oesophageal disease: systemic fluconazole.`,`Unexplained thrush in an older child: test for HIV.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`GINA 2026 citation for inhaled-steroid thrush prevention (spacer, rinse-gargle-spit) - advice unchanged`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Oral thrush and candidiasis`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Opportunistic overgrowth of commensal **Candida** (C. albicans 50-90%) on oral mucosa - always a marker of changed host or environment.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Acute pseudomembranous (thrush)**, acute erythematous (antibiotic sore mouth), chronic erythematous (denture stomatitis), **chronic hyperplastic (premalignant, biopsy)**, angular cheilitis, median rhomboid glossitis. HIV: persistent oral = **stage 3**, oesophageal = **stage 4**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Neonates (5%), antibiotics, **inhaled steroids without spacer/rinse**, diabetes, HIV, chemotherapy, xerostomia, dentures worn at night, haematinic deficiency, CMC/APECED; **C. krusei fluconazole-resistant**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Loss of saliva, flora, epithelium or **Th17 immunity** -> yeast-to-hypha switch, proteinases, candidalysin, biofilm -> pseudomembrane of hyphae and debris -> **scrapes off to raw red base**.`],[`Key history`,`Feeding refusal and intake; bottles, teats, dummies; **mother's nipple pain**; antibiotics, inhaled steroid technique; prematurity, sepsis signs; duration and recurrence; odynophagia; HIV risk; polyuria; dentures; napkin rash.`],[`Key examination`,`Whole mouth with light; **scrape test** (thrush raw base, milk curd normal mucosa, leukoplakia does not come off); red depapillated areas; angles; weight and hydration; sepsis signs in neonate; HIV signs; napkin area; mother's nipples.`],[`Investigations`,`Typical infant thrush - **clinical**; KOH or Gram stain (pseudohyphae) if unclear; culture and speciation only for failure or recurrence; **HIV test** for unexplained thrush beyond infancy; glucose; biopsy if non-scrapable or persisting.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Creamy curd-like plaques that wipe off to raw red, sometimes bleeding base, with a predisposing factor; erythematous form - red glazed painful mucosa without plaque.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Nystatin 100,000 U/mL**: neonate **1 mL QID**, child 1-2 mL QID, **after feeds, painted on all mucosa, 7-14 days and 48 h after clearance**; or **miconazole gel 2%** 1.25 mL QID (4 months-2 years) - avoid under 4 months and with warfarin.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Fluconazole 6 mg/kg day 1 then 3 mg/kg/day for 7-14 days** (neonate every 48-72 h); **oesophageal - 3-6 mg/kg/day for 14-21 days**; refractory - itraconazole, posaconazole, echinocandin (specialist).`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Boil teats, bottles, dummies, pump parts 5-10 min daily; spacer and **rinse-gargle-spit** after ICS; dentures out at night, soak in chlorhexidine 0.2%; nystatin and chlorhexidine 30 min apart.`],[`Special groups`,`**Breastfeeding dyad** - treat baby and nipples together (miconazole cream after each feed 14 days); HIV - **ART is definitive**; preterm with central line - septic screen for invasive candidiasis.`],[`Complications`,`Feeding refusal and dehydration, oesophageal candidiasis, invasive candidiasis in VLBW, ping-pong reinfection, malignant change in hyperplastic form.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Unexplained thrush in older child or adult (HIV, diabetes); odynophagia; unwell or preterm neonate; failure to thrive; non-scrapable white patch beyond 2-3 weeks; mouth, nail and skin candidiasis from infancy.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review at **7-14 days**; if fails - wrong diagnosis, poor technique, uncorrected cause, resistant species; weigh infant; link HIV-positive to ART centre.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Rational antibiotic use, inhaler technique, sterile feeding equipment, breastfeeding support; HIV testing under **NACO** with PPTCT.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No guideline change; GINA citation updated to **2026** for ICS-thrush prevention (spacer, rinse-gargle-spit).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Not all candidiasis is white; give nystatin **after** feeds; oesophageal disease always needs systemic azole; in HIV, ART not repeated fluconazole treats recurrence.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`White patch in the mouth - decide and treat`,steps:[{label:`Does it scrape off?`,detail:`Raw red base = thrush; normal mucosa = milk curd; does not come off = leukoplakia, lichen planus, hairy leukoplakia`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Well term infant`,detail:`Nystatin 1 mL QID after feeds, 48 h beyond clearance; treat nipples and teats`,tone:`good`},{label:`Odynophagia or immunosuppressed`,detail:`Systemic fluconazole; oesophageal 14-21 days`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Unexplained in older child`,detail:`HIV test, glucose, haematinics; WHO stage 3 if persistent`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Failure at 2 weeks`,detail:`Check technique and cause, culture for C. krusei/glabrata, biopsy persistent lesions`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Nystatin versus miconazole gel versus fluconazole`,columns:[`Feature`,`Nystatin`,`Miconazole gel`,`Fluconazole`],rows:[[`Class`,`Polyene, not absorbed`,`Topical azole`,`Systemic azole`],[`Age`,`Any, preferred under 4 months`,`From 4 months`,`Any (neonate 48-72 hourly)`],[`Dose`,`1-2 mL QID (neonate 1 mL)`,`1.25 mL QID (4 m-2 y), 2.5 mL QID over 2 y`,`6 mg/kg day 1, then 3 mg/kg daily`],[`Key caution`,`Give after feeds; continue 48 h`,`Choking; raises warfarin INR`,`QT, hepatotoxicity, CYP interactions`]]}]},"pediatrics-neonatal-jaundice":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Jaundice becomes visible in a newborn at about **5 mg/dL** and spreads head to toe.`,`It affects about **60% of term and 80% of preterm** babies in the first week.`,`**Physiological jaundice** appears after 24 hours and peaks on **day 3-5** in term babies.`,`**Jaundice in the first 24 hours is always pathological.**`,`**Prolonged jaundice** lasts beyond 14 days in term or 21 days in preterm babies.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Physiological versus pathological, and **unconjugated versus conjugated**.`,`**Direct bilirubin over 1 mg/dL** at any age is pathological.`,`First 24 hours: haemolysis, sepsis, TORCH.`,`Day 2-7: physiological, poor intake, haemolysis, bruising, sepsis.`,`After 2 weeks conjugated: **biliary atresia** until proved otherwise.`,`Thresholds come from the **AAP 2022 hour-specific curves**, not a fixed number.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Newborns make **twice the adult bilirubin** from many short-lived red cells.`,`2. The liver enzyme **UGT1A1** is only about 1% of adult activity at birth.`,`3. Gut deconjugation and slow stooling **recirculate bilirubin** back to blood.`,`4. Only **free bilirubin** crosses into the brain; acidosis, sepsis and ceftriaxone increase it.`,`5. It damages basal ganglia, brainstem and cochlear nuclei: **kernicterus**.`,`6. Blue-green light turns bilirubin into water-soluble **lumirubin**, excreted without conjugation.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask the **age in hours** when jaundice started.`,`Ask gestation, mother's blood group and Rh, and previous babies with phototherapy.`,`Ask about breastfeeds (8-12 a day), wet nappies and **weight loss**.`,`Ask about **naphthalene mothballs** and herbal drops (G6PD triggers).`,`Ask about **pale stools and dark urine**; use the stool colour card.`,`Ask about lethargy, poor suck or high-pitched cry.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Blanch the skin in daylight and note the **Kramer zone**.`,`**Visual assessment is unreliable**, especially in dark skin; measure instead.`,`Look for **encephalopathy**: hypotonia, then arching, retrocollis and setting-sun eyes.`,`Check percentage weight loss; over 10% suggests poor intake.`,`Look for pallor, hepatosplenomegaly, cephalhaematoma and sepsis signs.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Measure **TSB or TcB** in every newborn of 35 weeks or more before discharge.`,`Measure **within 2 hours** for any jaundice in the first 24 hours.`,`Plot against **age in hours**; the gap to the threshold decides action.`,`Haemolysis screen: blood groups, **direct Coombs test**, Hb, smear, reticulocytes, G6PD.`,`Prolonged jaundice: direct bilirubin, stool colour, TSH, G6PD, urine culture.`,`Pale stools: fasting ultrasound and urgent hepatology referral.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Breastfeed **8-12 times a day**; continue breastfeeding during phototherapy.`,`Poor intake: fix latch and give **expressed breast milk top-ups**; never glucose water.`,`**Intensive phototherapy**: 460-490 nm light at 30 microW/cm2/nm or more.`,`Baby naked, eyes shielded, lamp at 30-45 cm, turned every 2-4 hours.`,`Repeat TSB within 12 hours; skin colour is no guide under lights.`,`Stop when TSB is **2 mg/dL below the starting threshold**; check rebound if high risk.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Escalation starts **2 mg/dL below the exchange threshold**.`,`Isoimmune haemolysis at escalation: **IVIG 0.5-1 g/kg** over 2 hours.`,`**Double-volume exchange** (160-170 mL/kg) at exchange level or any encephalopathy.`,`Sepsis: ampicillin plus gentamicin or cefotaxime; **avoid ceftriaxone**.`,`No sunlight, phenobarbitone, herbal drops or routine albumin.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Kernicterus tetrad**: athetoid CP, hearing loss, upward gaze palsy, enamel dysplasia.`,`**Any sign of encephalopathy** means emergency exchange, whatever the TSB.`,`Yellow palms and soles mean refer the same hour.`,`Phototherapy in a cholestatic baby causes **bronze baby syndrome**.`,`Bilirubin rising over 0.2 mg/dL per hour suggests haemolysis.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`A baby discharged before 48 hours is seen within **48-72 hours**.`,`Pale stools or direct bilirubin over 1 mg/dL: refer the same week.`,`**Kasai operation before 60 days** (ideally 45) for biliary atresia.`,`After severe jaundice, test hearing by **ABR** and follow development.`,`Prevent Rh disease with **anti-D 300 micrograms** at 28 weeks and after delivery.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Jaundice drugs and procedures`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`IVIG`,`0.5-1 g/kg IV over 2 hours`,`Repeat in 12 h if needed`,`Fluid overload, possible NEC; DCT-positive only`],[`Ampicillin`,`50 mg/kg IV 12-hourly (8-hourly after week 1)`,`7-14 days by culture`,`Rash, diarrhoea`],[`Gentamicin`,`4-5 mg/kg IV once daily`,`7-14 days by culture`,`Nephrotoxic, ototoxic`],[`Cefotaxime`,`50 mg/kg IV 12-hourly`,`As for sepsis`,`Use instead of ceftriaxone`],[`Levothyroxine`,`10-15 mcg/kg oral once daily`,`Long term`,`Not with soy or iron`],[`Ursodeoxycholic acid`,`10-20 mg/kg/day in 2-3 doses`,`In cholestasis, after referral`,`Diarrhoea; add vitamins A, D, E, K`],[`Exchange transfusion`,`160-170 mL/kg double volume`,`Over 60-90 minutes`,`Hypocalcaemia, thrombocytopenia, NEC`],[`Anti-D (mother)`,`300 mcg IM`,`28 weeks and within 72 h of delivery`,`Prevents Rh disease`]]},{heading:`Physiological or pathological?`,columns:[`Feature`,`Physiological`,`Pathological`],rows:[[`Onset`,`After 24 hours`,`Within 24 hours`],[`Peak`,`Day 3-5, below threshold`,`At or above threshold`],[`Rate of rise`,`Under 5 mg/dL per day`,`Over 0.2 mg/dL per hour`],[`Direct bilirubin`,`Under 1 mg/dL`,`Over 1 mg/dL`],[`Duration`,`Under 2 weeks (3 if preterm)`,`Longer`],[`Baby`,`Well, yellow stools`,`Lethargic, pale stools`]]},{heading:`Kramer zones`,columns:[`Zone`,`Extent`,`Approximate TSB (mg/dL)`],rows:[[`1`,`Head and neck`,`5-6`],[`2`,`Upper trunk`,`9`],[`3`,`Lower trunk and thighs`,`12`],[`4`,`Arms and legs below knees`,`15`],[`5`,`Palms and soles`,`Over 15; refer`]]}],remember:[`Jaundice in the first 24 hours is never physiological.`,`Plot bilirubin against age in hours; the gap to threshold decides.`,`Escalate 2 mg/dL below exchange; stop phototherapy 2 below the start.`,`Keep breastfeeding; never glucose water; sunlight is not phototherapy.`,`Pale stools plus jaundice is biliary atresia: Kasai before 60 days.`,`Kernicterus tetrad: athetosis, deafness, upward gaze palsy, enamel dysplasia.`,`No ceftriaxone in a jaundiced neonate.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - Neonatal jaundice`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Visible yellowing at about **5 mg/dL** bilirubin, cephalocaudal; affects 60% term and 80% preterm in week 1.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Physiological vs pathological**; **unconjugated vs conjugated (direct over 1 mg/dL = cholestasis)**; significance by **AAP 2022 hour-specific curves** adjusted for gestation and neurotoxicity risk; **prolonged** beyond 14 days (term) or 21 days (preterm).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`First 24 h - **Rh/ABO haemolysis, G6PD, sepsis, TORCH**; day 2-7 - physiological, suboptimal intake, bruising, polycythaemia; late - breast-milk jaundice, hypothyroidism, UTI; conjugated - **biliary atresia**, hepatitis, choledochal cyst, galactosaemia.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`High red-cell mass and short life -> **6-8 mg/kg/day bilirubin**; **UGT1A1 at 1% of adult** -> under-conjugation; enterohepatic recirculation; **free unbound bilirubin crosses BBB** (acidosis, sepsis, low albumin, ceftriaxone) -> basal ganglia, cochlear nuclei -> kernicterus.`],[`Key history`,`**Age in hours at onset**; gestation; maternal blood group, anti-D, previous affected baby; feeding frequency, wet nappies, weight loss; mothballs, ghutti; family history G6PD; danger signs; **stool and urine colour (stool card)**.`],[`Key examination`,`Blanch skin in daylight every 12 h, Kramer zone (screen only); neuro signs of encephalopathy; % weight loss; pallor, hepatosplenomegaly, cephalhaematoma; sepsis signs; nappy - pale stool, dark urine; hypothyroid features.`],[`Investigations`,`**TcB or TSB in every baby 35 weeks or more before discharge**, within 2 h if jaundice in first 24 h; plot on AAP 2022 curve; confirm TcB with TSB near threshold; **blood groups, DCT, Hb, smear, retics, G6PD**; direct bilirubin in prolonged jaundice.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Pathological: onset in 24 h, at or above hour-specific threshold, rise **over 0.2 mg/dL/h**, direct over 1 mg/dL, beyond 2-3 weeks, ill baby or pale stools.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Breastfeed **8-12 times/day**, EBM top-ups (never glucose water); **intensive LED phototherapy 460-490 nm, 30 microW/cm2/nm or more**, eyes covered, naked, turn 2-4 hourly; repeat TSB within 12 h; stop **2 mg/dL below start threshold**, rebound check if haemolysis.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Escalation at 2 mg/dL below exchange threshold**: NICU, TSB 2-hourly, **IVIG 0.5-1 g/kg over 2 h** in DCT-positive isoimmune disease; **double-volume exchange 160-170 mL/kg** at exchange level or any encephalopathy.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`LED unit at manufacturer distance, radiometer check, side curtains, fibre-optic blanket; TcB on sternum (not under phototherapy); exchange via umbilical vein, 5-10 mL aliquots over 60-90 min.`],[`Special groups`,`Preterm - **lower thresholds**; G6PD - avoid naphthalene, oxidants; Rh-negative mothers - **anti-D 300 mcg at 28 weeks and within 72 h**; cholestasis - **ursodeoxycholic acid**, ADEK vitamins; hypothyroidism - levothyroxine 10-15 mcg/kg.`],[`Complications`,`**Acute bilirubin encephalopathy** (BIND score) -> **kernicterus tetrad** (athetoid CP, auditory neuropathy, upward gaze palsy, enamel dysplasia); bronze baby if phototherapy in cholestasis; exchange complications.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Jaundice in first 24 h; **palms and soles**; at or above threshold; lethargy, poor suck, hypertonia, high-pitched cry; **pale stools**; sepsis signs; rise despite phototherapy; prolonged jaundice.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`By **gap to threshold** at discharge (under 2 - phototherapy, 2-3.4 - recheck 4-24 h, 3.5-5.4 - 1-2 days); discharged before 48 h seen at 48-72 h; **Kasai before 60 days (ideally 45)**; ABR after severe hyperbilirubinaemia.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Early and frequent breastfeeding, universal pre-discharge bilirubin, anti-D prophylaxis, stool colour card, G6PD awareness, **SNCU referral** from PHC; RBSK DEIC follow-up.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - **AAP 2022** hour-specific thresholds and escalation-of-care concept remain current; NICE CG98 (2023), NNF 2020.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Jaundice in 24 h is never physiological; sunlight is not phototherapy; phenobarbitone not a treatment; **avoid ceftriaxone in jaundiced neonates**; pale stools = biliary atresia until proved otherwise.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Jaundiced newborn - AAP 2022 approach`,steps:[{label:`Onset in first 24 hours?`,detail:`Pathological - TSB within 2 h, DCT, blood groups, G6PD, sepsis screen`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Measure and plot by hours`,detail:`TcB/TSB on gestation and risk-factor curve; confirm TcB near threshold`,tone:`decision`},{label:`At or above phototherapy line`,detail:`Intensive phototherapy, continue breastfeeding, repeat TSB within 12 h`},{label:`Within 2 mg/dL of exchange line`,detail:`Escalation: NICU, TSB 2-hourly, IVIG 0.5-1 g/kg if isoimmune, cross-match`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Exchange level or encephalopathy`,detail:`Double-volume exchange 160-170 mL/kg immediately`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Below threshold`,detail:`Follow-up by gap to threshold; stool colour check`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Physiological versus pathological jaundice`,columns:[`Feature`,`Physiological`,`Pathological`],rows:[[`Onset`,`After 24 h`,`Within 24 h`],[`Peak (term)`,`Day 3-5, below threshold`,`At or above hour-specific threshold`],[`Rate of rise`,`Under 5 mg/dL/day`,`Over 0.2 mg/dL/h`],[`Direct bilirubin`,`Under 1 mg/dL`,`Over 1 mg/dL`],[`Duration`,`Under 2 weeks term (3 preterm)`,`Beyond 2 weeks term (3 preterm)`]]}]},"pediatrics-low-birth-weight-preterm-kmc":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Low birth weight** is under 2500 g whatever the gestation; VLBW under 1500 g, ELBW under 1000 g.`,`**Preterm** is birth before 37 weeks; **SGA** is weight below the 10th centile for gestation.`,`NFHS-5: about **18% LBW**; prematurity and LBW are the **leading cause of neonatal death**.`,`**Most Indian LBW babies are term but growth restricted**, so maternal nutrition matters most.`,`Preterm birth: previous preterm birth, twins, PPROM, infection, cervical insufficiency.`,`Growth restriction: maternal undernutrition, anaemia, pre-eclampsia and tobacco.`,`A mother who was LBW herself continues the **intergenerational cycle**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Two axes: **by weight** (LBW, VLBW, ELBW) and **by gestation-for-weight** (SGA, AGA, LGA).`,`A baby can be **preterm and SGA at once**; this has the worst prognosis.`,`Preterm grades: extremely under 28 weeks, very 28-31, moderate to late 32-36.`,`**Symmetrical IUGR** starts early; head, length and weight are all small.`,`**Asymmetrical IUGR** starts late from placental insufficiency, with head sparing.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Large surface area, thin skin and little brown fat make LBW babies **cool fast**.`,`2. Cold burns oxygen and glucose, causing **hypoxia, hypoglycaemia and acidosis**.`,`3. Too little **surfactant** before 34 weeks causes respiratory distress syndrome.`,`4. An immature brainstem causes **apnoea of prematurity**.`,`5. **Suck-swallow-breathe coordination** matures only at 32-34 weeks.`,`6. Most calcium, phosphate and iron cross in the third trimester, so stores are low.`,`7. **KMC** turns the mother's chest into a natural heat source.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Confirm gestation from the early scan and the actual birth weight.`,`Ask whether **antenatal corticosteroids** were given.`,`Ask how the baby is fed and how many feeds in 24 hours.`,`Ask the mother how many hours a day she does **KMC**; under 8 is inadequate.`,`Ask about apnoea, blue spells, cold body, abdominal distension or blood in stool.`,`Ask about the mother's mood; postpartum depression is commoner after preterm birth.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Weigh and plot on INTERGROWTH-21st or Fenton charts.`,`LBW babies should **regain birth weight by 10-14 days**.`,`Use the **New Ballard score** when dates are uncertain.`,`Temperature: **cold feet with a warm abdomen** mean cold stress.`,`Count respiratory rate for a full minute; watch for apnoeic pauses.`,`Observe a breastfeed and check tone, suck and abdomen.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Glucometer checks at 2, 6, 12, 24, 48 and 72 hours; act **under 45 mg/dL**.`,`Measure bilirubin; preterm phototherapy thresholds are lower.`,`Venous haematocrit **over 65%** confirms polycythaemia.`,`Cranial ultrasound for babies under 32 weeks or under 1500 g.`,`**ROP screen** if under 34 weeks or under 2000 g, first by **day 30**.`,`Screen at day 20 if under 28 weeks or under 1200 g; also do a hearing screen.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Keep the **warm chain**; delay the bath and use a plastic bag under 32 weeks.`,`**Mother's own milk first**, then donor milk, formula only as last resort.`,`34 weeks or more: breastfeed; 32-33: **paladai or cup**; 28-31: orogastric tube.`,`Start feeds at **60-80 mL/kg/day**; increase by 20-30 to 150-180 mL/kg/day.`,`**Immediate KMC** for every baby under 2000 g (WHO 2022).`,`KMC at least **8 hours a day**, ideally 20-24, in sessions of at least 1 hour.`,`Vaccinate by **chronological age**, not corrected age.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Antenatal **dexamethasone 6 mg IM 12-hourly x 4** at 24-34 weeks.`,`Maternal **magnesium sulphate before 32 weeks** reduces cerebral palsy.`,`Apnoea: **caffeine citrate 20 mg/kg load**, then 5-10 mg/kg daily.`,`RDS: early **CPAP 5-7 cm H2O**, surfactant if oxygen needs exceed 30-40%.`,`Give **vitamin D 400 IU** daily and iron 2-3 mg/kg/day from 2-6 weeks to 1 year.`,`Add **human milk fortifier** for babies under 32 weeks or under 1500 g.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**A cold baby is a septic baby** until proved otherwise.`,`Moderate hypothermia: immediate skin-to-skin, recheck every 30 minutes.`,`Keep SpO2 **90-95%**, not higher, to prevent ROP.`,`Distension, bilious aspirates or bloody stools suggest **NEC**; stop feeds and refer.`,`Long-term risks: BPD, cerebral palsy, ROP blindness and learning difficulty.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`SNCU admission for **under 1800 g or under 34 weeks**, or any sick baby.`,`Refer **in KMC position** with the mother, warm and fed.`,`Discharge when gaining **15-20 g/kg/day** for 3 days and temperature is stable.`,`Review within 48-72 hours, then weekly until 2500 g.`,`Plot growth by **corrected age until 2 years**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`LBW and preterm drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Dexamethasone (mother)`,`6 mg IM 12-hourly`,`4 doses at 24-34 weeks`,`Maternal hyperglycaemia`],[`Magnesium sulphate (mother)`,`4 g IV over 20 min, then 1 g/hour`,`Until birth or 24 hours`,`Under 32 weeks for neuroprotection`],[`Vitamin K1`,`1 mg IM (0.5 mg if under 1000 g)`,`Once at birth`,`Local pain`],[`Caffeine citrate`,`20 mg/kg load, then 5-10 mg/kg daily`,`To about 34 weeks corrected`,`Hold if heart rate over 180`],[`Surfactant (poractant)`,`200 mg/kg intratracheal`,`Repeat once if needed`,`Bradycardia, desaturation`],[`Vitamin D3`,`400 IU oral daily (VLBW up to 800)`,`Week 1 to 1 year`,`Hypercalcaemia only with overdose`],[`Elemental iron`,`2-3 mg/kg/day oral, max 15 mg`,`2-6 weeks to 12 months`,`Dark stools, tooth staining`],[`Human milk fortifier`,`1 g per 25 mL breast milk`,`From 100 mL/kg/day feeds`,`Feed intolerance`],[`10% dextrose`,`2 mL/kg IV, then GIR 6-8 mg/kg/min`,`Until glucose stable`,`Never 25% or 50% dextrose`],[`Ampicillin + gentamicin`,`50 mg/kg 12-hourly + 5 mg/kg daily`,`7-10 days if culture positive`,`Gentamicin 36-48 hourly if very preterm`]]},{heading:`Newborn classification`,columns:[`Category`,`Definition`,`Main problems`],rows:[[`LBW`,`Under 2500 g`,`Hypothermia, feeding, sepsis`],[`VLBW`,`Under 1500 g`,`Tube feeds, apnoea, ROP, IVH`],[`ELBW`,`Under 1000 g`,`RDS, IVH, NEC`],[`Preterm AGA`,`Under 37 weeks, normal weight for age`,`RDS, apnoea, poor suck, jaundice`],[`Term SGA`,`37 weeks or more, under 10th centile`,`Hypoglycaemia, polycythaemia, asphyxia`]]},{heading:`Feeding method by gestation`,columns:[`Gestation`,`Start with`,`Progress to`],rows:[[`Under 28 weeks`,`IV fluids plus trophic feeds`,`Orogastric feeds`],[`28-31 weeks`,`Orogastric expressed milk`,`Cup or paladai`],[`32-33 weeks`,`Cup, spoon or paladai`,`Direct breastfeeding`],[`34 weeks or more`,`Direct breastfeeding`,`Exclusive breastfeeding`]]}],remember:[`LBW is under 2500 g; SGA is under the 10th centile for gestation.`,`Most Indian LBW babies are term and growth restricted.`,`Immediate KMC for every baby under 2000 g; cuts deaths by about a quarter.`,`Suck-swallow coordination at 32-34 weeks decides tube, paladai or breast.`,`A cold newborn is a septic newborn.`,`ROP screen: under 34 weeks or 2000 g, by day 30.`,`Growth by corrected age; vaccines by chronological age.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`WHO PSBI 2024: simplified regimen when referral is refused - IM gentamicin 7 days (2 days if not feasible) plus oral amoxicillin 7 days; fast breathing alone at 7-59 days gets amoxicillin without gentamicin`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Low birth weight, preterm and KMC`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**LBW under 2500 g** whatever the gestation; **VLBW under 1500 g, ELBW under 1000 g**; **preterm under 37 completed weeks**; SGA = under 10th centile for gestation.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Two axes: **by weight** (LBW, VLBW, ELBW) and **by gestation-for-weight** (SGA, AGA, LGA); preterm - extremely under 28, very 28-31, moderate-late 32-36 weeks; IUGR symmetrical vs asymmetrical (**ponderal index under 2.0**).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Preterm - previous preterm, multiple pregnancy, **PPROM, infection**, pre-eclampsia; FGR - **maternal undernutrition, anaemia, short stature, hypertension, tobacco**; India: about 18% LBW, **most are term growth-restricted**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Large surface area, thin skin, little brown fat -> **hypothermia -> hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, acidosis**; surfactant deficiency -> RDS; immature brainstem -> apnoea; suck-swallow at **32-34 weeks**; germinal matrix -> IVH; VEGF -> ROP.`],[`Key history`,`Gestation and birth weight; **antenatal steroids**; maternal risks; resuscitation; feeding method and number (2-hourly for VLBW); wet nappies; **KMC hours**; danger signs; jaundice; supplements; ROP and hearing screen booked; mother's mood and home.`],[`Key examination`,`Warm weighing; length, HC on INTERGROWTH/Fenton; New Ballard; **axillary temp** (cold stress 36.0-36.4); RR and distress; perfusion; jaundice (measure, do not eyeball); observe breastfeed; tone and suck; abdomen and umbilicus.`],[`Investigations`,`**Glucose at 2, 6, 12, 24, 48, 72 h** (act under 45 mg/dL); bilirubin on gestation charts; venous Hct (polycythaemia over 65%); blood culture if sepsis; cranial US under 32 weeks; **ROP screen under 34 weeks or 2000 g by day 30** (day 20 if under 28 weeks/1200 g); OAE.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Birth weight within first hour; gestation by first-trimester scan; **SGA by centile**, not by weight alone.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Warm chain**, mother's milk (then donor milk), feeds **60-80 mL/kg/day day 1, +20-30/day to 150-180**; **KMC for every baby under 2000 g started immediately after birth** (WHO 2022), at least 8 h and ideally 20-24 h a day; vitamin K 1 mg (0.5 mg under 1000 g).`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Feeding by maturity: **34 weeks direct breastfeed, 32-33 cup/paladai, 28-31 orogastric, under 28 IV + trophic**; HMF under 32 weeks or 1500 g; **caffeine 20 mg/kg load, 5-10 mg/kg/day**; CPAP 5-7 cm and surfactant if FiO2 over 0.30-0.40.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**KMC technique** - naked except nappy, cap, socks, upright between breasts, frog-like hips, binder; mother reclines 15-30 degrees; polyethylene bag under 32 weeks at birth; paladai; radiant warmer.`],[`Special groups`,`Antenatal **dexamethasone 6 mg IM 12-hourly x 4** at 24-34 weeks; **MgSO4 before 32 weeks** for neuroprotection; vaccinate by **chronological age**; PSBI with refused referral - **gentamicin 7 days + amoxicillin 7 days** (WHO 2024).`],[`Complications`,`Hypothermia, hypoglycaemia, RDS, apnoea, sepsis, **NEC**, jaundice, IVH/PVL, **ROP**, BPD, anaemia, metabolic bone disease, CP, learning difficulty; IUGR - adult metabolic syndrome.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Temp under 35.5 C or failure to rewarm; apnoea, grunting, chest indrawing; distension, bilious aspirates, bloody stools; jaundice in 24 h or palms-soles; glucose under 45 not correcting; **under 1800 g or under 34 weeks -> SNCU** (transfer in KMC position).`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Discharge on KMC when feeding well and gaining **15-20 g/kg/day for 3 days**; review 48-72 h then weekly to 2500 g; **corrected age to 2 years**; HBNC extra visits; high-risk follow-up clinic, DEIC.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**INAP**, SNCU/NBSU, Comprehensive Lactation Management Centres, HBNC/HBYC, maternal IFA, calcium, balanced energy-protein, aspirin for high-risk, 24-month spacing, no tobacco.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WHO PSBI 2024** simplified regimen with 7-day gentamicin; WHO 2022 immediate KMC remains the core recommendation.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Iron 2-3 mg/kg/day from 2-6 weeks and vitamin D 400 IU** to 1 year; a cold newborn is septic until proved otherwise; iKMC trial cut deaths by about a quarter; **never bottle-feed** - paladai.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`LBW baby - first-day decisions`,steps:[{label:`Weigh within the first hour`,detail:`Under 2500 g LBW; under 2000 g KMC immediately; under 1800 g or under 34 weeks - SNCU`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Warm chain and KMC`,detail:`Dry, skin-to-skin, cap and socks; KMC 8-24 h a day; check temp`,tone:`good`},{label:`Choose feeding by maturity`,detail:`34 wk breast, 32-33 paladai, 28-31 orogastric, under 28 IV + trophic; 60-80 mL/kg/day day 1`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Screen glucose and bilirubin`,detail:`Glucose 2, 6, 12, 24, 48, 72 h - act under 45 mg/dL; bilirubin on gestation chart`},{label:`Danger sign?`,detail:`Hypothermia, apnoea, distress, NEC signs - first antibiotic dose, refer in KMC position`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Supplements and screening timeline for the preterm baby`,steps:[{label:`Birth`,detail:`Vitamin K 1 mg IM (0.5 mg under 1000 g); BCG, OPV-0, HepB`},{label:`Week 1`,detail:`Vitamin D 400 IU daily (VLBW up to 800 IU); HMF once on 100 mL/kg/day if under 32 wk or 1500 g`},{label:`Day 20-30`,detail:`First ROP screen (day 20 if under 28 weeks or 1200 g); hearing OAE/AABR`,tone:`warn`},{label:`2-6 weeks`,detail:`Iron 2 mg/kg/day (3 mg/kg under 1500 g, max 15 mg) to 12 months`},{label:`Follow-up to 2 years`,detail:`Growth and development by corrected age; vaccines by chronological age`,tone:`good`}]}]},"pediatrics-development-behaviour-scholastic":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Development has four domains: **gross motor, fine motor, language and social**.`,`The **sequence is constant but the rate varies**, so red flags use limit ages.`,`**DQ = developmental age / chronological age x 100**; under 70 is significant delay.`,`Use **corrected age** for preterm babies until 2 years.`,`About **1 in 8** Indian children aged 2-9 has a neurodevelopmental disorder.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Global developmental delay**: delay in two or more domains under 5 years.`,`After 5 years the term becomes **intellectual disability** (IQ under about 70).`,`**Regression** is loss of acquired skills; it is a different and urgent problem.`,`**Autism**: social-communication deficits plus restricted, repetitive behaviours; levels 1-3.`,`**ADHD**: 6 symptoms, for 6 months, before 12 years, in two settings.`,`**Specific learning disorder**: dyslexia, dysgraphia or dyscalculia with normal intelligence.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`Prenatal: Down syndrome, **fragile X**, TORCH, fetal alcohol, congenital hypothyroidism.`,`Perinatal: birth asphyxia, prematurity, kernicterus, neonatal hypoglycaemia.`,`Postnatal: meningitis, head injury, malnutrition, iron deficiency, lead, deprivation.`,`Autism is about **80% heritable**; ADHD about 70-80%.`,`Regression: **Rett syndrome**, autistic regression, Landau-Kleffner, SSPE, neurodegeneration.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. The brain reaches about **80% of adult size by 2-3 years**.`,`2. Myelination runs head to toe, so **head control precedes sitting** and walking.`,`3. Primitive reflexes must fade; persistence beyond 6 months suggests cerebral palsy.`,`4. Language needs sound input in the first 2-3 years, so **deafness delays speech**.`,`5. ADHD reflects low dopamine and noradrenaline in frontal circuits.`,`6. Dyslexia is a **phonological processing deficit**, not laziness.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Start with the **parents' concern**; it is itself a red flag.`,`Take milestones domain by domain and **ask about lost skills**.`,`Ask about birth asphyxia, NICU stay, severe jaundice and neonatal seizures.`,`Autism clues: poor eye contact, **no response to name**, no pointing, lining up toys.`,`ADHD needs reports from **home and school**.`,`A **recent fall in a good student** suggests depression, abuse, drugs, bullying or absence epilepsy.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Plot **head circumference**; microcephaly and macrocephaly both matter.`,`Assess development by play, not only by report.`,`Look for dysmorphism and **neurocutaneous markers**.`,`**Hand preference before 18 months** is abnormal.`,`A boy walking late: look for **Gowers sign** and check CK.`,`Test **vision and hearing** in every child with delay or school failure.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Screen at **9, 18 and 30 months** with TDSC (0-2 years) or DDST-II.`,`**M-CHAT-R/F at 18 and 24 months**: 3-7 needs follow-up, 8 or more refer.`,`**Isolated speech delay: test hearing first.**`,`First bloods: TSH, haemoglobin and ferritin, CK in boys with motor delay.`,`**Chromosomal microarray and fragile X** are first genetic tests.`,`EEG for staring spells or regression; MRI only for focal signs or abnormal head size.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Refer early to the **District Early Intervention Centre (DEIC)** without waiting for a label.`,`Autism: early intensive **behavioural therapy**, speech and occupational therapy.`,`ADHD under 6 years: **parent training first**.`,`SLD: remedial phonics teaching and **exam accommodations** such as extra time.`,`No screens under 2 years; under 1 hour a day at 2-5 years.`,`Warn against **stem cells, chelation** and other unproven cures.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`ADHD at 6 years or more: **methylphenidate** plus behavioural and classroom measures.`,`Methylphenidate IR **5 mg twice daily**, up by 5-10 mg weekly, max 60 mg/day.`,`Atomoxetine if stimulants fail or with tics or misuse risk; effect in 4-6 weeks.`,`Autism irritability: **risperidone**; it does not treat the core social deficit.`,`Sleep-onset insomnia: sleep hygiene, then **melatonin 1-3 mg**.`,`Breath-holding spells: reassure, and give **iron** even without anaemia.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`**RBSK** screens 0-18 years for the 4 Ds and refers to the DEIC.`,`Refer the same week for **any regression**.`,`Refer no words by 16 months, no phrases by 24 months, no walking by 18 months.`,`On stimulants, plot **height, weight, pulse and BP** every 3-6 months.`,`Help families get a **UDID disability card** under the RPwD Act 2016.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in development and behaviour`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Methylphenidate IR`,`5 mg twice daily, max 60 mg/day`,`Long term with review`,`Poor appetite, insomnia, growth slowing; Schedule X`],[`Methylphenidate ER`,`18 mg each morning, max 54 mg`,`Long term with review`,`Swallow whole; not in structural heart disease`],[`Atomoxetine`,`0.5 mg/kg/day for 7 days, then 1.2 mg/kg/day`,`Effect in 4-6 weeks`,`Nausea; suicidal thoughts warning`],[`Clonidine`,`0.05 mg at bedtime, up to 0.1-0.3 mg/day`,`As needed`,`Sedation; never stop suddenly`],[`Risperidone`,`0.25-0.5 mg/day; range 0.5-3 mg/day`,`Review after 6 months stable`,`Weight gain, raised prolactin, EPS`],[`Aripiprazole`,`2 mg daily, up to 5-15 mg/day`,`As for risperidone`,`Akathisia; less weight gain`],[`Melatonin`,`1-3 mg (max 5-6 mg) at night`,`After sleep hygiene`,`Morning drowsiness`],[`Iron (breath-holding)`,`5-6 mg/kg/day elemental`,`3-4 months`,`Black stools; not with milk`]]},{heading:`Key milestones`,columns:[`Age`,`Gross motor`,`Fine motor`,`Language`,`Social`],rows:[[`3 months`,`Neck holding`,`Hands open`,`Cooing`,`Social smile`],[`6 months`,`Sits with support`,`Transfers objects`,`Monosyllables`,`Stranger anxiety`],[`9 months`,`Sits alone, crawls`,`Immature pincer`,`Bisyllables`,`Waves bye-bye`],[`12 months`,`Walks`,`Mature pincer`,`1-2 words`,`Pat-a-cake`],[`18 months`,`Runs stiffly`,`Tower of 3-4`,`10 or more words`,`Points to body parts`],[`2 years`,`Stairs two feet per step`,`Tower of 6-7`,`2-word phrases`,`Parallel play`],[`3 years`,`Tricycle`,`Copies circle`,`3-word sentences`,`Knows age and sex`]]},{heading:`Refer if (limit ages)`,columns:[`Domain`,`Red flag`],rows:[[`Gross motor`,`No head control 4 months; no sitting 9 months; no walking 18 months`],[`Fine motor`,`Fisting after 3 months; hand preference before 18 months`],[`Language`,`No babble 12 months; no words 16 months; no phrases 24 months`],[`Social`,`No smile 3 months; no pointing 12 months`],[`Any domain`,`Any regression at any age`]]}],remember:[`Sequence constant, rate variable; limit ages define red flags.`,`GDD under 5 years; intellectual disability after 5.`,`Isolated speech delay: test hearing first.`,`Hand preference before 18 months is abnormal.`,`ADHD: 6 symptoms, 6 months, before 12, two settings.`,`Risperidone treats irritability, not the core of autism.`,`A falling good student: depression, abuse, drugs, bullying or absences.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - Development, autism, ADHD and scholastic problems`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Development = skills in **gross motor, fine motor, language, personal-social** - **sequence constant, rate variable**; **GDD** = delay in 2 or more domains under 5 years; regression = loss of acquired skills.`],[`Relevant classification`,`DSM-5-TR neurodevelopmental disorders: **ID, communication disorders, ASD (levels 1-3), ADHD (combined, inattentive, hyperactive-impulsive), specific learning disorder, motor disorders**; DQ under 70 significant.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Prenatal - Down, **fragile X**, TORCH, alcohol, **congenital hypothyroidism**; perinatal - **HIE, prematurity, kernicterus, hypoglycaemia**; postnatal - meningitis, SAM, iron deficiency, lead, deprivation; ASD and ADHD highly heritable.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Synaptogenesis and myelination in first 1000 days -> pruning; **ASD - weak long-range connectivity, poor joint attention**; **ADHD - fronto-striatal dopamine/noradrenaline hypofunction, cortical maturation delay**; dyslexia - phonological deficit.`],[`Key history`,`Parental concern; milestones by domain; **any loss of skills**; perinatal events; hearing and vision; ASD - eye contact, name response, **pointing, pretend play**, stereotypy; ADHD - before 12 years, **two settings**; school decline timing; staring spells, snoring; family and psychosocial history.`],[`Key examination`,`Weight, height, **HC** (micro/macrocephaly); play-based assessment; dysmorphism, **neurocutaneous markers**; tone, reflexes, **hand preference before 18 months**; Gowers sign in boys; vision and hearing; social interaction; reading and writing sample; baseline HR, BP before stimulants.`],[`Investigations`,`Screening at **9, 18, 30 months** (TDSC 0-2 y, DDST-II); **M-CHAT-R/F at 18 and 24 months** (0-2 low, 3-7 follow-up, 8+ refer); INDT-ASD/ADHD; **hearing test for every speech delay**; Vanderbilt scales; TSH, Hb, ferritin, CK; microarray and fragile X; MRI/EEG only if indicated.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**ASD**: all 3 social-communication deficits + 2 of 4 restricted-repetitive; **ADHD**: 6 symptoms (5 from 17 y), 6 months, before 12, two settings; **SLD**: persists 6 months despite targeted help, normal IQ.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Early intervention via DEIC (RBSK)**; ASD - ABA, ESDM, parent-mediated therapy 15-25 h/week, speech and OT; **ADHD under 6 - parent training first**; SLD - remedial phonics, exam accommodations; nurturing care, no screens under 2.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`ADHD 6 years+: **methylphenidate IR 5 mg BD, up 5-10 mg/week, max 60 mg/day** (OROS 18-54 mg); **atomoxetine 0.5 then 1.2 mg/kg/day**; clonidine; ASD irritability - **risperidone 0.25-0.5 mg/day** or aripiprazole; melatonin 1-3 mg for sleep.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`TDSC chart, M-CHAT-R/F, DASII, Vineland, Binet-Kamat, NIMHANS SLD battery, ISAA; disability certificate and **UDID** under RPwD Act 2016.`],[`Special groups`,`Preterm - **corrected age to 2 years**; breath-holding spells - reassure, **iron 5-6 mg/kg/day** even without anaemia, ECG if pallid with syncope; absence epilepsy as school failure - ethosuximide or valproate.`],[`Complications`,`Stimulants - **appetite and growth slowing**, insomnia, raised HR/BP; atomoxetine suicidality; risperidone weight gain, prolactin; families exploited by unproven cures (stem cells, chelation).`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Any regression**; no babble, pointing or name response by 12 months; no words by 16, no phrases by 24 months; not sitting 9 or walking 18 months; hand preference before 18 months; HC abnormal; staring spells; sudden school decline with withdrawal.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Development every 3 months in infancy, 6-monthly later; on stimulants monthly then 3-6 monthly with **plotted height, weight, pulse, BP**; antipsychotics - weight, glucose, lipids, prolactin at 3 months.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**RBSK 4 Ds** screening (Anganwadi twice a year, schools once), DEIC; newborn TSH screening; nurturing care; iodine; iron; RPwD Act benefits, Niramaya insurance.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - AAP ADHD 2019, AAP autism 2020, NICE NG87 and DSM-5-TR remain current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Isolated speech delay - test hearing first; GDD under 5, ID after 5; risperidone treats irritability, not core autism; recent decline in a good student = depression, abuse, drugs, bullying until proved otherwise.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Suspected developmental delay - OPD pathway`,steps:[{label:`Any regression?`,detail:`Loss of skills - same-week neurology referral (Rett, LKS, epileptic encephalopathy)`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Screen by domain`,detail:`TDSC or DDST-II at 9, 18, 30 months; M-CHAT-R/F at 18 and 24 months`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Hearing and vision first`,detail:`OAE/BERA for any speech delay; fix and follow, Snellen after 3-4 years`},{label:`Look for a cause`,detail:`HC, dysmorphism, neurocutaneous signs, TSH, Hb, CK in boys, microarray and fragile X`},{label:`Refer to DEIC now`,detail:`Do not wait for a label - early intervention plus parent training`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Autism versus ADHD versus specific learning disorder`,columns:[`Feature`,`Autism`,`ADHD`,`SLD`],rows:[[`Core problem`,`Social communication + restricted, repetitive behaviour`,`Inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity`,`Reading, writing or maths despite normal IQ`],[`Screen`,`M-CHAT-R/F, INDT-ASD`,`Vanderbilt parent and teacher, INDT-ADHD`,`NIMHANS SLD battery`],[`First treatment`,`Early behavioural intervention, speech, OT`,`Parent training under 6; methylphenidate + behavioural from 6`,`Remedial phonics and accommodations`],[`Drug role`,`Risperidone/aripiprazole for irritability only`,`Methylphenidate, atomoxetine, clonidine`,`None`]]}]},"pediatrics-rheumatic-fever":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Acute rheumatic fever** follows group A streptococcal sore throat after **2-4 weeks**.`,`**Rheumatic heart disease** is the permanent valve damage it leaves.`,`Peak age is **5-15 years**; India has the largest number of RHD cases.`,`Only 0.3-3% of untreated strep throats cause ARF; recurrence risk is up to 50%.`,`**Juvenile mitral stenosis** before 20 years is typical of the Indian subcontinent.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**2015 revised Jones criteria**: GAS evidence plus 2 major, or 1 major and 2 minor.`,`A **recurrent attack** can also be diagnosed with 3 minor criteria.`,`India is **moderate-high risk**: monoarthritis and polyarthralgia count as major.`,`Minor criteria in India: **fever 38 C or more, ESR 30 or more**, long PR.`,`**Subclinical carditis** on echo counts as major; echo every suspected case.`,`**Chorea and indolent carditis** need no proof of recent streptococcal infection.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Streptococcal throat infection triggers antibodies and T cells.`,`2. **Molecular mimicry**: M protein resembles cardiac myosin and valve proteins.`,`3. The attack is a **pancarditis**, but valvulitis decides the outcome.`,`4. The mitral valve is affected most (65-70%), then mitral plus aortic.`,`5. Acute attacks cause regurgitation; **repeated attacks cause stenosis**.`,`6. Chorea comes from antibodies against **basal ganglia** neurons.`,`7. Removing streptococci with penicillin **prevents further valve scarring**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about a sore throat 1-5 weeks earlier; many children do not remember one.`,`Arthritis is in large joints, **migratory**, very painful, and responds to aspirin in 48 hours.`,`Ask whether **NSAIDs were already given**; they mask migration.`,`Ask about breathlessness, chest pain and swelling.`,`Chorea: new clumsiness, **worsening handwriting**, emotional lability, mostly in girls.`,`Ask about previous attacks and **missed penicillin injections**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Sleeping tachycardia** out of proportion to fever suggests carditis.`,`Listen for **apical pansystolic MR murmur** and early diastolic AR murmur.`,`The **Carey Coombs** mid-diastolic murmur shows acute mitral valvulitis.`,`Chorea signs: **milkmaid grip**, pronator sign, darting tongue.`,`Look for **erythema marginatum** on the trunk and nodules over extensor surfaces.`,`In chronic RHD look for loud S1, opening snap, AF and endocarditis signs.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**ASO titre** over about 320 Todd units, or a rising titre, proves recent GAS.`,`Add **anti-DNase B**, which stays high longer.`,`ESR and CRP are minor criteria and guide tapering.`,`ECG for a prolonged PR interval.`,`**Echocardiography with Doppler** for every suspected ARF.`,`ASO proves past infection, not current activity.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Eradicate GAS: **benzathine penicillin 6 lakh units (under 27 kg) or 12 lakh units** IM once.`,`Oral alternative: amoxicillin 50 mg/kg once daily for 10 days.`,`Withhold NSAIDs until the diagnosis is clear; give paracetamol meanwhile.`,`Arthritis and mild carditis: **aspirin 50-60 mg/kg/day** for 2 weeks, then taper.`,`Naproxen 10-20 mg/kg/day is a better-tolerated alternative.`,`Bed rest with carditis until symptoms and CRP settle.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Moderate or severe carditis: **prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg/day** for 2-3 weeks, then taper.`,`Overlap aspirin during steroid taper to prevent **rebound**.`,`Heart failure: furosemide and enalapril; digoxin only cautiously.`,`Chorea: **valproate or carbamazepine**; haloperidol for severe cases.`,`Secondary prophylaxis: **BPG 12 lakh units every 3 weeks** (6 lakh under 27 kg).`,`On penicillin prophylaxis, use **azithromycin** for IE prophylaxis.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`RHD is the **commonest heart disease in pregnancy** in India.`,`Mitral stenosis tolerates pregnancy poorly; continue BPG, use beta-blockers.`,`Severe MS with pliable valve: **balloon mitral valvotomy**.`,`AF with mitral stenosis needs **warfarin to INR 2-3**, not DOACs.`,`Warfarin in the first trimester causes embryopathy; plan before conception.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`Primary prevention: treat strep sore throat with penicillin **within 9 days**.`,`Secondary prophylaxis is the most cost-effective step in the RHD pathway.`,`Keep a **prophylaxis register** and recall defaulters; target over 80% of injections.`,`Primordial prevention: less crowding, better housing and nutrition.`,`RBSK school screening finds murmurs and chorea.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Rheumatic fever drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Benzathine penicillin G`,`6 lakh U (under 27 kg) or 12 lakh U deep IM`,`Once, then every 3-4 weeks for years`,`Pain; rare anaphylaxis; observe 30 min`],[`Amoxicillin`,`50 mg/kg oral once daily, max 1 g`,`10 days`,`Rash, diarrhoea`],[`Azithromycin`,`12 mg/kg oral once daily, max 500 mg`,`5 days`,`GI upset, QT`],[`Aspirin`,`50-60 mg/kg/day in 4 doses (up to 80-100)`,`2 weeks, taper over 4-6 weeks`,`Tinnitus, gastritis, Reye syndrome`],[`Naproxen`,`10-20 mg/kg/day in 2 doses, max 1 g`,`2-4 weeks`,`Dyspepsia`],[`Prednisolone`,`1-2 mg/kg/day, max 60 mg`,`2-3 weeks, then taper`,`Hyperglycaemia, hypertension; overlap aspirin`],[`Sodium valproate`,`15-20 mg/kg/day in 2-3 doses`,`Until chorea settles`,`Hepatotoxic, teratogenic`],[`Furosemide`,`1-2 mg/kg/dose once or twice daily`,`During heart failure`,`Hypokalaemia`]]},{heading:`Jones criteria 2015 (India: moderate-high risk)`,columns:[`Type`,`Criteria`],rows:[[`Major`,`Carditis (clinical or echo), arthritis or polyarthralgia, chorea, erythema marginatum, nodules`],[`Minor`,`Monoarthralgia, fever 38 C or more, ESR 30 or CRP 3 mg/dL, long PR`],[`Initial attack`,`GAS evidence plus 2 major, or 1 major and 2 minor`],[`Recurrent attack`,`As above, or 3 minor`],[`No GAS proof needed`,`Chorea and indolent carditis`]]},{heading:`Secondary prophylaxis duration (whichever is longer)`,columns:[`Category`,`Duration`],rows:[[`ARF without carditis`,`5 years after last attack or to age 21`],[`Carditis, no residual disease`,`10 years or to age 21`],[`Carditis with residual RHD`,`10 years or to age 40; often lifelong`],[`After valve surgery or PTMC`,`Lifelong`]]}],remember:[`ARF licks the joints and bites the heart.`,`India is moderate-high risk: monoarthritis is major.`,`Echo every suspected ARF; subclinical carditis counts.`,`Carditis means valvulitis.`,`No response to aspirin in 48 hours: rethink the diagnosis.`,`BPG every 3 weeks: 5/21, 10/21, 10/40 or lifelong.`,`On penicillin prophylaxis, use azithromycin for IE cover.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - Rheumatic fever and RHD`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Autoimmune multisystem illness **2-4 weeks after group A strep pharyngitis**; **RHD** is the chronic valve damage - the only permanent sequel.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Revised Jones 2015 - India moderate-high risk**: initial attack = GAS evidence + **2 major or 1 major + 2 minor**; recurrence may also be **3 minor**; major includes **monoarthritis, polyarthralgia, subclinical carditis**; fever 38 C, **ESR 30**, CRP 3 mg/dL.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Untreated GAS pharyngitis (0.3-3% develop ARF), age **5-15**, poverty, crowding; recurrence up to 50% without prophylaxis; HLA-DR7/DR4.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`**Molecular mimicry** - M protein vs cardiac myosin, GlcNAc vs valve -> valvulitis (Aschoff bodies) -> MR acutely; repeated attacks -> **commissural fusion, fish-mouth MS**; chorea - antibodies to basal ganglia.`],[`Key history`,`Sore throat 1-5 weeks ago; **migratory large-joint arthritis**, NSAIDs already given; breathlessness; chorea (handwriting, clumsiness); rash and nodules; previous ARF and missed injections; penicillin allergy; household crowding.`],[`Key examination`,`Sleeping tachycardia; joints daily; **apical pansystolic MR, AR, Carey Coombs**; heart failure, rub; **milkmaid grip, pronator sign**; erythema marginatum (trunk); nodules (severe carditis); in RHD - MS signs, AF.`],[`Investigations`,`**ASO over 320-333 Todd units or rising**, anti-DNase B; ESR, CRP; **ECG (long PR)**; **echocardiography for every suspected ARF** (WHF 2023 criteria); CBC (exclude leukaemia); blood cultures if fever with valve lesion.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Jones 2015 as above; **chorea and indolent carditis need no GAS evidence**; carditis must include valvulitis.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Eradicate GAS: **benzathine penicillin 6 lakh U (under 27 kg) or 12 lakh U IM** or amoxicillin 50 mg/kg/day x 10 days; arthritis - **aspirin 50-60 mg/kg/day** (or naproxen 10-20 mg/kg/day) 2 weeks then taper; withhold NSAIDs until diagnosis clear.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Moderate-severe carditis: **prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg/day (max 60) 2-3 weeks then taper with aspirin overlap**; heart failure - furosemide, enalapril, cautious digoxin; chorea - **valproate 15-20 mg/kg/day** or carbamazepine, haloperidol if severe.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`BPG deep IM slowly over 2-3 min, room-temperature vial, lignocaine diluent, **observe 30 min with adrenaline ready**; PTMC for severe MS (area 1.5 cm2 or less, Wilkins 8 or less).`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy - continue BPG, beta-blocker for MS, **PTMC in 2nd trimester**, warfarin embryopathy (ESC 2025); AF with MS - **warfarin INR 2-3, not DOACs**; IE prophylaxis on penicillin - use **azithromycin 15 mg/kg**.`],[`Complications`,`RHD (MR, MS, AR), heart failure, AF and stroke, infective endocarditis, pulmonary hypertension, recurrence with missed prophylaxis.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Heart failure, pericarditis, new murmur or sleeping tachycardia, **arthritis not responding to aspirin in 48 h** (rethink), severe chorea, fever with valve lesion, AF, haemoptysis or syncope in MS.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Weekly during acute phase with CRP; RHD review 3-6 monthly, **echo every 1-2 years**; adherence target **over 80% of injections**; register and recall defaulters.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Primary**: treat GAS sore throat within 9 days; **secondary**: **BPG every 3 weeks in India** - duration **5 y/21, 10 y/21, 10 y/40 or lifelong** (whichever longer); primordial - housing, crowding; RBSK school screening.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - **WHO 2024** RF/RHD guideline, Jones 2015, **WHF 2023 echo criteria and ESC 2025 VHD/pregnancy** already reflected.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`ARF licks the joints and bites the heart; **echo every suspected case**; carditis means valvulitis; BPG every 3 weeks is the most cost-effective RHD intervention; juvenile MS is an Indian pattern.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Suspected ARF - confirm and treat`,steps:[{label:`Evidence of recent GAS?`,detail:`ASO/anti-DNase B or culture; not needed for chorea or indolent carditis`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Apply Jones 2015 (moderate-high risk)`,detail:`2 major or 1 major + 2 minor (3 minor for recurrence)`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Echo every case`,detail:`Subclinical carditis counts as major`},{label:`Eradicate and start prophylaxis`,detail:`BPG 6 lakh U (under 27 kg) or 12 lakh U IM - the same dose starts secondary prophylaxis`,tone:`good`},{label:`Anti-inflammatory by severity`,detail:`Aspirin or naproxen for arthritis/mild carditis; prednisolone for carditis with failure`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Secondary prophylaxis duration (whichever is longer)`,steps:[{label:`ARF without carditis`,detail:`5 years after last attack or to age 21`},{label:`Carditis, no residual disease`,detail:`10 years after last attack or to age 21`},{label:`Carditis with residual RHD`,detail:`10 years or to age 40, often lifelong`,tone:`warn`},{label:`After valve surgery or PTMC`,detail:`Lifelong in high-exposure settings; BPG 12 lakh U every 3 weeks`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"pediatrics-glomerular-disease-child":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Nephrotic syndrome**: nephrotic-range proteinuria, albumin under 3 g/dL, and oedema.`,`Nephrotic range is first-morning **UPCR 2 mg/mg or more** or dipstick 3+.`,`**Nephritic syndrome**: haematuria with **RBC casts**, oliguria, oedema and hypertension.`,`**80-90%** of nephrotic children aged 1-10 years have minimal change disease.`,`**PSGN** is the commonest acute nephritis in Indian children aged 5-12 years.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Nephrotic syndrome is classified by **steroid response**, which predicts prognosis.`,`**Frequent relapses**: 2 or more in 6 months, or 3 or more in 12 months.`,`**Steroid dependence**: 2 relapses on alternate-day steroids or within 14 days of stopping.`,`**Steroid resistance**: no remission after 4 weeks of prednisolone 2 mg/kg/day.`,`Onset under 1 year is usually **genetic** and steroid-resistant.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. In minimal change disease, podocyte foot processes flatten; light microscopy is normal.`,`2. Albumin leaks, oncotic pressure falls, and the kidney also retains sodium.`,`3. Loss of antithrombin and protein S causes **thrombosis**.`,`4. Loss of IgG and factor B causes **peritonitis** and other infections.`,`5. PSGN: streptococcal antigens form immune complexes that **lower C3**, with normal C4.`,`6. PSGN hypertension is **volume-driven**, from salt and water retention.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`**Puffy eyes on waking** need a urine dipstick, not an allergy label.`,`Frothy urine suggests proteinuria; **cola-coloured urine** suggests nephritis.`,`Ask about **sore throat 1-3 weeks** or skin sores and scabies 3-6 weeks earlier.`,`Ask about headache, blurred vision and fits (hypertensive encephalopathy).`,`In known nephrotics, check the **home urine diary** and steroid adherence.`,`Ask about TB contact and chickenpox exposure before steroids.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record **weight daily**, height centile and BP with the right cuff.`,`Nephrotic oedema: soft, pitting, with ascites and scrotal swelling.`,`Nephrotic child with **cold peripheries and tachycardia** is hypovolaemic.`,`Examine the abdomen for **peritonitis**; steroids may mask it.`,`In PSGN look for crackles, raised JVP and **scabies or impetigo**.`,`Look for steroid toxicity: cushingoid face, cataract, poor growth.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Urine dipstick and **first-morning UPCR**; microscopy for RBC casts.`,`Serum albumin, cholesterol, creatinine and electrolytes.`,`**C3**: normal in minimal change; low in PSGN, normal again **by 8 weeks**.`,`ASO after throat infection; **anti-DNase B after skin infection**.`,`**No biopsy** for a typical nephrotic child aged 1-12 years.`,`Biopsy if under 1 or over 12, low C3, steroid resistance or persistent hypertension.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`ISPN initial course: **prednisolone 2 mg/kg/day** (max 60 mg) for 6 weeks.`,`Then **1.5 mg/kg on alternate days** (max 40 mg) for 6 weeks, then stop without taper.`,`Relapse: 2 mg/kg daily until remission, then 1.5 mg/kg alternate days for 4 weeks.`,`No added salt during oedema; normal protein; no fluid restriction.`,`Give **PCV then PPSV23**, and annual influenza vaccine.`,`PSGN: restrict salt and fluid; **furosemide is the first antihypertensive**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Give daily prednisolone for 5-7 days with each cold to **prevent relapses**.`,`Frequent relapses: alternate-day prednisolone or **levamisole 2-2.5 mg/kg** alternate days.`,`Next: mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide, tacrolimus or **rituximab**.`,`Steroid resistance: biopsy, genetic tests, calcineurin inhibitor plus ACE inhibitor.`,`PSGN persistent hypertension: add **amlodipine**.`,`Penicillin in PSGN only eradicates the strain; **no long-term prophylaxis**.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Abdominal pain with cold feet means **hypovolaemia**: fluids or albumin, **never diuretics**.`,`Fever with abdominal tenderness: **peritonitis**; give IV ceftriaxone.`,`Headache or seizures: cerebral venous thrombosis, or hypertensive encephalopathy in PSGN.`,`PSGN pulmonary oedema: sit up, oxygen, IV furosemide.`,`Hyperkalaemia or refractory overload may need dialysis.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Start steroids in primary care only if age 1-12, normal BP and C3, no gross haematuria.`,`Refer steroid resistance, frequent relapses, low C3 or raised creatinine.`,`PSGN: diuresis in 1 week, C3 normal by 8 weeks, haematuria may last 1-2 years.`,`Over **95% of PSGN** recovers fully.`,`Treat **scabies in the whole household** to prevent PSGN.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Glomerular disease drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Prednisolone (initial)`,`2 mg/kg/day, then 1.5 mg/kg alternate days`,`6 weeks plus 6 weeks`,`Cushingoid, hypertension, cataract, infection`],[`Levamisole`,`2-2.5 mg/kg alternate days`,`12-24 months`,`Neutropenia; WBC every 2-3 months`],[`Mycophenolate`,`1000-1200 mg/m2/day in 2 doses`,`12-24 months`,`Diarrhoea, leucopenia, teratogenic`],[`Cyclophosphamide`,`2 mg/kg/day oral`,`12 weeks`,`Leucopenia, cystitis, gonadal toxicity`],[`Tacrolimus`,`0.1-0.2 mg/kg/day in 2 doses`,`Trough 3-7 ng/mL`,`Nephrotoxicity, hyperglycaemia`],[`Rituximab`,`375 mg/m2 IV, 1-2 doses`,`As needed`,`Infusion reactions, infection`],[`Furosemide`,`1-2 mg/kg/dose (up to 4 in PSGN oliguria)`,`While oedematous`,`Never if hypovolaemic`],[`Amlodipine`,`0.1-0.2 mg/kg/day, max 5-10 mg`,`2-4 weeks in PSGN`,`Ankle oedema, flushing`],[`20% albumin`,`0.5-1 g/kg IV over 4 hours`,`Refractory oedema or shock`,`Pulmonary oedema`],[`Ceftriaxone (peritonitis)`,`100 mg/kg/day IV, max 2 g`,`7-10 days`,`After blood and ascitic culture`]]},{heading:`Nephrotic or nephritic?`,columns:[`Feature`,`Nephrotic (minimal change)`,`Nephritic (PSGN)`],rows:[[`Age`,`2-8 years`,`5-12 years`],[`Onset`,`Insidious, often after a cold`,`1-3 weeks after throat, 3-6 after skin`],[`Oedema`,`Marked, with ascites`,`Mild, periorbital`],[`Urine`,`Frothy, 3-4+ protein`,`Cola-coloured, RBC casts`],[`Blood pressure`,`Usually normal`,`Raised in 60-80%`],[`C3`,`Normal`,`Low, normal by 8 weeks`],[`Treatment`,`Prednisolone`,`Salt and fluid restriction, furosemide`]]},{heading:`Response definitions`,columns:[`Term`,`Definition`],rows:[[`Remission`,`Nil or trace on 3 consecutive mornings`],[`Relapse`,`3+ or more on 3 consecutive days`],[`Frequent relapses`,`2 in 6 months or 3 in 12 months`],[`Steroid dependence`,`2 relapses on alternate-day steroids or within 14 days`],[`Steroid resistance`,`No remission after 4 weeks of 2 mg/kg/day`]]}],remember:[`Nephrotic is about protein; nephritic is about blood and pressure.`,`ISPN: 6 weeks daily plus 6 weeks alternate day; no taper.`,`Steroid response predicts prognosis better than biopsy.`,`Nephrotic child with abdominal pain and cold feet: no diuretics.`,`PSGN after impetigo may have normal ASO; ask for anti-DNase B.`,`Low C3 that normalises by 8 weeks is PSGN.`,`PSGN hypertension is volume-driven: furosemide first.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - Nephrotic syndrome and PSGN in children`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Nephrotic**: first-morning **UPCR 2 mg/mg or more** (dipstick 3+), **albumin under 3 g/dL**, oedema. **Nephritic (PSGN)**: haematuria with RBC casts, oliguria, oedema, hypertension, **1-3 weeks after sore throat or 3-6 weeks after impetigo/scabies**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`By steroid response (ISPN 2021/IPNA 2023): **remission, relapse, frequent relapses (2 in 6 months or 3 in 12), steroid dependence (2 relapses on alternate-day or within 14 days of stopping), steroid resistance (no remission after 4 weeks of 2 mg/kg)**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Age 1-10 years - **minimal change in 80-90%**; under 1 year - genetic (NPHS1/2) or infective; PSGN - nephritogenic GAS (**skin infection now as common as throat in India**), age 5-12.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`MCD - T-cell factor / **anti-nephrin antibodies** -> podocyte effacement -> selective proteinuria -> underfill + ENaC overfill; loss of AT III, IgG -> **thrombosis, peritonitis**; PSGN - immune complexes, **alternative pathway -> low C3, normal C4** -> volume-driven hypertension.`],[`Key history`,`Periorbital puffiness on waking, frothy vs **cola urine**, urine volume; recent sore throat or skin sores in family; headache, vision, fits (hypertensive encephalopathy); breathlessness; home urine diary and relapses; abdominal pain, fever; TB contact; steroid side effects.`],[`Key examination`,`Daily weight, height centile, **BP with correct cuff**; pitting oedema, ascites, effusion; **hypovolaemia signs despite oedema** (cold peripheries, tachycardia); peritonitis; circulatory congestion in PSGN; scabies and impetigo; SLE/HSP features; steroid toxicity incl. cataract.`],[`Investigations`,`Dipstick and first-morning UPCR; urine microscopy (RBC casts); albumin, cholesterol, creatinine, electrolytes; **C3 (low in PSGN, normal by 8 weeks)**; ASO after throat, **anti-DNase B after skin**; CBC, Mantoux/CXR before steroids; biopsy only for atypical features.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Typical steroid-sensitive NS: **age 1-12, normal BP, normal C3, no gross haematuria, normal creatinine** - treat without biopsy; PSGN: nephritis + evidence of GAS + low C3 recovering by 8 weeks.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**ISPN initial: prednisolone 2 mg/kg/day (max 60) x 6 weeks, then 1.5 mg/kg (max 40) alternate days x 6 weeks, stop without taper**; relapse - 2 mg/kg daily to remission then 1.5 mg/kg alternate days 4 weeks; no added salt; PSGN - **salt and fluid restriction + furosemide 1-2 mg/kg**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Frequent relapse/dependence: long-term alternate-day prednisolone, **levamisole 2-2.5 mg/kg alternate days**, MMF, cyclophosphamide 2 mg/kg x 12 weeks, tacrolimus, **rituximab 375 mg/m2**; SRNS - biopsy, genetics, CNI + ACE inhibitor; PSGN persistent BP - amlodipine.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Home **dipstick or boiling test** diary; steroid card; **20% albumin 0.5-1 g/kg over 4 h with furosemide** for refractory oedema; dialysis for refractory overload, hyperkalaemia, uraemia.`],[`Special groups`,`Vaccines: **PCV then PPSV23**, annual influenza; live vaccines when prednisolone under 1 mg/kg/day or stopped 4 weeks; varicella exposure - **acyclovir 20 mg/kg QID x 7 days from day 7-10** or VZIG.`],[`Complications`,`Nephrotic - **hypovolaemia, peritonitis (ceftriaxone), thrombosis (CVST, renal vein)**, AKI, steroid toxicity; PSGN - **hypertensive encephalopathy (PRES)**, pulmonary oedema, hyperkalaemia, AKI.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Abdominal pain with cold feet (no diuretics), fever with abdominal tenderness, headache or seizures, oliguria over 24 h, K over 6 mmol/L, steroid resistance, onset under 1 or over 12 years, low C3 beyond 8 weeks.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Each visit: weight, height, BP, urine diary, eyes, steroid dose; PSGN - diuresis 1 week, creatinine normal 3-4 weeks, **C3 normal by 8 weeks**, proteinuria gone 6 months, microscopic haematuria up to 1-2 years.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Treat **scabies (permethrin 5%) and impetigo in the whole household**; daily prednisolone for 5-7 days during colds prevents relapse; no long-term penicillin for PSGN.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - **ISPN 2021, IPNA 2023 (SSNS) and IPNA 2020 (SRNS)** remain the operative paediatric guidance.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Nephrotic is about protein, nephritic about blood and pressure; steroid response predicts prognosis better than biopsy; **PSGN after impetigo may have normal ASO**; hypertension in PSGN is volume-driven - furosemide first.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Puffy child - nephrotic or nephritic`,steps:[{label:`Dipstick and microscopy`,detail:`3+ protein bland sediment vs RBC casts with 1-2+ protein`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Nephrotic - typical?`,detail:`Age 1-12, normal BP, C3 and creatinine, no gross haematuria - start ISPN course`,tone:`good`},{label:`Atypical nephrotic`,detail:`Under 1 or over 12, low C3, hypertension, haematuria, steroid resistance - nephrologist, biopsy`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Nephritic - check C3 and GAS`,detail:`Low C3, ASO (throat) or anti-DNase B (skin); salt and fluid restriction, furosemide`},{label:`Emergencies`,detail:`Hypertensive encephalopathy, pulmonary oedema, K over 6, anuria - admit, dialysis if refractory`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Steroid-sensitive nephrotic syndrome - escalation`,steps:[{label:`Initial episode`,detail:`Prednisolone 2 mg/kg/day x 6 weeks, then 1.5 mg/kg alternate days x 6 weeks; stop`},{label:`Infrequent relapse`,detail:`2 mg/kg daily to remission, then 1.5 mg/kg alternate days x 4 weeks`},{label:`Frequent relapse / dependence`,detail:`Alternate-day prednisolone 0.5-0.7 mg/kg or levamisole 2-2.5 mg/kg alternate days 12-24 months`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Steroid-sparing agents`,detail:`MMF 1000-1200 mg/m2/day, cyclophosphamide 2 mg/kg x 12 weeks, tacrolimus, rituximab 375 mg/m2`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Steroid resistant`,detail:`Biopsy, genetics, CNI + enalapril; nephrologist`,tone:`good`}]}]},"pediatrics-asthma-wheeze-child":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and classification`,points:[`**Asthma** is chronic airway inflammation with **variable airflow limitation** and variable symptoms.`,`**Wheeze** is musical, expiratory and intrathoracic; **stridor** is inspiratory, from the upper airway.`,`Asthma is the **commonest chronic childhood disease**; 1 in 3 wheeze before 3, most outgrow it.`,`Under 5: **episodic viral wheeze** (only with colds) versus **multiple-trigger wheeze** (likely asthma).`,`Treatment uses **GINA steps 1-5** in three age bands: 5 and under, 6-11, 12 and over.`,`Control asks day symptoms, night waking, reliever use, activity limit: none well, 1-2 partly, 3-4 uncontrolled.`,`Acute attacks are graded mild-moderate, severe or life-threatening.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Viruses, mites or smoke injure the epithelium, releasing alarmins (**TSLP, IL-33**).`,`2. **Th2 cytokines** drive IgE, eosinophils and mucus.`,`3. Mast cells release histamine and leukotrienes, causing **bronchospasm** within minutes.`,`4. A late inflammatory phase follows hours later; steroids, not salbutamol, treat it.`,`5. Small child airways narrow easily, because resistance rises steeply as radius falls.`,`6. **SABA alone** relieves symptoms while inflammation grows, raising severe-attack risk.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask what the parent means by wheeze; many mean rattly breathing.`,`Wheeze with **running, laughing, cold air or at night** points to asthma.`,`**Sudden choking** then one-sided wheeze suggests foreign body.`,`Ask about **TB contact** and weight loss; hilar nodes can cause wheeze.`,`Ask about eczema, allergic rhinitis and family history of asthma.`,`Ask about smoke, biomass fuel, mosquito coils and incense at home.`,`Ask about past ICU admission and oral steroid courses.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record respiratory rate, heart rate, **SpO2** and ability to talk or feed.`,`Asthma gives **bilateral polyphonic expiratory wheeze**.`,`**Unilateral or monophonic wheeze** suggests foreign body or compression.`,`**Clubbing is not a feature of asthma**; think CF or bronchiectasis.`,`Look for allergic shiners, nasal crease and eczema.`,`Watch the child use their own inhaler.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Under 5: diagnosis is clinical plus a **3-month trial of daily low-dose ICS**.`,`From 6 years: spirometry with **FEV1 rise over 12%** after salbutamol.`,`PEF diary: average daily variability **over 13%** supports asthma.`,`FeNO 35 ppb or more supports eosinophilic inflammation.`,`Chest X-ray only for atypical features such as foreign body or TB.`,`No routine IgE, eosinophil counts or antibiotics.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Under 5, step 2: **daily low-dose ICS** for at least 3 months, by spacer.`,`6-11 years: **as-needed budesonide-formoterol** or ICS taken with each SABA.`,`From 6 years (GINA 2026), **SABA-only treatment is not recommended**.`,`12 and over: as-needed budesonide-formoterol 200/6, then MART.`,`**MDI with spacer** for all ages; a mask under 4 years.`,`Every child gets a **written action plan**, with a copy at school.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Before stepping up, check **technique, adherence, triggers and diagnosis**.`,`Step 3: low-dose ICS-LABA or very-low-dose MART (6-11 years).`,`Step down after **3 months of good control**.`,`Step 5 is specialist: tiotropium or biologics.`,`Montelukast is an alternative under 5; warn about **neuropsychiatric effects**.`,`Stop oral salbutamol syrups, theophylline and cough syrups.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Oxygen only if **SpO2 under 92%**; upper limit 95% at 6-11 years.`,`Acute: **salbutamol by spacer every 20 minutes** for the first hour.`,`Give **prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg** for 3-5 days, no taper.`,`Add ipratropium if moderate-severe; **IV magnesium** if poor response.`,`**Silent chest**, cyanosis or drowsiness means a life-threatening attack.`,`A normal or rising PaCO2 in a tiring child means impending failure.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Bronchiolitis** in infants does not respond to salbutamol or steroids.`,`Bronchiolitis care is oxygen, gentle suction, and small frequent or tube feeds.`,`Treat allergic rhinitis with nasal steroid; it coexists in up to 80%.`,`Give annual influenza vaccine in persistent asthma.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Discharge when reliever is needed 3-4 hourly or less; **review in 2-7 days**.`,`Review 1-3 months after starting, then every 3-12 months.`,`Refer neonatal onset, failure to thrive, clubbing or focal signs.`,`Refer uncontrolled asthma at step 3-4 despite good technique.`,`Suspected foreign body needs **rigid bronchoscopy**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Asthma drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Salbutamol`,`2-4 puffs (100 mcg) by spacer; acute 2-6 (under 5) or 4-10 puffs`,`As needed; acute every 20 min for 1 h`,`Tremor, tachycardia, low potassium`],[`Low-dose ICS`,`Budesonide neb 500 mcg/day (under 5); 100-200 mcg/day (6-11)`,`Daily, at least 3 months`,`Thrush, hoarseness, slight growth slowing`],[`Budesonide-formoterol`,`100/6 (6-11) or 200/6 (12+) as needed or MART`,`Long term`,`Seek care if over 8 (6-11) or 12 (12+) puffs a day`],[`Montelukast`,`4 mg (under 6), 5 mg (6-14), 10 mg (15+) evening`,`Long term`,`Boxed warning: nightmares, depression`],[`Prednisolone`,`1-2 mg/kg once daily (max 20/30/40 mg by age)`,`3-5 days, no taper`,`Vomiting, mood change`],[`Ipratropium`,`250 mcg neb or 80-160 mcg by spacer`,`Every 20 min for 1 hour`,`Dry mouth`],[`Magnesium sulphate`,`40-50 mg/kg IV, max 2 g, over 20 min`,`Single dose`,`Flushing, hypotension`],[`Mometasone nasal`,`50 mcg per nostril (2-11 y); 100 mcg (12+)`,`Daily for weeks`,`Epistaxis`]]},{heading:`Acute attack severity (6-11 years)`,columns:[`Grade`,`Talks`,`Signs`,`SpO2`],rows:[[`Mild-moderate`,`Phrases`,`HR 100-120, not agitated`,`90-95%`],[`Severe`,`Words`,`Agitated, RR over 30, HR over 120`,`Under 90%`],[`Life-threatening`,`Unable`,`Drowsy, silent chest, cyanosis`,`Low despite oxygen`]]},{heading:`Inhaler device by age`,columns:[`Device`,`Best for`,`Key step`],rows:[[`MDI + spacer with mask`,`0-3 years`,`Seal mask; 1 puff, 5-6 tidal breaths`],[`MDI + spacer with mouthpiece`,`4 years and over`,`1 puff, slow breath held 10 s`],[`500 mL bottle spacer`,`Low-resource settings`,`MDI fitted into base hole`],[`Dry powder inhaler`,`6 years and over, stable`,`Fast deep breath; not in severe attack`],[`Nebuliser`,`Severe hypoxic attack`,`Oxygen-driven; not better than spacer`]]}],remember:[`All that wheezes is not asthma: think TB nodes and foreign body.`,`Bronchiolitis: no salbutamol, no steroids.`,`MDI with spacer equals a nebuliser.`,`From 6 years, an ICS-containing reliever; no SABA alone.`,`Check technique, adherence, triggers, diagnosis before stepping up.`,`Prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg for 3-5 days, no taper.`,`Reliever opens the airway; preventer heals it.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`GINA 2026: as-needed low-dose budesonide-formoterol (AIR) added as a Steps 1-2 option for children 6-11 (CARE trial); SABA-only treatment no longer recommended from 6 years`,`GINA 2026: oxygen only if SpO2 under 92%, titrated to an upper limit of 95% at 6-11 years (92% or more at 5 and under) - replaces the 94-98% target`,`GINA 2026: 'maximum 8/12 inhalations a day' of budesonide-formoterol replaced by a seek-care prompt - more than 8 (6-11 years) or 12 (12+) in 24 hours`,`GINA 2026: ICS-formoterol an alternative to SABA for mild exacerbations at 6 years and over`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Wheeze and asthma in children (GINA 2026)`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Asthma** = chronic airway inflammation with **variable respiratory symptoms plus variable expiratory airflow limitation**; wheeze = expiratory, intrathoracic; stridor = inspiratory, extrathoracic.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Under 5: **episodic viral vs multiple-trigger wheeze**; **GINA steps 1-5 by age band (5 and under, 6-11, 12+)**; control by 4 questions over 4 weeks; attack severity mild-moderate, severe, life-threatening.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Atopy, family history, eczema, rhinovirus/RSV, **biomass smoke, second-hand smoke, mosquito coils**, house dust mite, prematurity; mAPI predicts persistence; India - about 3% current wheeze, mostly undiagnosed.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Epithelial alarmins -> **Th2: IL-4 (IgE), IL-5 (eosinophils), IL-13** -> early-phase spasm (SABA) + late-phase inflammation (ICS); small airways and **Poiseuille fourth-power** law explain toddler wheeze; bronchiolitis = debris, not smooth muscle.`],[`Key history`,`What parent means by wheeze; **only with colds or also between** (exercise, laughing, night); onset age; **sudden choking** (foreign body); TB contact; atopy; home smoke; ED visits, **ICU ever, OCS courses**; current medicines (syrups); inhaler technique.`],[`Key examination`,`RR, HR, **SpO2**, speech/feeding; indrawing; **bilateral polyphonic wheeze** (unilateral/monophonic - FB, node); clubbing points away from asthma; Harrison sulcus; nose and skin atopy; growth; PEF/spirometry from 6 years; observe technique.`],[`Investigations`,`Under 5 - **clinical + ICS trial 2-3 months**; 6+ - **spirometry FEV1 rise over 12%** after bronchodilator; PEF diurnal variability over 13%; FeNO 35 ppb or more supportive; **CXR only for atypical features**; no routine IgE or antibiotics.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Recurrent multiple-trigger symptoms + documented variability (or response to ICS trial under 5); exclude FB, TB nodes, CF, cardiac failure.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Under 5: Step 1 SABA by spacer; **Step 2 daily low-dose ICS 3 months** (budesonide neb 500 mcg, FP 50 mcg, BDP 100 mcg). **6-11: Steps 1-2 as-needed low-dose budesonide-formoterol (AIR, GINA 2026)** or ICS with each SABA or daily low-dose ICS; **no SABA-only**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`6-11 Step 3: low-dose ICS-LABA or **budesonide-formoterol 100/6 MART** (seek care if over 8 inhalations/24 h); Step 4 medium dose, refer; **12+ Track 1 budesonide-formoterol 200/6** (seek care if over 12/24 h); Step 5 tiotropium, omalizumab, mepolizumab, dupilumab.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**0-3 y MDI + spacer + mask, 4-5 y spacer + mouthpiece, 6+ spacer, DPI**; one puff at a time, 5-6 tidal breaths; **500 mL bottle spacer**; wash spacer monthly, air-dry; check technique every visit.`],[`Special groups`,`Acute attack: **salbutamol 2-6 puffs (under 5) or 4-10 puffs (6-11) every 20 min x 1 h**; mild attack 6+ ICS-formoterol alternative; **oxygen only if SpO2 under 92% (upper limit 95% at 6-11)**; prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg 3-5 days (max 20/30/40 mg); ipratropium; **IV MgSO4 40-50 mg/kg**.`],[`Complications`,`Life-threatening attack, respiratory failure (**rising PaCO2**), pneumothorax, remodelling and reduced lung growth, ICS thrush and small growth effect, montelukast neuropsychiatric effects.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Silent chest, cyanosis, drowsiness; **SpO2 under 92%**; no response to 3 doses in first hour; sudden choking with unilateral wheeze; neonatal onset, FTT, clubbing; TB contact with localised wheeze; previous ICU.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review 1-3 months after starting, then 3-12 monthly: control, exacerbations, technique, adherence, growth; step down after 3 months control; **before stepping up check technique, adherence, triggers, diagnosis**; discharge review in 2-7 days.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Written action plan** with school copy; smoke-free home, clean fuel (Ujjwala), dust-mite measures; annual influenza vaccine (trivalent) and PCV; treat allergic rhinitis; RBSK school detection.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**GINA 2026**: AIR for 6-11 at Steps 1-2, no SABA-only from 6 years, ICS-formoterol for mild attacks, **oxygen thresholds lowered (start under 92%, upper limit 95%)**, seek-care prompts replace daily maximums.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`All that wheezes is not asthma; bronchiolitis does not respond to salbutamol or steroids; **MDI with spacer equals nebuliser**; prednisolone without taper; montelukast boxed warning.`]]},flows:[{kind:`ladder`,heading:`GINA 2026 - controller steps for children 6-11 years`,steps:[{label:`Steps 1-2: anti-inflammatory reliever`,detail:`As-needed low-dose budesonide-formoterol (AIR), or ICS with each SABA, or daily low-dose ICS; no SABA-only`},{label:`Step 3`,detail:`Low-dose ICS-LABA, medium-dose ICS, or budesonide-formoterol 100/6 MART 1 daily + as needed`},{label:`Step 4`,detail:`Medium-dose ICS-LABA or MART 100/6 twice daily; refer`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Step 5 - specialist`,detail:`Tiotropium 2.5 mcg x 2; omalizumab, mepolizumab 40 mg, dupilumab`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Step down after 3 months control`,detail:`Reduce ICS 25-50%; seek care if over 8 budesonide-formoterol inhalations in 24 h`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Acute asthma in a child - first hour`,steps:[{label:`Grade severity`,detail:`Talks in words, RR over 30, HR over 120, SpO2 under 90%, PEF 50% or less = severe; silent chest, drowsy = life-threatening`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Oxygen only if SpO2 under 92%`,detail:`Titrate to upper limit 95% at 6-11 years; 92% or more at 5 and under`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Salbutamol by spacer every 20 min`,detail:`2-6 puffs (5 and under), 4-10 puffs (6-11) for 1 hour; ICS-formoterol alternative in mild attack 6+`},{label:`Steroid in first hour`,detail:`Prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg (max 20/30/40 mg) 3-5 days; add ipratropium if moderate-severe`},{label:`Poor response`,detail:`IV MgSO4 40-50 mg/kg over 20 min; rising PaCO2 - PICU`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Discharge plan`,detail:`Reliever 3-4 hourly or less, ICS started or stepped up, action plan, review in 2-7 days`,tone:`good`}]}]},"pediatrics-stridor-croup":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Stridor** is a harsh, mainly inspiratory sound from a narrowed larynx or trachea.`,`Stridor is a sign, never a diagnosis.`,`**Croup** is the commonest cause of acute stridor, at 6 months to 3 years.`,`Croup is mostly caused by **parainfluenza virus type 1**.`,`**Laryngomalacia** is the commonest congenital cause.`,`**Loud stridor is not a measure of severity**; a quietening stridor can mean arrest.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Inspiratory: extrathoracic lesion such as laryngomalacia or epiglottitis.`,`**Biphasic**: fixed glottic or subglottic lesion such as croup or stenosis.`,`Expiratory: intrathoracic lesion such as tracheomalacia or bronchial foreign body.`,`Acute febrile: croup, epiglottitis, tracheitis, retropharyngeal abscess, diphtheria.`,`Acute afebrile: **foreign body**, spasmodic croup, anaphylaxis.`,`**Westley score**: 2 or less mild, 3-5 moderate, 6-11 severe, 12 or more impending failure.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. A child's airway is narrowest at the **cricoid**, a rigid ring.`,`2. Swelling there cannot expand outward, so the lumen narrows.`,`3. Resistance rises with the fourth power of the radius; 1 mm of oedema matters hugely.`,`4. Crying makes inspiratory pressure more negative, collapsing the airway further.`,`5. **Dexamethasone** reduces oedema within about 2 hours and lasts the illness.`,`6. **Adrenaline** shrinks mucosa in 10-30 minutes but wears off in under 2 hours.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Croup: coryza for 1-3 days, then **barking cough** and stridor at night.`,`Epiglottitis: hours of high fever, **drooling** and toxicity.`,`Foreign body: **sudden choking** while eating or playing.`,`Ask about neck pain and stiffness (retropharyngeal abscess).`,`Ask about **Hib and DPT immunisation**; think diphtheria if incomplete.`,`Congenital stridor: onset, cry, feeding, weight gain and position effect.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Look before you touch**; keep the child calm on the parent's lap.`,`Record SpO2; **under 92%**, cyanosis or drowsiness mean severe obstruction.`,`Check retractions, air entry and **stridor at rest**.`,`Epiglottitis 4 Ds: **drooling, dysphagia, dysphonia, distress**, with tripod posture.`,`**Never use a tongue depressor** or lie the child down if epiglottitis is possible.`,`Grey adherent membrane with bull neck suggests **diphtheria**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Croup is a clinical diagnosis**; no neck X-ray is needed.`,`Pulse oximetry is the one essential bedside test.`,`Lateral neck X-ray only if stable: **thumb sign** in epiglottitis.`,`Foreign body: inspiratory and expiratory film; **a normal film does not exclude it**.`,`Rigid bronchoscopy confirms and removes a foreign body.`,`Flexible laryngoscopy for congenital or chronic stridor.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Every child with croup, even mild, gets dexamethasone.**`,`Dose: **0.15-0.6 mg/kg orally once**, max 16 mg; 0.15 mg/kg is enough for mild-moderate.`,`If vomiting, give it IM or IV, or nebulised budesonide 2 mg.`,`Moderate-severe: **nebulised adrenaline 1:1000, 0.5 mL/kg** (max 5 mL).`,`Oxygen if SpO2 under 92%; no mist, antibiotics or cough syrups.`,`Observe **2-4 hours after adrenaline** before discharge.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Admit if stridor at rest persists after adrenaline, or the child is under 6 months.`,`Epiglottitis: secure the airway **in theatre**, then ceftriaxone 100 mg/kg/day.`,`Hib contacts: **rifampicin 20 mg/kg daily for 4 days**.`,`Bacterial tracheitis: intubation, bronchoscopy, vancomycin plus ceftriaxone.`,`Diphtheria: **antitoxin on clinical suspicion**, plus penicillin or erythromycin for 14 days.`,`Subglottic haemangioma: propranolol 1-3 mg/kg/day.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Toxic child with croup not responding to adrenaline means **bacterial tracheitis**.`,`Quietening stridor with poor air entry means **impending arrest**.`,`Diphtheria causes **myocarditis in week 2** and neuropathy in week 3.`,`Choking then persistent cough or one-sided wheeze needs bronchoscopy.`,`Congenital stridor with poor growth or apnoea needs laryngoscopy.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`Infant choking: **5 back blows, then 5 chest thrusts** with the heel of one hand.`,`Child over 1 year: 5 back blows alternating with 5 abdominal thrusts.`,`**Never a blind finger sweep.**`,`No peanuts, whole nuts or chana under 4-5 years.`,`Hib in pentavalent and DPT boosters prevent epiglottitis and diphtheria.`,`Diphtheria is notifiable; give contacts erythromycin and a booster.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Stridor drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Dexamethasone`,`0.15-0.6 mg/kg oral (IM/IV if vomiting), max 16 mg`,`Single dose`,`Vomiting`],[`Budesonide nebulised`,`2 mg`,`Single dose`,`Alternative if oral fails`],[`L-adrenaline 1:1000`,`0.5 mL/kg nebulised, max 5 mL`,`Repeat every 15-20 min if severe`,`Tachycardia; rebound after 2 h`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`100 mg/kg/day IV, max 2 g`,`7-10 days (epiglottitis)`,`After airway secured`],[`Rifampicin`,`20 mg/kg once daily, max 600 mg`,`4 days (Hib contacts)`,`Orange secretions, interactions`],[`Diphtheria antitoxin`,`20,000-100,000 units IV by extent`,`Once`,`Anaphylaxis; test dose, adrenaline ready`],[`Penicillin G / erythromycin`,`100,000-150,000 U/kg/day IV, or 40-50 mg/kg/day`,`14 days`,`Allergy; GI upset`],[`Adrenaline IM (anaphylaxis)`,`0.01 mg/kg of 1 mg/mL, max 0.5 mg`,`Repeat every 5 min`,`Thigh injection`]]},{heading:`Acute stridor differential`,columns:[`Feature`,`Viral croup`,`Epiglottitis`,`Bacterial tracheitis`,`Foreign body`],rows:[[`Age`,`6 months-3 years`,`2-7 years`,`3-10 years`,`1-3 years`],[`Onset`,`Gradual after coryza`,`Hours`,`Croup that worsens`,`Sudden choking`],[`Fever`,`Low, not toxic`,`High, toxic`,`High, toxic`,`Absent`],[`Cough`,`Barking`,`Absent`,`Brassy`,`Paroxysmal`],[`Drooling`,`No`,`Yes, tripod`,`No`,`No`],[`X-ray`,`Steeple sign`,`Thumb sign`,`Ragged trachea`,`Expiratory hyperinflation`]]},{heading:`Westley croup score`,columns:[`Item`,`Points`],rows:[[`Stridor: with agitation / at rest`,`1 / 2`],[`Retractions: mild / moderate / severe`,`1 / 2 / 3`],[`Air entry: decreased / markedly decreased`,`1 / 2`],[`Cyanosis: with agitation / at rest`,`4 / 5`],[`Conscious level: disoriented`,`5`]]}],remember:[`Stridor is a sign: acute or chronic, febrile or not, which phase.`,`Every croup gets one dose of dexamethasone; 0.15 mg/kg is enough.`,`Adrenaline rebounds: observe 2-4 hours.`,`A quiet stridor can be more dangerous than a loud one.`,`Epiglottitis: airway in theatre, not the X-ray room.`,`A normal chest X-ray does not rule out a foreign body.`,`Diphtheria antitoxin on clinical suspicion.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`AHA 2025: infant choking - 5 back blows then 5 chest thrusts with the heel of one hand on the lower half of the sternum (replaces two-finger technique); child over 1 year - back blows alternating with abdominal thrusts`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Stridor, croup and upper airway obstruction`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Stridor** = harsh, mainly inspiratory sound from turbulent flow through narrowed **larynx or trachea**; croup = viral laryngotracheobronchitis with **barking cough, hoarse voice, stridor**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`By phase: **inspiratory** (supraglottic/glottic), **biphasic** (subglottic), **expiratory** (intrathoracic); by onset: acute febrile, acute afebrile, chronic/congenital; **Westley score - 2 or less mild, 3-5 moderate, 6-11 severe, 12+ impending failure**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Croup - **parainfluenza 1**, 6 months-3 years; epiglottitis (Hib, rare after vaccine); bacterial tracheitis (S. aureus); retropharyngeal abscess under 5; **diphtheria in under-immunised**; foreign body 1-3 years; laryngomalacia commonest congenital.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Narrowest at **cricoid** (non-expansile) -> 1 mm oedema raises resistance about 16-fold (**Poiseuille**); dexamethasone reduces oedema (onset 2 h, lasts 36-54 h); adrenaline alpha-vasoconstriction for under 2 h -> rebound.`],[`Key history`,`Coryza then barking cough at night (croup) vs hours of high fever and drooling (epiglottitis) vs **sudden choking** (FB); neck pain and dysphagia; immunisation (Hib, DPT); recurrence; allergen exposure; congenital - onset, feeding, cry, intubation history.`],[`Key examination`,`**Look before you touch** - child on parent's lap; RR, HR, SpO2; retractions, air entry, stridor at rest; **4 Ds of epiglottitis** (drooling, dysphagia, dysphonia, distress), tripod; **no tongue depressor if epiglottitis possible**; grey membrane, bull neck; unilateral signs (FB).`],[`Investigations`,`**Croup is clinical** - no neck X-ray; pulse oximetry; lateral neck X-ray only in stable child (thumb sign, prevertebral swelling); inspiratory-expiratory CXR for FB (**normal film does not exclude**); throat swab for diphtheria; laryngoscopy/bronchoscopy definitive.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Croup - typical age, barking cough, stridor, low fever, no toxicity; quietening stridor with falling air entry = deterioration, not improvement.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Every croup: dexamethasone 0.15-0.6 mg/kg orally once (max 16 mg)** - 0.15 mg/kg enough for mild-moderate; alternatives - IM/IV dexamethasone, **nebulised budesonide 2 mg**, prednisolone 1 mg/kg; keep calm, oxygen if SpO2 under 92%.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Moderate-severe: **nebulised L-adrenaline 1:1000 0.5 mL/kg (max 5 mL)** undiluted, repeat 15-20 min if severe; observe 2-4 h; heliox; intubate with tube 0.5-1 mm smaller; epiglottitis - **airway in theatre, ceftriaxone 100 mg/kg/day**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Choking (AHA 2025)**: infant - 5 back blows + 5 chest thrusts with **heel of one hand** on lower sternum; child - 5 back blows alternating with 5 abdominal thrusts; CPR if unresponsive; **no blind finger sweep**; rigid bronchoscopy for FB.`],[`Special groups`,`Under 6 months croup - admit; diphtheria - **antitoxin 20,000-100,000 U on suspicion** + penicillin/erythromycin 14 days, ECG; subglottic haemangioma - **propranolol 1-3 mg/kg/day**; laryngomalacia - reassure, supraglottoplasty if severe.`],[`Complications`,`Respiratory failure, hypoxic arrest, bacterial tracheitis, pneumonia; diphtheria myocarditis (week 2) and neuropathy (week 3); FB obstructive emphysema, recurrent pneumonia.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Drooling or tripod (epiglottitis), stridor at rest after adrenaline, **SpO2 under 92%, cyanosis, drowsiness**, toxic child not responding (tracheitis), neck stiffness with dysphagia, choking history, grey membrane, congenital stridor with FTT or apnoea.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Discharge croup with no stridor at rest, normal SpO2, drinking, **2-4 h after adrenaline**; review 24 h; recurrent or atypical croup - laryngoscopy; diphtheria - ECG and neuro review 6-8 weeks.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Hib in pentavalent at 6, 10, 14 weeks**; DPT boosters and Td; no peanuts or hard seeds under 4-5 years; teach choking first aid at immunisation visits; diphtheria notifiable.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**AHA 2025** choking sequences - heel-of-hand chest thrusts in infants, back blows added before abdominal thrusts in children; croup treatment unchanged.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Stridor is a sign, not a diagnosis; loud stridor reassures more than a quiet one; **every croup gets dexamethasone**; epiglottitis airway secured in theatre, not X-ray room; antitoxin on suspicion.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Acute stridor - sort and treat`,steps:[{label:`Drooling, tripod, toxic?`,detail:`Epiglottitis - sit up with parent, no throat exam, theatre for airway, ceftriaxone`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Sudden choking, afebrile?`,detail:`Foreign body - back blows and thrusts if obstructed; rigid bronchoscopy`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Barking cough after coryza`,detail:`Croup - grade by Westley score`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Mild croup`,detail:`Dexamethasone 0.15 mg/kg once; home care, return signs`,tone:`good`},{label:`Moderate-severe croup`,detail:`Dexamethasone 0.6 mg/kg + nebulised adrenaline 0.5 mL/kg; observe 2-4 h; admit if stridor at rest persists`,tone:`warn`},{label:`No response, toxic`,detail:`Bacterial tracheitis - intubation, bronchoscopy, vancomycin + ceftriaxone`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Croup versus epiglottitis versus tracheitis versus foreign body`,columns:[`Feature`,`Croup`,`Epiglottitis`,`Tracheitis`,`Foreign body`],rows:[[`Age`,`6 months-3 years`,`2-7 years`,`3-10 years`,`1-3 years`],[`Onset`,`Gradual after coryza`,`Hours, fulminant`,`Croup that worsens`,`Sudden choking`],[`Toxicity`,`Not toxic`,`Toxic, high fever`,`Toxic`,`Afebrile`],[`Cough and posture`,`Barking; comfortable`,`No cough; drooling, tripod`,`Brassy, purulent`,`Paroxysmal; unilateral signs`],[`X-ray`,`Steeple sign`,`Thumb sign`,`Ragged tracheal wall`,`Expiratory hyperinflation`],[`Treatment`,`Dexamethasone, adrenaline`,`Airway in theatre, ceftriaxone`,`Intubation, vancomycin + ceftriaxone`,`Rigid bronchoscopy`]]}]},"pediatrics-exanthematous-fevers":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`An **exanthem** is a generalised rash with a systemic infection; an enanthem is its mucosal form.`,`Diagnosis rests on the **day the rash appears**, its morphology and its spread.`,`**Measles** spreads by air with a secondary attack rate over 90%.`,`Measles is infectious from **4 days before to 4 days after** the rash.`,`Varicella is infectious until **all lesions crust**.`,`Measles needs **95% two-dose coverage** for elimination.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Maculopapular**: measles, rubella, roseola, dengue, EBV, drugs.`,`**Vesicular**: varicella, hand-foot-mouth disease, herpes.`,`**Petechial-purpuric**: meningococcaemia, dengue, rickettsia, ITP.`,`**Erythematous-desquamating**: scarlet fever, Kawasaki disease, toxic shock.`,`Rash day: varicella 1, scarlet 2, measles 4, typhus 5, dengue 6, enteric 7.`,`**Roseola** is the exception: its rash appears as the fever falls.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Measles enters immune cells, then spreads to all epithelia, causing **cough, coryza, conjunctivitis**.`,`2. The measles rash is **T cells attacking infected endothelium**, on day 4.`,`3. Measles kills memory cells (**immune amnesia**), so pneumonia and TB follow.`,`4. Measles uses up **vitamin A**, risking keratomalacia and blindness.`,`5. Rubella before 11-12 weeks of pregnancy damages the fetus in up to 90%.`,`6. Varicella comes in crops, so all lesion stages coexist; it hides in nerve ganglia.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask on **which day of fever** the rash came, where it started, how it spread.`,`Ask the prodrome: 3 Cs (measles), sore throat (scarlet), high fever in a well infant (roseola).`,`Ask about MR and varicella vaccination and contacts.`,`Ask about drugs; **amoxicillin in EBV** causes a rash.`,`Ask about field or bush exposure (scrub typhus) and mosquito season (dengue).`,`Ask whether a **pregnant woman** is in the household.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Check shock and sepsis first: pulse, capillary refill, BP, conscious level.`,`**Glass test**: a non-blanching rash with fever is meningococcaemia until proved otherwise.`,`**Koplik spots** opposite lower molars are pathognomonic of measles.`,`Postauricular and suboccipital nodes suggest **rubella**.`,`Search the axilla, groin and waist for an **eschar** (scrub typhus).`,`Fever 5 days or more: look for **Kawasaki** features.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Most exanthems are clinical diagnoses.`,`Every fever with maculopapular rash: **measles and rubella IgM** plus swab for PCR.`,`Petechial rash or dengue: CBC with **platelets and haematocrit**.`,`Dengue NS1 in days 1-5, IgM after day 5; scrub typhus IgM ELISA.`,`Suspected meningococcaemia: blood culture, but **never delay antibiotics**.`,`Kawasaki: CRP, ESR, platelets and **echocardiography**.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Supportive care: fluids, feeding, **paracetamol**; never aspirin.`,`**Vitamin A for every measles case** on days 1 and 2, dose by age.`,`Repeat vitamin A at 2-4 weeks if there are eye signs.`,`Varicella itch: calamine and cetirizine; trim nails.`,`**Avoid ibuprofen** in varicella and dengue.`,`Isolate measles until 4 days after rash; varicella until all lesions crust.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Oral acyclovir 20 mg/kg four times daily for 5 days** within 24 hours of rash.`,`Give it to adolescents, secondary household cases and children on steroids.`,`Immunocompromised or neonatal varicella: **IV acyclovir**.`,`Scarlet fever: **amoxicillin for 10 days** to prevent rheumatic fever.`,`Rickettsia: **doxycycline 2.2 mg/kg twice daily** for 7 days, at any age.`,`Meningococcaemia: **benzylpenicillin or ceftriaxone** in the clinic before transfer.`,`Kawasaki: **IVIG 2 g/kg within 10 days** plus aspirin.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Mother's varicella **5 days before to 2 days after delivery**: newborn needs VZIG.`,`Seronegative pregnant woman exposed to varicella: **VZIG within 10 days**.`,`Measles contacts: **MR vaccine within 72 hours**, or immunoglobulin within 6 days.`,`Parvovirus causes **aplastic crisis** in thalassaemia and sickle cell disease.`,`Defer live vaccines for **11 months after IVIG**.`]},{title:`Complications, red flags and prevention`,points:[`Measles: **pneumonia** is the commonest killer; also blindness and late SSPE.`,`Congenital rubella: **Gregg's triad** of cataract, PDA and deafness.`,`Varicella: secondary skin infection, cerebellar ataxia, **necrotising fasciitis**.`,`Untreated Kawasaki: **coronary aneurysm** in about 25%; dengue warning signs include abdominal pain, bleeding and rising haematocrit.`,`**MR vaccine at 9-12 and 16-24 months** under UIP; varicella vaccine (IAP only) 2 doses from 15 months.`,`Notify every suspected measles-rubella case under IDSP; trace zero-dose children via Mission Indradhanush.`,`Check rubella immunity before pregnancy.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Exanthem drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Vitamin A (measles)`,`50,000 / 1 lakh / 2 lakh IU oral by age`,`Days 1 and 2; day 14-28 if eye signs`,`Vomiting, bulging fontanelle`],[`Acyclovir oral`,`20 mg/kg (max 800 mg) four times daily`,`5 days, start within 24 h`,`Nausea; give with fluids`],[`Acyclovir IV`,`10 mg/kg 8-hourly (20 mg/kg neonate)`,`7-10 days`,`Nephrotoxicity`],[`VZIG`,`125 units IM`,`Within 96 h, up to 10 days`,`Defer live vaccines after`],[`Amoxicillin (scarlet)`,`50 mg/kg once daily, max 1 g`,`10 days`,`Rash, diarrhoea`],[`Doxycycline`,`2.2 mg/kg twice daily, max 100 mg`,`7 days`,`Oesophagitis; stay upright`],[`Benzylpenicillin`,`300 mg (under 1), 600 mg (1-9), 1.2 g (10+)`,`Once before transfer`,`Rare anaphylaxis`],[`IVIG (Kawasaki)`,`2 g/kg over 10-12 hours`,`Once, within 10 days`,`Infusion reactions`],[`Aspirin (Kawasaki)`,`30-50 mg/kg/day, then 3-5 mg/kg/day`,`6-8 weeks`,`Reye syndrome with flu or varicella`]]},{heading:`Common exanthems`,columns:[`Disease`,`Rash timing`,`Rash`,`Key clue`],rows:[[`Measles`,`Day 4 after 3 Cs`,`Maculopapular, head to toe`,`Koplik spots`],[`Rubella`,`Day 1`,`Pink, discrete, gone by day 3`,`Postauricular nodes`],[`Varicella`,`Day 1`,`Vesicles in crops, centripetal`,`All stages together`],[`Roseola`,`As fever falls (day 3-5)`,`Rose-pink`,`Febrile seizure in infant`],[`Scarlet fever`,`Day 2`,`Sandpaper, Pastia lines`,`Strawberry tongue`],[`HFMD`,`Day 1-2`,`Vesicles on palms and soles`,`Painful mouth ulcers`],[`Kawasaki`,`Fever 5 days or more`,`Polymorphous, no vesicles`,`Red eyes, red lips, swollen hands`]]},{heading:`Rash by morphology`,columns:[`Type`,`Examples`,`First action`],rows:[[`Maculopapular`,`Measles, rubella, dengue`,`Notify and send MR IgM`],[`Vesicular`,`Varicella, HFMD`,`Acyclovir if high risk`],[`Petechial-purpuric`,`Meningococcus, dengue, rickettsia`,`Check shock; antibiotics if septic`],[`Desquamating`,`Scarlet, Kawasaki, toxic shock`,`Penicillin or IVIG as indicated`]]}],remember:[`Very Sick Person Must Take Double Tablets: rash days 1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7.`,`Roseola: rash comes as the fever goes.`,`Koplik spots are pathognomonic of measles.`,`Every measles case gets two doses of vitamin A.`,`Gregg's triad: cataract, PDA, deafness.`,`Non-blanching rash with fever: penicillin before transfer.`,`Kawasaki: IVIG 2 g/kg within 10 days.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`AHA 2024 Kawasaki statement (Jone et al.): adjunctive steroids with first IVIG for high-risk children (young infants, early coronary dilatation); infliximab or second IVIG for resistance`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Fever with rash in children`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Exanthem** = generalised rash with systemic (usually viral) infection; **enanthem** = mucosal counterpart (Koplik spots).`],[`Relevant classification`,`Morphology: **maculopapular** (measles, rubella, roseola, dengue), **vesicular** (varicella, HFMD), **petechial-purpuric** (meningococcus, dengue, rickettsia), **erythematous-desquamating** (scarlet, Kawasaki, TSS). Day of rash: **varicella 1, scarlet 2, measles 4, typhus 5, dengue 6, enteric 7**; roseola as fever falls.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Measles (secondary attack over 90%), rubella, VZV, HHV-6, parvovirus B19, GAS, coxsackie/EV71, dengue, Orientia, meningococcus, Kawasaki; unimmunised, malnourished, immunocompromised, pregnant contacts.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Measles - CD150/nectin-4, **immune-mediated rash day 4**, **immune amnesia** -> pneumonia, diarrhoea, TB flare; vitamin A depletion -> keratomalacia; rubella teratogenic before 12 weeks; VZV crops and latency; Kawasaki - medium-vessel vasculitis, coronaries.`],[`Key history`,`Day of fever when rash appeared, start and spread, itch; prodrome (3 Cs); **immunisation** (MR, varicella); contacts and exposure date; drugs before rash; field exposure; danger symptoms; immunosuppression; **pregnant household member**; fever 5 days or more.`],[`Key examination`,`Vitals and perfusion first; **glass test** for non-blanching; rash morphology and distribution; mouth (**Koplik, strawberry tongue**, ulcers); eyes (Bitot, corneal clouding, limbal-sparing conjunctivitis); nodes; **eschar**; hepatosplenomegaly; tourniquet test.`],[`Investigations`,`**Every fever-maculopapular rash = suspected measles-rubella - IgM + throat swab/urine PCR**; CBC, Hct, platelets for petechiae/dengue; NS1 days 1-5, IgM after; scrub IgM; blood culture (never delay antibiotics); Kawasaki - CRP, ESR, **echo at diagnosis, 1-2 and 4-6 weeks**.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Measles - fever + maculopapular rash + cough, coryza or conjunctivitis; Kawasaki - fever 5 days + 4 of 5 (conjunctivitis, oral changes, rash, extremity changes, node 1.5 cm).`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Supportive care; **vitamin A days 1 and 2 in every measles case (50,000 / 1 lakh / 2 lakh IU by age; third dose at 2-4 weeks if eye signs)**; paracetamol only (**no aspirin; no ibuprofen in varicella and dengue**); calamine and cetirizine for itch.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Acyclovir 20 mg/kg (max 800 mg) QID x 5 days within 24 h** for over 12 years, secondary household cases, chronic skin/lung disease, steroids; **IV 10 mg/kg 8-hourly** for immunocompromised; scarlet - amoxicillin 50 mg/kg x 10 days; rickettsia - **doxycycline 2.2 mg/kg BD**; meningococcaemia - **benzylpenicillin or ceftriaxone before transfer**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Glass tumbler test; tourniquet test (10 or more petechiae per square inch); airborne isolation; IgM sampling under case-based surveillance.`],[`Special groups`,`**Kawasaki - IVIG 2 g/kg within 10 days + aspirin 30-50 then 3-5 mg/kg/day**, steroids with IVIG if high risk (AHA 2024), defer live vaccines 11 months; neonatal varicella exposure - **VZIG**; pregnant seronegative - VZIG within 10 days; measles PEP - **MR within 72 h** or immunoglobulin within 6 days.`],[`Complications`,`Measles - **pneumonia (main killer)**, diarrhoea, keratomalacia, encephalitis, **SSPE**; CRS - **cataract, PDA, deafness**; varicella - skin infection, cerebellar ataxia, necrotising fasciitis; EV71 brainstem encephalitis; Kawasaki coronary aneurysm.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Non-blanching rash, shock, altered sensorium, seizures, respiratory distress, corneal clouding, dengue warning signs, Kawasaki criteria, varicella in neonate or immunocompromised, myoclonic jerks in HFMD.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review measles and varicella at **48-72 h**; isolation - measles 4 days after rash, varicella until crusted, rubella 7 days; CRS infants - eye, hearing, cardiac assessment; catch-up vaccination of family.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**MR at 9-12 and 16-24 months**, MR elimination target, fever-rash surveillance under **IDSP/IHIP**, outbreak vitamin A and ring vaccination; IAP varicella 2 doses; rubella immunity before pregnancy.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`Kawasaki reference updated to **AHA 2024** (adjunctive steroids for high-risk, infliximab option); no change to measles, varicella or meningococcal management.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Very Sick Person Must Take Double Tablets**; Koplik spots precede rash; roseola rash comes as fever goes; Gregg's triad; two doses of vitamin A halve measles mortality.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Fever with rash - first look`,steps:[{label:`Shock or non-blanching rash?`,detail:`Glass test; purpura with fever = meningococcaemia until proved otherwise - benzylpenicillin or ceftriaxone now, transfer`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Day of rash and morphology`,detail:`Vesicular, maculopapular, petechial or desquamating; distribution; palms-soles`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Maculopapular + 3 Cs`,detail:`Suspected measles - vitamin A days 1 and 2, isolate, notify, IgM and PCR`},{label:`Fever 5 days or more`,detail:`Check Kawasaki criteria - IVIG 2 g/kg within 10 days, echo`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Petechiae in season`,detail:`Dengue - CBC, Hct, NS1/IgM; warning signs = admit; no NSAIDs`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Viral exanthem, well child`,detail:`Paracetamol, fluids, isolation advice, return signs`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Measles, rubella, varicella, roseola - at a glance`,columns:[`Feature`,`Measles`,`Rubella`,`Varicella`,`Roseola`],rows:[[`Prodrome`,`3-4 days of 3 Cs`,`Minimal`,`Brief`,`3-5 days high fever, well child`],[`Rash day`,`Day 4, cephalocaudal, confluent`,`Day 1, pink, fades by day 3`,`Day 1, centripetal, crops`,`As fever falls`],[`Clue`,`Koplik spots`,`Postauricular, suboccipital nodes`,`All stages together`,`Febrile seizure`],[`Key treatment`,`Vitamin A days 1 and 2`,`Supportive; protect pregnant contacts`,`Acyclovir for high-risk within 24 h`,`Paracetamol only`]]}]},"pediatrics-child-abuse-pocso":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Child maltreatment** is abuse, neglect or exploitation of anyone **under 18**, causing harm.`,`It happens within a relationship of trust, responsibility or power.`,`The **age of consent is 18**; sex with a minor is an offence even if called consensual.`,`**Neglect is the commonest** form.`,`In **over 90%** of POCSO cases the offender is known to the child.`,`POCSO is **gender-neutral**; boys are abused too and disclose less.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Physical abuse**, including abusive head trauma.`,`**Sexual abuse**, contact or non-contact, including online grooming.`,`**Emotional abuse**, including exposure to domestic violence.`,`**Neglect** of physical, medical, educational or emotional needs.`,`**Fabricated or induced illness** by a carer.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Risk builds from child, parent and social factors together.`,`2. Shaking tears bridging veins, causing **subdural haemorrhage** and brain swelling.`,`3. Shaking also causes **multilayered retinal haemorrhages** to the ora serrata.`,`4. Squeezing the chest causes **posterior rib fractures**; pulling limbs causes metaphyseal fractures.`,`5. Grooming builds trust and secrecy, so disclosure is delayed and often retracted.`,`6. Genital tissues **heal quickly**, so a normal exam does not exclude abuse.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Red flags: **delay in seeking care**, a changing history, injury not matching development.`,`Talk to the child separately with open questions.`,`Record the child's **exact words in quotation marks**.`,`**Do not interview the child repeatedly.**`,`Ask who lives at home and who cares for the child.`,`Ask about behaviour change, secondary enuresis and sexualised behaviour.`,`In sexual assault, note the **time since the event** for PEP and contraception.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Treat life-threatening injuries first**; care never waits for police.`,`Record every injury on a **body map** and plot growth.`,`**Those who don't cruise rarely bruise**: any bruise in a non-mobile infant needs evaluation.`,`**TEN-4-FACESp**: torso, ears, neck, frenulum, jaw angle, cheeks, eyelids, patterned bruises.`,`A girl is examined by a **woman doctor** with a trusted adult; **two-finger test is banned**.`,`Straddle injuries are **anterior**; penetrating injuries are **posterior**.`,`Look for mimics: osteogenesis imperfecta, rickets, Mongolian spots, lichen sclerosus.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Under 2 years: **skeletal survey**, repeated at 2 weeks.`,`CT head for infants under 1 year and **fundoscopy**.`,`Bruising: CBC, platelets and clotting to exclude a bleeding disorder.`,`Fractures: calcium, phosphate, ALP and vitamin D to exclude rickets.`,`Sexual assault: forensic samples within 72-96 hours with **chain of custody**.`,`Test for HIV, hepatitis B, syphilis, gonorrhoea, chlamydia and pregnancy.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Believe and reassure the child; **never promise secrecy**.`,`**POCSO Section 19**: every doctor must report suspected sexual abuse to police.`,`Report even if the adolescent says it was consensual.`,`Emergency care is **free**, with no FIR needed (POCSO Rule 6, BNSS S397).`,`Consent: parent under 12; child plus parent from 12 to 18.`,`If home is unsafe, admit and inform the **Child Welfare Committee** or **CHILDLINE 1098**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Emergency contraception: **levonorgestrel 1.5 mg** within 72 hours (up to 120).`,`**HIV PEP within 72 hours**: TDF, lamivudine and dolutegravir for 28 days in adolescents.`,`Adolescent STI cover: **ceftriaxone 1 g IM**, doxycycline 7 days, metronidazole 2 g once.`,`Complete hepatitis B vaccine; HBIG if the source is HBsAg-positive.`,`Give tetanus cover and trauma-focused CBT.`,`Pregnancy in a minor can be terminated up to 24 weeks; report without naming her.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Penetrative assault: **at least 10 years**, at least 20 if the child is under 16.`,`Aggravated penetrative assault: **at least 20 years, life or death**.`,`**Section 21**: failure to report is punishable by up to 6 months or fine.`,`Section 23 bars revealing the child's identity; Section 24 governs recording statements.`,`Special Courts sit **in camera** and aim to finish trial within 1 year.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review survivors at **2 weeks, 6 weeks, 3 months and 6 months**.`,`Refer suicidal ideation or self-harm urgently.`,`Involve the CWC whenever the abuser still has access to the child.`,`Send the medico-legal report to police **within 7 days**.`,`Keep clear, contemporaneous records; they will be read in court.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs after sexual assault`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Levonorgestrel`,`1.5 mg oral once`,`Within 72 h (up to 120 h)`,`Nausea; repeat if vomited in 3 h`],[`TDF + 3TC + DTG`,`300 + 300 + 50 mg once daily (30 kg+)`,`28 days, start within 72 h`,`Nausea, headache; no antacids near DTG`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`1 g IM (45 kg+); 25-50 mg/kg, max 250 mg`,`Once`,`Injection pain`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg twice daily (45 kg+)`,`7 days`,`Oesophagitis; take upright`],[`Azithromycin`,`1 g once, or 20 mg/kg (max 1 g) in children`,`Once`,`Nausea`],[`Metronidazole`,`2 g once (adolescent); 15 mg/kg/day child`,`Once; 7 days for child`,`No alcohol for 48 h`],[`Hepatitis B vaccine / HBIG`,`Vaccine 0, 1, 6 months; HBIG 0.06 mL/kg`,`HBIG within 72 h`,`Different injection sites`],[`Fluoxetine (PTSD, depression)`,`10 mg daily, then 20 mg`,`After psychotherapy if needed`,`Watch for suicidal thoughts`]]},{heading:`Types of maltreatment`,columns:[`Type`,`Examples`,`Key clue`],rows:[[`Physical`,`Hitting, shaking, burns`,`Injury not matching history`],[`Sexual`,`Contact, online grooming`,`Genital injury, STI, pregnancy`],[`Emotional`,`Threats, domestic violence`,`Withdrawal, self-harm`],[`Neglect`,`Missed care, poor hygiene`,`Failure to thrive, missed vaccines`],[`Fabricated illness`,`Carer invents symptoms`,`Symptoms only when carer present`]]},{heading:`POCSO offences`,columns:[`Offence`,`Section`,`Punishment`],rows:[[`Penetrative sexual assault`,`S3/S4`,`10 years to life; 20+ if under 16`],[`Aggravated penetrative`,`S5/S6`,`20 years, life or death`],[`Sexual assault`,`S7/S8`,`3-5 years`],[`Aggravated sexual assault`,`S9/S10`,`5-7 years`],[`Sexual harassment`,`S11/S12`,`Up to 3 years`],[`Failure to report`,`S21`,`Up to 6 months or fine`]]}],remember:[`Section 19: every doctor must report; silence is an offence (S21).`,`Emergency care first, and free.`,`Those who don't cruise rarely bruise.`,`TEN-4-FACESp for suspicious bruising.`,`A normal genital exam does not exclude abuse.`,`Straddle anterior, penetration posterior.`,`Know CHILDLINE 1098 and the Child Welfare Committee.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`WHO STI 2024: gonorrhoea - ceftriaxone 1 g IM single dose for adolescents (was 500 mg); chlamydia - doxycycline 100 mg BD 7 days preferred, azithromycin 1 g single dose as alternative`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Child abuse, neglect and POCSO`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`All forms of physical, sexual, emotional ill-treatment, **neglect (commonest)** and exploitation of a **child under 18** causing actual or potential harm in a relationship of trust or power; **age of consent 18**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Five types: **physical** (incl. abusive head trauma), **sexual** (contact, non-contact, online grooming), **emotional**, **neglect**, **fabricated or induced illness**; plus exploitation (labour, trafficking, child marriage).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Child - infancy, disability, prematurity; parent - young, substance use, mental illness, own abuse, domestic violence; social - poverty, crowding, hostels; **over 90% of POCSO offenders known to child** (NCRB 2022).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Shaking -> bridging vein tear (**SDH**), multilayer **retinal haemorrhages**, axonal shear; **classic metaphyseal and posterior rib fractures** from torsion and squeezing; toxic stress -> HPA activation -> adult ACE disease.`],[`Key history`,`**Delay, vague or changing history, injury inconsistent with development**; child interviewed once, separately, **exact words in quotes**; carers, previous injuries; behaviour change, secondary enuresis; HEADSS for adolescents; time since assault (72-120 h windows); bleeding disorders.`],[`Key examination`,`**Treat emergencies first**; head-to-toe body map with photographs; **'those who don't cruise rarely bruise'**; **TEN-4-FACESp**; patterned injuries, immersion burns; torn frenulum, fundoscopy; genital exam by **woman doctor** for girls, no internal exam, **two-finger test banned**; neglect signs; mimics.`],[`Investigations`,`Under 2 years: **skeletal survey (repeat at 2 weeks), CT head under 1 year, fundoscopy**; bleeding screen; bone profile; LFT, lipase, urine blood; forensic samples within 72-96 h with **chain of custody**; HIV, HBsAg, VDRL, NAAT GC/CT, pregnancy test.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Pattern recognition - injury unexplained by mechanism or development, sentinel injuries in infants, STI beyond neonatal period, disclosure; **normal genital exam does not exclude abuse**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Believe, reassure, **never promise secrecy**; **report under POCSO Section 19** to SJPU/police (failure = offence, Section 21); **free emergency care** (POCSO Rule 6, BNSS S397); safety - admit, inform **CWC via 1098/112**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**EC - levonorgestrel 1.5 mg within 72 h (to 120 h)** or ulipristal 30 mg; **HIV PEP TDF+3TC+DTG 28 days within 72 h**; adolescents **ceftriaxone 1 g IM + doxycycline 100 mg BD 7 days + metronidazole 2 g** (WHO 2024); HepB vaccine +/- HBIG; trauma-focused CBT.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Body map, scaled photographs, sealed labelled forensic packets; consent - **parent under 12, child + parent 12-18**; MLC report to investigating officer **within 7 days** (BNSS S184).`],[`Special groups`,`Children under 45 kg - ceftriaxone 25-50 mg/kg (max 250 mg), azithromycin 20 mg/kg; pregnancy in a minor - **MTP up to 24 weeks**, report without naming minor; boys abused too (POCSO gender-neutral).`],[`Complications`,`Death or disability from AHT, PTSD, depression, self-harm, substance use, STI, pregnancy, revictimisation, adult chronic disease.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Any bruise in non-mobile infant; posterior rib or metaphyseal fractures; unexplained infant drowsiness or seizures; **STI or pregnancy in a child**; disclosure; suicidal ideation; abuser with ongoing access.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Survivor review at **2 weeks, 6 weeks, 3 months, 6 months** (PEP adherence, repeat HIV, syphilis, HepB, pregnancy, mental health); growth and school in neglect; legible records for court.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**POCSO Act 2012 (amended 2019), Rules 2020, JJ Act 2015, Mission Vatsalya**, CWC, DCPU, SJPU, **CHILDLINE 1098 integrated with ERSS 112**, NCPCR POCSO e-box, positive parenting, body-safety teaching (WHO INSPIRE).`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WHO STI 2024**: ceftriaxone 1 g IM for gonorrhoea and doxycycline 7 days preferred for chlamydia in adolescents.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Every doctor must report (S19); emergency care free without FIR; straddle injuries anterior, penetrating posterior; **TEN-4-FACESp**; record the child's exact words.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Suspected child sexual abuse - what the doctor does`,steps:[{label:`Treat emergencies first, free`,detail:`No FIR or police requisition needed (POCSO Rule 6, BNSS S397)`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Consent and history once`,detail:`Parent under 12, child + parent 12-18; child's exact words; woman doctor for a girl`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Examine and collect evidence`,detail:`Body map, photographs; forensic samples within 72-96 h; chain of custody; no two-finger test`},{label:`Prophylaxis`,detail:`EC within 120 h; HIV PEP within 72 h for 28 days; STI cover (ceftriaxone 1 g + doxycycline + metronidazole); HepB vaccine`},{label:`Report and protect`,detail:`Police/SJPU under Section 19; CWC via 1098/112 within 24 h; admit if home unsafe`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Abusive versus accidental injury`,columns:[`Feature`,`Suggests abuse`,`Suggests accident`],rows:[[`Bruise site`,`Torso, ears, neck, cheeks, buttocks, genitalia (TEN-4-FACESp)`,`Shins, forehead, elbows over bony prominences`],[`Age`,`Any bruise in non-mobile infant or under 4 months`,`Cruising or walking child`],[`Fractures`,`Posterior rib, metaphyseal corner, different ages`,`Single long-bone fracture with matching mechanism`],[`Burns`,`Glove-and-stocking with tide-lines, cigarette burns 8-10 mm`,`Irregular splash with drip marks`],[`History`,`Delayed, vague, changing, inconsistent with development`,`Prompt, consistent, plausible`]]}]},"pediatrics-adolescent-health":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`WHO: **adolescents 10-19 years**, youth 15-24, young people 10-24.`,`India has about **253 million adolescents**, one in five people.`,`NFHS-5: **59.1% of girls** and 31.1% of boys aged 15-19 are anaemic.`,`23.3% of women aged 20-24 married before 18.`,`Suicide and road injuries are leading causes of adolescent death.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Early (10-13)**: growth spurt, body image, concrete thinking.`,`**Middle (14-16)**: peer pressure, risk-taking, sexual interest.`,`**Late (17-19)**: stable identity, future plans, relationships.`,`**Precocious puberty**: before 8 in girls, 9 in boys.`,`**Delayed puberty**: no breasts by 13 or no testicular enlargement by 14.`,`Evaluate **primary amenorrhoea at 15** (or 13 with no breasts).`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Kisspeptin switches on pulsatile **GnRH**, raising LH and FSH.`,`2. Sex steroids amplify growth hormone, driving the growth spurt.`,`3. Iron needs rise with growth and menstruation; Indian diets are low in haem iron.`,`4. Early cycles are **anovulatory** for 2-3 years, so irregular cycles are normal.`,`5. The **reward system matures before the prefrontal cortex**, so risk-taking rises.`,`6. About **half of adult mental disorders** begin by 14 years.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`See the adolescent **alone** for part of every visit.`,`Explain confidentiality and its limits first: risk, abuse, legal duty.`,`**HEADSSS**: Home, Education, Eating, Activities, Drugs, Sexuality, Suicide, Safety.`,`Go from least to most sensitive topic.`,`**Ask about suicide directly**; it does not plant the idea.`,`Take a menstrual history: cycle, heavy bleeding, pain, acne and hirsutism.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Plot height, weight and BMI on **IAP 2015 charts**.`,`Measure BP; from 13 years, **130/80 or more** is stage 1 hypertension.`,`Assess **Tanner staging** with consent and chaperone.`,`Look for pallor, acanthosis nigricans, acne, hirsutism and goitre.`,`Look for **self-harm scars** and signs of substance use.`,`Eating disorder danger: **heart rate under 50**, low BP, low BMI.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Haemoglobin at least yearly in girls.`,`Heavy bleeding at menarche: CBC, clotting and **von Willebrand screen**.`,`Pregnancy test for amenorrhoea or abnormal bleeding if sexually active.`,`Obese adolescents: glucose or HbA1c, lipids and ALT.`,`**PHQ-A** for depression; any self-harm answer needs same-day assessment.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**WIFS**: one **blue tablet** (60 mg iron + 500 mcg folic acid) weekly.`,`**Albendazole 400 mg** on 10 February and 10 August.`,`Anaemia: **60 mg iron daily** if mild, 120 mg if moderate, for 2 months.`,`Dysmenorrhoea: **NSAIDs started at onset** of pain for 2-3 days.`,`60 minutes of activity a day, under 2 hours screens, 8-10 hours sleep.`,`Age alone is **not a contraindication** to any contraceptive.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Dysmenorrhoea not settling after 3 cycles: **combined pill**; failure suggests endometriosis.`,`Heavy bleeding: **tranexamic acid 1 g three times daily** for up to 5 days.`,`Emergency contraception: **levonorgestrel 1.5 mg** within 72 hours.`,`**HPV vaccine**: single dose for girls 9-15 (IAP); national campaign at 14.`,`Moderate-severe depression: **fluoxetine 10 mg then 20 mg**, with weekly review.`,`Substance use: brief intervention; tobacco quitline 1800-11-2356.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Suicidal plan or recent self-harm: **same-day safety assessment**.`,`Sexual abuse disclosure: **POCSO report** and protection.`,`Pregnancy under 18: higher anaemia, pre-eclampsia, obstructed labour and LBW.`,`Severe anaemia under 8 g/dL: refer.`,`Sudden school decline with withdrawal or hallucinations: refer.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**RKSK (2014)** covers all 10-19-year-olds in six priority areas.`,`Six areas: nutrition, SRH, NCDs, substance misuse, injuries and violence, mental health.`,`**Adolescent Friendly Health Clinics** at PHC, CHC and district hospital.`,`**Saathiya** peer educators: four per village (two boys, two girls).`,`School Health and Wellness Programme: two ambassadors per school.`,`Tele-MANAS 14416 and CHILDLINE 1098 for support.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Adolescent drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`IFA (blue tablet)`,`60 mg iron + 500 mcg folic acid weekly`,`All 10-19 years`,`Black stools, nausea`],[`Iron (treatment)`,`60 mg daily (mild); 120 mg daily (moderate)`,`2 months`,`Between meals with vitamin C`],[`Albendazole`,`400 mg chewed`,`Twice a year`,`Not in first trimester`],[`Mefenamic acid / ibuprofen`,`500 mg TDS / 400 mg TDS-QID`,`2-3 days from pain onset`,`Dyspepsia; avoid in ulcer`],[`Tranexamic acid`,`1 g TDS`,`Up to 5 days in menses`,`Avoid with clot history`],[`Combined pill`,`EE 20-30 mcg + levonorgestrel 150 mcg`,`21 days, 7-day break`,`MEC 1 from menarche`],[`Levonorgestrel EC`,`1.5 mg single dose`,`Within 72 h`,`Nausea; repeat if vomited in 3 h`],[`HPV vaccine`,`0.5 mL IM deltoid`,`Single dose girls 9-15`,`Syncope; observe 15 min`],[`Fluoxetine`,`10 mg for 1 week, then 20 mg`,`6 months after remission`,`Early suicidality; weekly review`]]},{heading:`RKSK six priority areas`,columns:[`Area`,`Key interventions`],rows:[[`Nutrition`,`Weekly IFA, deworming, counselling`],[`Sexual and reproductive health`,`AFHC, contraception, menstrual hygiene, delay marriage`],[`Non-communicable diseases`,`BMI and BP screening, activity, tobacco control`],[`Substance misuse`,`Brief intervention, de-addiction referral`],[`Injuries and violence`,`Road safety, POCSO reporting`],[`Mental health`,`Counselling, life skills, Tele-MANAS`]]},{heading:`HEADSSS assessment`,columns:[`Letter`,`Ask about`],rows:[[`H - Home`,`Who lives with you, relationships`],[`E - Education/Eating`,`School performance, bullying, dieting, vomiting`],[`A - Activities`,`Friends, sport, screens, sleep`],[`D - Drugs`,`Tobacco, alcohol, cannabis, inhalants`],[`S - Sexuality`,`Relationships, consent, contraception`],[`S - Suicide, Safety`,`Low mood, self-harm, violence, online safety`]]}],remember:[`Adolescents 10-19; youth 15-24; young people 10-24.`,`HEADSSS, from least to most sensitive.`,`See the adolescent alone; explain confidentiality limits.`,`WIFS: one blue tablet weekly; albendazole twice a year.`,`Heavy bleeding at menarche: exclude von Willebrand disease.`,`NSAIDs for dysmenorrhoea at onset of pain.`,`Asking about suicide saves lives.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`MoHFW 2026: national HPV campaign (28 February 2026) - free single dose of quadrivalent vaccine for every girl aged 14`,`IAP ACVIP 2025: HPV single dose for immunocompetent girls 9-15; 2 doses boys; 3 doses at 15-26 or immunocompromised (was 2 doses at 9-14)`,`WHO MEC 6th edition (2025) cited for adolescent contraception - age categories unchanged (DMPA under 18 and IUCD under 20 remain MEC 2)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Adolescent health and RKSK`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`WHO: **adolescents 10-19, youth 15-24, young people 10-24**; India about **253 million adolescents** - largest in the world.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Stages: **early 10-13, middle 14-16, late 17-19**; puberty by **SMR (Tanner 1-5)**; precocious puberty before 8 (girls) / 9 (boys); delayed - no breasts by 13, no testicular enlargement by 14; primary amenorrhoea at 15.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Anaemia (**NFHS-5: 59.1% girls, 31.1% boys 15-19**), child marriage (23.3%), teenage pregnancy, tobacco (8.5% of 13-15), mental disorders (7.3% of 13-17), suicide and road injury, obesity, substance use.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Kisspeptin -> pulsatile GnRH -> LH/FSH; **reward system matures before prefrontal cortex** -> risk-taking; anovulatory early cycles -> irregular, heavy bleeding; **prostaglandin F2-alpha -> dysmenorrhoea**; half of adult mental disorders start by 14.`],[`Key history`,`See **alone** after explaining confidentiality and its limits (POCSO); **HEADSSS** - Home, Education, Eating, Activities, Drugs, Sexuality, Suicide, Safety; menstrual history; puberty concerns; WIFS adherence; marriage plans; family NCD history.`],[`Key examination`,`Height, weight, **BMI on IAP 2015 charts** (23/27); **BP - 130/80 or more is stage 1 from 13 years**; SMR with chaperone; pallor, acanthosis, hirsutism, acne; self-harm scars; eating-disorder signs (HR under 50); vision, teeth, scoliosis.`],[`Investigations`,`Hb yearly in girls; heavy bleeding at menarche - **CBC, coagulation, von Willebrand screen**, pregnancy test; obese - glucose/HbA1c, lipids, ALT; delayed puberty - LH, FSH, sex steroids, TSH, prolactin, bone age; **PHQ-A** (10+ assess, item 9 same-day).`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Anaemia from 15 years under 12 (girls) / 13 (boys); depression often presents as **irritability and school decline**; dysmenorrhoea primary if pelvic exam normal and NSAID-responsive.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Trusted adult, brief practical advice; nutrition, **60 min activity**, screen under 2 h, 8-10 h sleep; menstrual hygiene; **WIFS blue tablet 60 mg Fe + 500 mcg FA weekly**; **albendazole 400 mg on 10 Feb and 10 Aug**; dysmenorrhoea **mefenamic acid 500 mg TDS or ibuprofen 400 mg from onset**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Anaemia **60 mg (mild) or 120 mg (moderate) iron daily x 2 months**; COC for refractory dysmenorrhoea (failure - endometriosis); tranexamic acid 1 g TDS for heavy bleeding; **fluoxetine 10 then 20 mg** with psychotherapy for moderate-severe depression; NRT for smokers.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Contraception - **condoms (dual protection), COC (MEC 1), DMPA (MEC 2 under 18), centchroman, IUCD (MEC 2 under 20)** (WHO MEC 2025); **EC levonorgestrel 1.5 mg within 72 h**; **HPV single dose** with 15-min observation.`],[`Special groups`,`Married adolescents - **delay first pregnancy to 20, space 3 years**; teenage pregnancy - anaemia, pre-eclampsia, obstructed labour, LBW; eating disorders; out-of-school girls - Scheme for Adolescent Girls.`],[`Complications`,`Suicide, injuries, substance dependence, STI/HIV, unintended pregnancy, anaemia, adult NCDs from adolescent behaviours, SSRI early suicidality.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Suicidal plan or recent attempt, psychosis, **eating disorder with HR under 50 or BMI under 15**, severe anaemia, pregnancy under 18, sexual abuse (POCSO), substance dependence, primary amenorrhoea at 15.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Annual check (HEADSSS, BMI, BP, Hb, SMR, vaccines); anaemia Hb at 1 and 2 months; **SSRI weekly for 4 weeks**; link to AFHC, Tele-MANAS **14416**, CHILDLINE 1098.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**RKSK 2014** - 6 priority areas (nutrition, SRH, NCD, substance misuse, injuries and violence, mental health); **AFHCs, Saathiya peer educators (4 per 1000)**, AHWDs, WIFS/AMB, Menstrual Hygiene Scheme, School Health and Wellness Programme, RBSK, **national HPV campaign 2026**.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**HPV**: national single dose at 14 years (2026) and IAP 2025 single dose for girls 9-15; **WHO MEC 6th edition 2025**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`See the adolescent alone; asking about suicide does not plant the idea; age alone is not a contraindication to any contraceptive; heavy bleeding at menarche - exclude von Willebrand first.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Adolescent consultation - structure`,steps:[{label:`Together, then alone`,detail:`See parent and adolescent, then the adolescent alone for part of every visit`},{label:`Confidentiality and its limits`,detail:`Risk to self or others, abuse, legal duty (POCSO under 18)`,tone:`decision`},{label:`HEADSSS, least to most sensitive`,detail:`Home, Education, Eating, Activities, Drugs, Sexuality, Suicide, Safety`},{label:`Examine and screen`,detail:`BMI, BP, SMR, pallor, self-harm signs; Hb; PHQ-A`},{label:`Same-day action if risk`,detail:`Suicidal plan, abuse, pregnancy under 18, eating disorder with HR under 50`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Link to RKSK services`,detail:`AFHC, WIFS, deworming, HPV single dose at 14, Saathiya, Tele-MANAS 14416`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`RKSK - six priority areas`,root:`Rashtriya Kishor Swasthya Karyakram (2014)`,arms:[{label:`Nutrition`,steps:[`WIFS weekly blue IFA`,`Biannual albendazole`,`Counselling`]},{label:`Sexual and reproductive health`,steps:[`AFHC counselling and contraception`,`Menstrual hygiene`,`Delay marriage and first pregnancy`]},{label:`Non-communicable diseases`,steps:[`BMI and BP screening`,`Activity and diet`,`Tobacco control`]},{label:`Substance misuse`,steps:[`Brief intervention (5 As)`,`De-addiction referral`]},{label:`Injuries and violence`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Road safety`,`Gender-based violence response`,`POCSO reporting`]},{label:`Mental health`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Life skills, counselling`,`Tele-MANAS 14416`,`Referral`]}]}]},"pediatrics-neonatal-seizures-common-problems":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Neonatal seizures occur in the first 28 days, mostly in the **first week**.`,`About **half are subtle**: eye deviation, lip smacking, pedalling or apnoea.`,`Only about one third of EEG seizures are clinically visible.`,`Main causes: **HIE, hypoglycaemia, hypocalcaemia and sepsis-meningitis**.`,`Treat neonatal glucose **under 45 mg/dL**.`,`**Infant colic**: crying in a thriving baby under 5 months with no illness.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Subtle** (commonest), **clonic**, **tonic** and **myoclonic** seizures.`,`Focal clonic suggests a focal lesion such as stroke; better prognosis.`,`Myoclonic suggests inborn errors; worst prognosis.`,`Hypothermia: cold stress 36.0-36.4, moderate 32.0-35.9, **severe under 32 C**.`,`Wessel rule of threes for colic: 3 hours a day, 3 days a week, 3 weeks.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. In newborn neurons **GABA is excitatory**, so the brain seizes easily.`,`2. Poor myelination makes seizures fragmentary and subtle.`,`3. HIE seizures usually start **within 12-24 hours** of birth.`,`4. Infants of diabetic mothers have **fetal hyperinsulinism**, so glucose falls at 1-6 hours.`,`5. Late hypocalcaemia at day 5-10 follows **high-phosphate cow's milk** feeds.`,`6. Low magnesium blocks PTH, so calcium will not correct until magnesium is given.`,`7. Cold burns brown fat, causing **hypoxia, hypoglycaemia and acidosis**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Get an eyewitness account; did the movement **stop when the limb was held**?`,`Onset in the first 24 hours suggests HIE, hypoglycaemia or sepsis.`,`Onset at **day 5-10** suggests late hypocalcaemia.`,`Ask about birth asphyxia, maternal diabetes, PROM and maternal drugs.`,`Ask about **cow's milk** and prelacteal feeds.`,`Crying: timing, consolability, vomiting, stools; screen mother with **EPDS**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Jitteriness stops when the limb is held**; a seizure does not.`,`Apnoea with tachycardia suggests seizure; apnoea with bradycardia suggests prematurity.`,`Check temperature, respiratory rate, perfusion and **bedside glucose**.`,`Touch method: cold feet with warm abdomen means **cold stress**.`,`Check fontanelle, tone, suck and Sarnat stage.`,`Crying baby: undress fully; look for **hair tourniquet**, hernia, fracture and bruises.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Bedside glucose immediately**; do not delay treatment for lab confirmation.`,`Calcium, magnesium, sodium, blood gas, blood culture and CSF.`,`Cranial ultrasound; EEG and MRI once stable.`,`At-risk babies: glucose at **2, 6, 12, 24, 48 and 72 hours** before feeds.`,`Persistent hypoglycaemia: critical sample (insulin, cortisol, GH, ketones).`,`**Colic needs no tests.**`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Step 1: ABC, warmth and glucose; **10% dextrose 2 mL/kg** if under 45 mg/dL.`,`Then dextrose infusion at **GIR 6-8 mg/kg/min**.`,`Step 2: **10% calcium gluconate 2 mL/kg** over 10 minutes with heart monitoring.`,`Step 3: **phenobarbitone 20 mg/kg IV**, up to 40 mg/kg.`,`**Avoid diazepam** in neonates.`,`Asymptomatic low glucose: **40% dextrose gel 0.5 mL/kg** buccal, then breastfeed.`,`Hypothermia: **skin-to-skin**, cover, feed and recheck in 1 hour.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Second line: **levetiracetam 40-60 mg/kg**, phenytoin or midazolam.`,`Refractory seizures with no cause: **pyridoxine 100 mg IV**.`,`Low magnesium: **magnesium sulphate 50%, 0.2 mL/kg**.`,`GIR over 10-12 mg/kg/min suggests **hyperinsulinism**; refer.`,`Stop anti-seizure drugs before discharge if the examination is normal.`,`Colic: reassure; **L. reuteri** may help breastfed babies; no dicyclomine under 6 months.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Hypothermia, hypoglycaemia and sepsis **travel together**.`,`Severe hypoglycaemia damages the occipital cortex, causing visual loss and epilepsy.`,`**HELIX**: cooling for HIE did not help and raised mortality in LMIC units.`,`Crying peaks at 6 weeks, when **abusive head trauma** also peaks.`,`Teach parents it is safe to **put the baby down and walk away**; never shake.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Every neonatal seizure is referred** to an SNCU or NICU.`,`Refer symptomatic or persistent hypoglycaemia.`,`Refer severe hypothermia or moderate hypothermia not warming in 1 hour.`,`HBNC visits on days 3, 7, 14, 21, 28 and 42.`,`Follow seizure, HIE and hypoglycaemia babies at the **DEIC** for development.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Neonatal emergency drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`10% dextrose`,`2 mL/kg IV, then GIR 6-8 mg/kg/min`,`Wean as feeds increase`,`Recheck glucose in 30 min`],[`40% dextrose gel`,`0.5 mL/kg (200 mg/kg) buccal`,`Up to 6 doses in 48 h`,`Follow with a breastfeed`],[`10% calcium gluconate`,`2 mL/kg IV over 10 min; then 8 mL/kg/day`,`48-72 hours`,`Stop if HR under 100; extravasation necrosis`],[`Magnesium sulphate 50%`,`0.2 mL/kg IM or IV`,`Repeat 12-hourly if needed`,`Hypotonia, apnoea`],[`Phenobarbitone`,`20 mg/kg IV, up to 40; then 3-5 mg/kg/day`,`Stop before discharge if well`,`Respiratory depression`],[`Levetiracetam`,`40-60 mg/kg IV over 15 min`,`Second line`,`Sedation`],[`Phenytoin`,`20 mg/kg IV over 20 min in saline`,`Second line`,`Arrhythmia; ECG monitoring`],[`Pyridoxine`,`100 mg IV once`,`15-30 mg/kg/day if responds`,`Apnoea after first dose`],[`Gentamicin + amoxicillin`,`5-7.5 mg/kg IM + 50 mg/kg twice daily`,`7 days (gentamicin 2 if not feasible)`,`Seizing baby is always referred`]]},{heading:`Neonatal seizure types`,columns:[`Type`,`Features`,`Setting and prognosis`],rows:[[`Subtle`,`Eye deviation, sucking, pedalling, apnoea`,`Commonest; variable`],[`Clonic`,`Rhythmic jerks of face or limb`,`Stroke, metabolic; better`],[`Tonic`,`Sustained posturing`,`Sick preterm with IVH; poor`],[`Myoclonic`,`Fast flexor jerks`,`Inborn errors; worst`],[`Electrographic only`,`No visible sign`,`After phenobarbitone, severe HIE`]]},{heading:`Hypothermia grades`,columns:[`Grade`,`Temperature`,`Action`],rows:[[`Normal`,`36.5-37.5 C`,`Continue warm chain`],[`Cold stress`,`36.0-36.4 C`,`Skin-to-skin, cover, feed, recheck 1 h`],[`Moderate`,`32.0-35.9 C`,`KMC or warmer, check glucose, look for sepsis`],[`Severe`,`Under 32 C`,`Warmer, IV dextrose, antibiotics, refer`]]}],remember:[`Subtle seizures are commonest; apnoea with tachycardia is a seizure.`,`Jitteriness stops when held; a seizure does not.`,`Glucose first, then calcium, then phenobarbitone 20 mg/kg.`,`No diazepam in neonates.`,`Calcium will not correct until magnesium is replaced.`,`GIR = % dextrose x mL/kg/day / 144.`,`Colic is a diagnosis of exclusion; look for the hair tourniquet.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`WHO PSBI 2024: when referral is refused, gentamicin IM once daily (5 mg/kg under 2 kg, 7.5 mg/kg at 2 kg or more) for 7 days, 2 days if not feasible, plus amoxicillin 7 days; a seizing baby is critical illness and always referred`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Neonatal seizures, hypoglycaemia, hypothermia and the crying baby`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Neonatal seizures in first 28 days, mostly **subtle**; hypoglycaemia - **operational threshold 45 mg/dL**; hypothermia under 36.5 C; colic (Rome IV) - recurrent crying under 5 months in a thriving infant.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Seizures (ILAE 2021): **electrographic-only vs electro-clinical**; clinical - **subtle (50%), clonic, tonic, myoclonic**; hypothermia **cold stress 36.0-36.4, moderate 32-35.9, severe under 32 C**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Seizures - **HIE (40-60%)**, hypoglycaemia, **hypocalcaemia** (early under 72 h; late 5-10 days with cow's milk), sepsis-meningitis, IVH, stroke, IEM, pyridoxine dependency, KCNQ2; hypoglycaemia - **IDM, SGA, preterm**, cold, sepsis.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Immature neurons **NKCC1 > KCC2 -> GABA excitatory** -> phenobarbitone often fails and electro-clinical uncoupling; IDM - fetal hyperinsulinism -> hypoglycaemia at 1-6 h; cold -> brown fat -> hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, acidosis; crying peak at 6 weeks = AHT peak.`],[`Key history`,`Eyewitness event (eye deviation, lip smacking, cycling, apnoea; **stops when held = jitteriness**); onset age in hours/days; perinatal events; feeding (cow's milk); consanguinity; maternal diabetes, beta-blockers; crying pattern and red flags; parental mood (EPDS); gripe water, opium.`],[`Key examination`,`Observe event (**apnoea with tachycardia = seizure**, bradycardia = apnoea of prematurity); vitals, glucose; **touch method** for temperature; neuro exam and **Sarnat** staging; cause clues; IDM signs; crying baby fully undressed - **hair tourniquet**, hernia, fracture, bruises.`],[`Investigations`,`**Bedside glucose first**; calcium (total under 7 mg/dL / ionised under 1 mmol/L), magnesium, sodium, gas, **blood culture and CSF**, cranial US, aEEG/EEG; glucose screening at **2, 6, 12, 24, 48, 72 h** before feeds; critical sample for persistent hypoglycaemia.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Seizure confirmed by EEG where possible; hypoglycaemia under 45 mg/dL (PES targets over 50 in 48 h, over 60 after); colic only in a thriving baby with normal examination.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Seizure: ABC, **10% dextrose 2 mL/kg** then GIR 6-8 mg/kg/min; **10% calcium gluconate 2 mL/kg over 10 min** with HR monitoring; **phenobarbitone 20 mg/kg IV, up to 40 mg/kg**, maintenance 3-5 mg/kg/day; hypothermia - skin-to-skin/KMC.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Levetiracetam 40-60 mg/kg IV**, phenytoin/fosphenytoin 20 mg/kg (KCNQ2), midazolam infusion, **pyridoxine 100 mg IV**; refractory hypoglycaemia - glucagon 20-30 mcg/kg, hydrocortisone, **diazoxide**; magnesium 0.2 mL/kg of 50% if calcium does not correct.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Glucometer, **40% dextrose gel 0.5 mL/kg buccal** for asymptomatic 20-45 mg/dL; **GIR = % dextrose x mL/kg/day / 144**; digital thermometer 3 min; radiant warmer; aEEG; KMC for transport.`],[`Special groups`,`IDM - glucose from 1-2 h, calcium at 24 h, Hct; **avoid diazepam** in neonates; HELIX - **no cooling outside tertiary NICU**; PSBI with refused referral - **gentamicin 7 days (2 if not feasible) + amoxicillin 7 days** (WHO 2024).`],[`Complications`,`Neurodevelopmental impairment, epilepsy, **cortical visual impairment after severe hypoglycaemia**, sclerema and DIC in severe cold, abusive head trauma from crying.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Every neonatal seizure referred**; glucose under 45 persisting after feed or GIR over 10; temp under 35.5 C or not rewarming; high-pitched cry with bulging fontanelle; bilious vomiting, red-currant stool, groin swelling; bruises or retinal haemorrhages.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Stop anti-seizure drugs before discharge in most provoked seizures; **DEIC neurodevelopmental follow-up** with vision and hearing; HBNC visits; colic review 1-2 weeks with weight and EPDS.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Warm chain (10 links), early breastfeeding 8-12/day, no cow's milk or prelacteals, antenatal glucose control, **HBNC/HBYC, SNCU, INAP**; teach parents never to shake a baby.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WHO PSBI 2024** simplified regimen (7-day gentamicin preferred); seizure treatment follows **ILAE 2023** (phenobarbitone first, levetiracetam second, stop before discharge).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Glucose first in every neonatal seizure; jitteriness stops when you hold the limb; calcium will not correct until magnesium is replaced; hypothermia, hypoglycaemia and sepsis travel together.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Seizing neonate - step by step`,steps:[{label:`ABC, warmth, bedside glucose`,detail:`Under 45 mg/dL - 10% dextrose 2 mL/kg then infusion GIR 6-8 mg/kg/min`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Calcium and magnesium`,detail:`Low or day 5-10 with cow's milk - 10% calcium gluconate 2 mL/kg over 10 min with HR monitoring; MgSO4 if refractory`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Phenobarbitone`,detail:`20 mg/kg IV over 15-20 min, further 10 mg/kg to 40 mg/kg; bag-mask ready`},{label:`Second line`,detail:`Levetiracetam 40-60 mg/kg, phenytoin 20 mg/kg (KCNQ2), midazolam infusion; then pyridoxine 100 mg IV`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Treat cause and refer`,detail:`Cultures, CSF, antibiotics if sepsis; acyclovir if HSV; SNCU/NICU in KMC position`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Seizure versus jitteriness versus apnoea of prematurity`,columns:[`Feature`,`Seizure`,`Jitteriness`,`Apnoea of prematurity`],rows:[[`Stimulus-sensitive`,`No`,`Yes`,`No`],[`Stops on restraint`,`No`,`Yes`,`Not applicable`],[`Eye deviation or autonomic change`,`Often`,`No`,`No`],[`Heart rate during event`,`Tachycardia or normal`,`Normal`,`Bradycardia`],[`Treatment`,`Glucose, calcium, phenobarbitone`,`Reassure; check glucose and calcium`,`Stimulation, caffeine citrate`]]}]},"pediatrics-genetic-disorders-haemoglobinopathies":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and classification`,points:[`**Haemoglobinopathies** are India's commonest preventable genetic disorders and are disabilities under the **RPwD Act 2016**.`,`Beta-thalassaemia carrier rate is about **3-4%**, with 10,000-15,000 new major births a year.`,`Sickle cell carrier rates reach **1-40% in tribal groups** of central India.`,`Genetic disorders: **chromosomal, autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, X-linked, multifactorial, mitochondrial**.`,`Thalassaemia: **trait**, **non-transfusion-dependent (NTDT)** and **transfusion-dependent (TDT)**.`,`SCD includes HbSS, HbS-beta thalassaemia, HbSC and HbSD; **sickle trait (HbAS)** is a carrier state.`,`**Down syndrome** is about 1 in 800: **95% free trisomy 21**, 3-4% translocation, 1-2% mosaic.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Thalassaemia: too little beta-globin leaves **unpaired alpha chains** that kill red cell precursors.`,`2. Symptoms start at **6-24 months**, when fetal HbF switches to adult HbA.`,`3. Marrow expands, causing **frontal bossing and chipmunk facies**; transfusion prevents it.`,`4. Each unit of blood adds **200-250 mg of iron**, damaging heart, liver and glands.`,`5. Sickle cell: **HbS polymerises** when deoxygenated, causing rigid sticky cells.`,`6. Vaso-occlusion causes pain crises, dactylitis, **acute chest syndrome** and stroke.`,`7. Splenic infarction causes **functional asplenia by 2-5 years**, so pneumococcal sepsis kills.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Pallor from 6-24 months with a big belly suggests **thalassaemia major**.`,`Ask community, native place and **consanguinity**; draw a three-generation pedigree.`,`Microcytosis that never responded to iron suggests a trait.`,`In transfused children ask the interval, pre-transfusion Hb and **chelator adherence**.`,`In SCD ask about pain crises, **dactylitis**, fevers and stroke symptoms.`,`In pregnancy, ask whether the **husband has been tested**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Pallor with icterus and a big spleen means haemolysis.`,`Plot growth and Tanner stage; delayed puberty signals **iron toxicity**.`,`Thalassaemic facies mean chronic **under-transfusion**.`,`A rapidly enlarging spleen in a young SCD child means **sequestration**.`,`Look for heart failure from iron and for leg ulcers and priapism in SCD.`,`Down features: upslanting eyes, flat face, **single palmar crease**, sandal gap, hypotonia.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC: low MCV with **high RBC count** and normal RDW suggests trait.`,`**HPLC**: HbA2 over 3.5% in beta-trait, HbF 90-98% in major, HbS in SCD.`,`**No HPLC within 3 months of transfusion**; test the parents instead.`,`Ferritin separates iron deficiency from trait; correct iron before borderline HbA2.`,`Before the first transfusion: extended phenotype, viral markers and HLA typing.`,`Down syndrome: **karyotype every child**, plus echo, TSH and CBC.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Transfuse when **Hb is under 7 g/dL** on two occasions 2 weeks apart.`,`Give matched packed cells **10-15 mL/kg** every 2-5 weeks.`,`Keep **pre-transfusion Hb 9.5-10.5 g/dL**.`,`Start chelation after **10-20 transfusions or ferritin over 1000**.`,`SCD: **hydroxyurea from 9 months** for every child with HbSS or HbS-beta0.`,`SCD: **penicillin V** from 2 months to 5 years, plus full vaccines.`,`**No iron tonics** in thalassaemia.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Chelators: **deferasirox** once daily, **deferiprone** three times daily, desferrioxamine SC at night.`,`Deferiprone needs **weekly ANC**; deferasirox needs monthly creatinine.`,`Cardiac iron needs intensified or combined chelation.`,`Hydroxyurea: start 10-15 mg/kg, increase by 5 mg/kg every 8 weeks, max 35.`,`Splenectomy after 5 years for hypersplenism, after vaccines.`,`**HLA-matched sibling transplant** cures over 90% of young low-risk children.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`SCD fever **38.5 C or more**: blood culture, then **ceftriaxone** within an hour.`,`Acute chest syndrome: oxygen, ceftriaxone plus azithromycin, transfusion.`,`Sequestration: small-volume transfusion of **5-10 mL/kg**.`,`SCD stroke: **exchange transfusion** to HbS under 30%.`,`Priapism over 2-4 hours is a urological emergency.`,`Fever on deferiprone: check ANC for **agranulocytosis**.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`Screen every pregnant woman; if she is a carrier, **test the husband at once**.`,`**CVS at 10-13 weeks** for prenatal diagnosis if both are carriers.`,`Recurrence: autosomal recessive **25% each pregnancy**; chance has no memory.`,`**PCPNDT Act** allows prenatal diagnosis for genetic disease but **bans sex determination**.`,`Genetic counselling is **non-directive** and confidential.`,`**NSCAEM (2023)**: screen 7 crore people aged 0-40 in 17 states; eliminate by 2047.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Thalassaemia: ferritin every 3 months; yearly endocrine, hearing and eye checks.`,`**Cardiac T2* MRI** yearly from 8-10 years; under 10 ms is high risk.`,`SCD: review every 3 months; yearly **TCD from 2 to 16 years**.`,`Down syndrome: TSH at birth, 6 and 12 months, then yearly; hearing and eye checks.`,`Ask about **atlanto-axial symptoms** in Down syndrome at every visit.`,`Refer carrier-carrier couples **before 10-12 weeks** of pregnancy.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Haemoglobinopathy drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Packed red cells`,`10-15 mL/kg over 3-4 h`,`Every 2-5 weeks`,`Keep pre-transfusion Hb 9.5-10.5`],[`Deferasirox`,`Dispersible 20-40 or film-coated 14-28 mg/kg once daily`,`Lifelong`,`Raised creatinine, transaminases`],[`Deferiprone`,`75-100 mg/kg/day in 3 doses`,`Lifelong`,`Agranulocytosis; weekly ANC`],[`Desferrioxamine`,`20-40 mg/kg/day SC over 8-12 h`,`5-7 nights a week`,`Hearing and eye toxicity, Yersinia`],[`Hydroxyurea`,`10-15 mg/kg once daily, max 35 mg/kg`,`Long term`,`Myelosuppression; teratogenic`],[`Penicillin V`,`125 mg BD (to 3 y); 250 mg BD (3-5 y)`,`2 months to 5 years`,`Rash`],[`Folic acid`,`1-5 mg once daily`,`Long term (SCD, NTDT)`,`Rare`],[`Morphine (pain crisis)`,`0.1 mg/kg IV or SC, max 10 mg`,`Every 2-4 h, titrated`,`Respiratory depression; no pethidine`],[`Ceftriaxone (SCD fever)`,`50-75 mg/kg IV, max 2 g`,`Within 1 hour of fever`,`After blood culture`]]},{heading:`Thalassaemia trait or iron deficiency?`,columns:[`Feature`,`Beta-thalassaemia trait`,`Iron deficiency`],rows:[[`RBC count`,`High (over 5 million)`,`Low`],[`RDW`,`Normal`,`High`],[`Mentzer index`,`Under 13`,`Over 13`],[`Ferritin`,`Normal`,`Low`],[`HbA2 on HPLC`,`Over 3.5%`,`Normal or low`],[`Response to iron`,`None`,`Hb up 1-2 g/dL in 3-4 weeks`]]},{heading:`Genetic disorders and recurrence risk`,columns:[`Type`,`Examples`,`Recurrence risk`],rows:[[`Chromosomal`,`Trisomy 21, Turner, 22q11`,`About 1% free trisomy; 10-15% if mother carries 14;21`],[`Autosomal dominant`,`Achondroplasia, NF1, Marfan`,`50% if a parent is affected`],[`Autosomal recessive`,`Thalassaemia, SCD, CF, SMA`,`25% each pregnancy`],[`X-linked recessive`,`Haemophilia, DMD, G6PD`,`50% of sons of carrier mother`],[`Multifactorial`,`NTD, cleft lip, CHD`,`2-5% after one child`],[`Mitochondrial`,`MELAS, LHON`,`Maternal transmission only`]]}],remember:[`HbA2 over 3.5% on HPLC means beta-thalassaemia trait.`,`Never long-term iron for microcytosis without a ferritin.`,`No HPLC within 3 months of transfusion.`,`Pre-transfusion Hb 9.5-10.5; chelate after 10-20 units or ferritin over 1000.`,`Deferiprone weekly ANC; deferasirox monthly creatinine; desferrioxamine hearing and eyes.`,`Hydroxyurea for every HbSS child from 9 months.`,`Karyotype every Down syndrome child; NIPT is screening, not diagnosis.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - Thalassaemia, sickle cell disease, Down syndrome and genetic counselling`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Genetic disorders - chromosomal, single-gene (AD, AR, X-linked), multifactorial, mitochondrial; India's commonest preventable ones are **beta-thalassaemia, sickle cell disease** and **Down syndrome**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Thalassaemia: **trait, NTDT, TDT**; SCD: **HbSS, HbS-beta0/beta+, HbSC/SD**; trait HbAS is a carrier state; Down: **95% free trisomy 21, 3-4% Robertsonian translocation, 1-2% mosaic**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Beta-thal carrier **3-4%** (higher in Sindhi, Punjabi, Lohana, Kutchi, Bengali, Parsi), **IVS1-5** commonest mutation; HbS **1-40% in central Indian tribal groups**; consanguinity; Down risk rises with maternal age but most born to younger mothers.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Thal - **unpaired alpha chains -> ineffective erythropoiesis** -> marrow expansion, **low hepcidin**, iron overload (each unit 200-250 mg) -> heart, liver, endocrine damage; SCD - **HbS polymerises when deoxygenated** -> vaso-occlusion, haemolysis, NO scavenging, functional asplenia by 2-5 years.`],[`Key history`,`Age at pallor (6-24 months), community, **three-generation pedigree**; iron tonics without response; transfusion interval, chelator and real adherence; complications; SCD pain, dactylitis, fevers, stroke symptoms, genetic status card; pregnancy and husband's test; Down feeding, snoring, atlanto-axial symptoms.`],[`Key examination`,`Pallor, icterus, **spleen and liver in cm**, thalassaemic facies (under-transfusion), growth and **Tanner stage**, cardiac signs, pigmentation, hypoparathyroid signs; SCD - dactylitis, leg ulcers, hip, SpO2; Down features and murmur, cataract.`],[`Investigations`,`CBC (**high RBC, low MCV, normal RDW** in trait), Mentzer under 13; ferritin; **HPLC - HbA2 over 3.5% (trait), HbF 90-98% (major), HbS%**; no HPLC within 3 months of transfusion; NESTROFT and solubility test for field; extended red cell phenotype before first transfusion; **karyotype in every Down syndrome**.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Beta-thal trait - **HbA2 over 3.5%** after iron correction; TDT - transfusion needed (Hb under 7 on two occasions); SCD - HbS on HPLC without HbA (HbSS).`],[`Treatment - first line`,`TDT: **leucoreduced matched packed cells 10-15 mL/kg every 2-5 weeks, pre-transfusion Hb 9.5-10.5 g/dL**; chelation after **10-20 transfusions or ferritin over 1000** - deferasirox 20-40 mg/kg (dispersible), deferiprone 75-100 mg/kg/day, desferrioxamine 20-40 mg/kg SC; SCD - **hydroxyurea from 9 months (10-15 mg/kg, NSCAEM 10 mg/kg), penicillin V to 5 years, folic acid**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Cardiac siderosis - **continuous IV desferrioxamine or DFO + deferiprone**; luspatercept in adult TDT; splenectomy after 5 years if needs over 200-250 mL/kg/year; **HLA-matched sibling HSCT** (over 90% cure); SCD hydroxyurea titrated to max 35 mg/kg/day.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Transfusion card, SC desferrioxamine pump, **cardiac T2* MRI from 8-10 years** (under 10 ms high risk), TCD 2-16 years in HbSS, CVS at 10-13 weeks for prenatal diagnosis.`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy - stop oral chelators, test husband immediately, CVS before 12 weeks; Down syndrome - **TSH at birth, 6, 12 months then yearly**, echo all newborns, hearing and eye checks, polysomnography by 4 years; disability under **RPwD Act 2016**.`],[`Complications`,`Thal - iron cardiomyopathy (main killer), hypogonadism, diabetes, hypothyroidism, infections; SCD - **acute chest syndrome, stroke, splenic sequestration, aplastic crisis, priapism**, AVN, CKD; deferiprone agranulocytosis.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`SCD fever **38.5 C or more (ceftriaxone within 1 h)**, chest pain/hypoxia, sudden pallor with big spleen, neuro signs, priapism over 2-4 h; thal breathlessness or palpitations; fever on deferiprone; bilious vomiting or neck symptoms in Down syndrome.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Thal - ferritin 3-monthly, yearly endocrine panel, T2* MRI; deferiprone weekly ANC, deferasirox monthly creatinine; SCD 3-monthly CBC on hydroxyurea (withhold if ANC under 1000, platelets under 80,000); refer carrier couples before 10-12 weeks.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**NSCAEM (1 July 2023, Shahdol)** - screen 7 crore aged 0-40 in 17 states, genetic status cards, eliminate SCD by 2047; antenatal haemoglobinopathy screening; cascade and premarital screening; **PCPNDT Act** (Form F, Form G, no sex determination); MTP Amendment 2021.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - TIF 2021, NHLBI 2014/ASH 2020 and NSCAEM 2023 remain the reference; voxelotor withdrawn (2024), hydroxyurea remains drug of choice.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`AR recurrence 25% each pregnancy - chance has no memory; never iron to microcytic child without ferritin; 21;21 translocation carrier - 100% recurrence; NIPT is screening, not diagnosis; counselling is non-directive.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Microcytic child - trait, iron deficiency or major`,steps:[{label:`CBC and indices`,detail:`MCV under 80, MCH under 27; RBC count and RDW; Mentzer index`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Ferritin`,detail:`Low - treat iron 3 months then retest; normal - go to HPLC`,tone:`decision`},{label:`HPLC (not within 3 months of transfusion)`,detail:`HbA2 over 3.5% trait; HbF 90-98% major; HbS% for sickle`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Trait`,detail:`No iron without deficiency; counsel; test partner and relatives (cascade)`,tone:`good`},{label:`Major or SCD`,detail:`Transfusion-chelation programme or hydroxyurea + penicillin; refer for HSCT assessment`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Thalassaemia major - transfusion and chelation targets`,steps:[{label:`Start transfusion`,detail:`Hb under 7 g/dL on two occasions over 2 weeks apart, or facial change, poor growth, fractures`},{label:`Maintain`,detail:`10-15 mL/kg leucoreduced matched cells every 2-5 weeks; pre-transfusion Hb 9.5-10.5 g/dL`},{label:`Start chelation`,detail:`After 10-20 transfusions or ferritin over 1000 ng/mL; deferasirox 20-40 mg/kg/day`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Monitor iron`,detail:`Ferritin 3-monthly (target 500-1000); cardiac T2* over 20 ms; liver iron under 7 mg/g`},{label:`Cure`,detail:`HLA-matched sibling HSCT early (Pesaro I-II), over 90% thalassaemia-free survival`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Iron chelators compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Deferasirox`,`Deferiprone`,`Desferrioxamine`],rows:[[`Route and dose`,`Oral 20-40 mg/kg once daily (dispersible)`,`Oral 75-100 mg/kg/day in 3 doses`,`SC 20-40 mg/kg over 8-12 h, 5-7 nights`],[`Strength`,`Once daily convenience`,`Best cardiac chelator`,`Long experience; IV for cardiac crisis`],[`Key toxicity`,`Creatinine rise, hepatitis, proteinuria`,`Agranulocytosis, arthropathy`,`Ototoxicity, retinopathy, growth failure, Yersinia`],[`Monitoring`,`Monthly creatinine and urine protein`,`Weekly ANC`,`Yearly audiometry and eye exam`]]}]}};export{e as default};